/* Countdown quiz deck: bio. Generated from the revision notes — do not edit by hand. */
QZ.load("bio", {"chapters":{"1":["1","Characteristics and classification of living organisms"],"2":["2","Cells"],"3":["3","Movement into and out of cells"],"4":["4","Biological molecules"],"5":["5","Enzymes"],"6":["6","Plant nutrition"],"7":["7","Human nutrition"],"8":["8","Transport in plants"],"9":["9","Transport in animals"],"10":["10","Diseases and immunity"],"11":["11","Respiration and gas exchange"],"12":["12","Coordination and response"],"13":["13","Excretion and homeostasis"],"14":["14","Reproduction in plants"],"15":["15","Reproduction in humans"],"16":["16","Chromosomes, genes and proteins"],"17":["17","Variation and selection (and drugs)"],"18":["18","Organisms and their environment"],"19":["19","Human influences on ecosystems"],"20":["20","Biotechnology and genetic modification"]},"topics":{"bio-form":["Formulas","Write the formula"],"bio-expl":["Describe & explain","Describe or explain"],"bio-diff":["Differences","State the differences"],"bio-facts":["Facts","Answer"]},"cards":[["n-b1-exp-classification-reflects-ev","bio-expl",1,"Classification reflects evolution","<p><strong>Classification systems aim to reflect evolutionary relationships.</strong> Organisms placed in the same group are thought to share a common ancestor. Traditionally based on <strong>morphology</strong> (external form) and <strong>anatomy</strong> (internal structure).</p>",1,""],["n-b1-exp-dna-base-sequences-in-clas","bio-expl",1,"DNA base sequences in classification","<p><strong>The sequences of bases in DNA are used as a means of classification. Groups of organisms which share a more recent ancestor (are more closely related) have base sequences in DNA that are more similar than those that share only a distant ancestor.</strong> DNA is more reliable than appearance (two unrelated organisms can look alike). Amino-acid sequences of proteins can be compared in the same way.</p>",1,""],["n-b1-exp-virus","bio-expl",1,"Virus","<p>Not a cell and not placed in any kingdom. Features you must state: <strong>a protein coat and genetic material</strong> (DNA or RNA). Viruses can only reproduce <strong>inside a living host cell</strong>, using the cell's machinery; on their own they show none of the seven characteristics, so they are not normally considered living. Much smaller than bacteria (measured in nanometres). Cause colds, flu, measles, COVID-19, HIV/AIDS.</p>",1,""],["n-b1-dif-fertile-vs-infertile","bio-diff",1,"Fertile vs infertile","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Fertile</th><th>Infertile</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Able to reproduce</td><td>Not able to reproduce</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>Horse × horse → horse (fertile foal)</td><td>Horse × donkey → mule (cannot reproduce)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Link to 'species'</td><td>Parents are the same species</td><td>Parents are different species</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b1-dif-plant-kingdom-vs-animal-ki","bio-diff",1,"Plant kingdom vs animal kingdom","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Plant kingdom</th><th>Animal kingdom</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cells</td><td>Multicellular; nucleus present</td><td>Multicellular; nucleus present</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cell wall</td><td>Present — made of <strong>cellulose</strong></td><td><strong>Absent</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chloroplasts / chlorophyll</td><td>Present in many cells</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nutrition</td><td><strong>Photosynthesis</strong> — make their own food</td><td>Feed on organic substances made by other organisms; most ingest and digest food internally</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vacuole</td><td>Large permanent sap vacuole</td><td>Small temporary vacuoles only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Storage carbohydrate</td><td>Starch</td><td>Glycogen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Movement</td><td>Do not move from place to place; spreading shape to catch light</td><td>Most move actively to find food</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Moss, fern, mango tree</td><td>Jellyfish, butterfly, human</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b1-dif-fungus-vs-prokaryote-vs-pr","bio-diff",1,"Fungus vs prokaryote vs protoctist kingdoms","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Fungus</th><th>Prokaryote</th><th>Protoctist</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Number of cells</td><td>Usually multicellular (made of <strong>hyphae</strong>); some unicellular, e.g. <strong>yeast</strong></td><td>Usually unicellular</td><td>Mostly unicellular; some multicellular (e.g. <strong>seaweeds</strong>)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nucleus</td><td>Present</td><td><strong>Absent</strong> — circular DNA free in cytoplasm (+ plasmids)</td><td>Present</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cell wall</td><td>Present, <strong>not cellulose</strong> (chitin)</td><td>Present, <strong>not cellulose</strong></td><td>Some (cellulose) / some none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chloroplasts</td><td><strong>None</strong> — no chlorophyll</td><td>None (some bacteria photosynthesise)</td><td>Some have them (plant-like), some don't (animal-like)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mitochondria</td><td>Present</td><td><strong>Absent</strong></td><td>Present</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Feed on dead/waste organic matter (saprotrophic) or parasitic; digest food outside, absorb products</td><td>Varied — decomposers, parasites, some photosynthesise</td><td>Some photosynthesise, others feed on organic substances</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reproduction</td><td>Spores</td><td>Binary fission (asexual)</td><td>Varied</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Mushroom, toadstool, mould (<em>Mucor</em>), <em>Penicillium</em>, yeast</td><td>Bacteria, e.g. <em>Vibrio cholerae</em>, <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></td><td><em>Amoeba</em>, <em>Paramecium</em> (animal-like), <em>Chlamydomonas</em> (plant-like), seaweed</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b1-dif-vertebrates-vs-invertebrat","bio-diff",1,"Vertebrates vs invertebrates","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Vertebrates</th><th>Invertebrates</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Backbone</td><td>Present (vertebral column)</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Skeleton</td><td>Internal (endoskeleton) of bone or cartilage</td><td>No internal skeleton; may have exoskeleton (arthropods), shell (snails) or none (worms)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals</td><td>Arthropods (insects, crustaceans, arachnids, myriapods), earthworms, snails, jellyfish</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b1-dif-the-five-vertebrate-classe","bio-diff",1,"The five vertebrate classes","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Fish</th><th>Amphibians</th><th>Reptiles</th><th>Birds</th><th>Mammals</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Skin covering</td><td><strong>Scales</strong> (wet)</td><td><strong>Moist skin, no scales</strong></td><td><strong>Dry scaly skin</strong></td><td><strong>Feathers</strong> (scales on legs)</td><td><strong>Hair / fur</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gas exchange</td><td><strong>Gills</strong> all life</td><td>Larva (tadpole): gills; adult: lungs + skin</td><td>Lungs</td><td>Lungs</td><td>Lungs (+ diaphragm)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Limbs</td><td><strong>Fins</strong></td><td>4 limbs (webbed back feet)</td><td>4 legs (none in snakes)</td><td>2 legs + <strong>2 wings</strong>; <strong>beak</strong></td><td>4 limbs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Eggs / young</td><td>Eggs with no shell, laid in water</td><td>Eggs with no shell (jelly), laid <strong>in water</strong>; metamorphosis</td><td>Eggs with <strong>soft, rubbery, waterproof shell</strong>, laid on land</td><td>Eggs with <strong>hard shell</strong></td><td><strong>Live young</strong>, develop in uterus with placenta; fed milk from <strong>mammary glands</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Other</td><td>Lateral line; cold-blooded</td><td>Cold-blooded</td><td>Cold-blooded</td><td>Warm-blooded</td><td>Warm-blooded; <strong>pinna</strong> (external ear flap); <strong>4 types of teeth</strong>; sweat glands</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Shark, herring, rohu</td><td>Frog, toad, salamander</td><td>Crocodile, snake, lizard, turtle</td><td>Pigeon, ostrich, kingfisher</td><td>Human, whale, pangolin</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b1-dif-insects-vs-crustaceans-vs","bio-diff",1,"Insects vs crustaceans vs arachnids vs myriapods","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Insects</th><th>Crustaceans</th><th>Arachnids</th><th>Myriapods</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Legs</td><td><strong>3 pairs</strong> (6 legs)</td><td><strong>More than 4 pairs</strong> (5+ pairs)</td><td><strong>4 pairs</strong> (8 legs)</td><td><strong>Many</strong> — jointed legs on <strong>every segment</strong> (centipede 1 pair, millipede 2 pairs per segment)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Antennae</td><td><strong>1 pair</strong></td><td><strong>2 pairs</strong></td><td><strong>None</strong></td><td><strong>1 pair</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Body parts</td><td>3: <strong>head, thorax, abdomen</strong></td><td>2: cephalothorax + abdomen</td><td>2: <strong>cephalothorax + abdomen</strong></td><td>Many similar segments (head + body)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wings</td><td>Usually <strong>2 pairs</strong> (one may be vestigial)</td><td>None</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Eyes</td><td>1 pair compound eyes</td><td>Compound eyes (often on stalks)</td><td>Several pairs of simple eyes</td><td>Simple eyes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gas exchange</td><td>Tracheae (tubes)</td><td>Gills (so live in water/damp places)</td><td>Book lungs/tracheae</td><td>Tracheae</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Other</td><td>Mainly terrestrial</td><td>Exoskeleton often hardened with calcium (carapace)</td><td>Chelicerae (fangs) for biting/poisoning prey</td><td>Long, thin body</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Butterfly, locust, dragonfly, ant</td><td>Crab, lobster, woodlouse</td><td>Spider, scorpion, tick</td><td>Centipede, millipede</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b1-dif-ferns-vs-flowering-plants","bio-diff",1,"Ferns vs flowering plants","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Ferns</th><th>Flowering plants</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Roots, stems, leaves</td><td>Yes — leaves are <strong>fronds</strong>; stem usually underground (<strong>rhizome</strong>)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flowers</td><td><strong>No flowers</strong></td><td><strong>Flowers</strong> present</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reproduction</td><td><strong>Spores</strong> made in sporangia on <strong>underside of fronds</strong></td><td><strong>Seeds</strong> made inside an <strong>ovary</strong> in the flower</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Young leaves</td><td>Coiled, unroll as they grow</td><td>Grow from buds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vascular tissue</td><td>Xylem and phloem present</td><td>Xylem and phloem present</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Bracken, tree fern</td><td>Mango, grass, hibiscus</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b1-dif-monocotyledons-vs-dicotyle","bio-diff",1,"Monocotyledons vs dicotyledons","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Monocotyledons (monocots)</th><th>Dicotyledons (dicots)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cotyledons (seed leaves) in seed</td><td><strong>One</strong></td><td><strong>Two</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Leaf shape</td><td>Usually long and narrow</td><td>Usually broad</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Leaf veins</td><td><strong>Parallel</strong></td><td><strong>Branching network</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flower parts (petals, sepals, carpels)</td><td>In <strong>threes</strong> (multiples of 3)</td><td>In <strong>fours or fives</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Roots</td><td>Fibrous — many roots grow directly from stem</td><td>Main (tap) root with side roots branching from it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vascular bundles in stem</td><td>Scattered randomly</td><td>Arranged in a <strong>ring</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Grass, maize, rice, wheat, lily, iris, palm</td><td>Bean, pea, hibiscus, sunflower, oak, mango</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b1-fac-arthropods-exoskeleton","bio-facts",1,"Arthropods – key feature?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Arthropods – exoskeleton</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The exoskeleton is waterproof and supports the body; it lets arthropods live in very dry places without drying out. Because it is rigid, the legs must be <strong>jointed</strong> to move, and the animal must <strong>moult</strong> to grow.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-chlamydomonas-plant-like-c","bio-facts",1,"Chlamydomonas – what kind of cell?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Chlamydomonas – plant-like cell</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Has cellulose cell wall + chloroplast; photosynthesises. Belongs to the <strong>protoctist</strong> kingdom (not plant kingdom) because it is a single cell.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-paramecium-animal-like-cel","bio-facts",1,"Paramecium – what kind of cell?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Paramecium – animal-like cell</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>No cell wall or chloroplast; feeds on other organisms. Also a <strong>protoctist</strong>.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-seaweed-multicellular-prot","bio-facts",1,"Seaweed – what kind of protoctist?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Seaweed – multicellular protoctist</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Most protoctists are unicellular, but seaweeds are multicellular protoctists (they have chloroplasts but are not true plants).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-species-is-the-smallest-gr","bio-facts",1,"Smallest and largest groups used in classification?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Species is the smallest group used in classification; kingdom is the largest.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The singular and plural of 'species' are the same word.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-viruses-do-not-belong-to-a","bio-facts",1,"Which kingdom do viruses belong to?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Viruses do not belong to any of the five kingdoms.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They are not made of cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-bacteria-are-the-oldest-gr","bio-facts",1,"Bacteria: how old is the group, and where do some live?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bacteria are the oldest group of organisms (oldest fossils) and some live &gt;3 km underground.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Some bacteria photosynthesise.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-yeast-is-a-unicellular-fun","bio-facts",1,"Yeast and Penicillium: what are they?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Yeast is a <strong>unicellular fungus</strong>; <em>Penicillium</em> (a fungus) makes the antibiotic penicillin.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Some fungi cause disease, e.g. athlete's foot, ringworm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-only-mammals-have-hair-mam","bio-facts",1,"Features found only in mammals?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Only mammals have hair, mammary glands, a pinna, a diaphragm and a placenta.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Birds and mammals are the only warm-blooded (homeothermic) vertebrates and both have a 4-chambered heart.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-birds-are-the-only-animals","bio-facts",1,"The only animals with feathers?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Birds are the only animals with feathers.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Reptile eggs have soft leathery shells; bird eggs have hard shells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-amphibians-must-return-to","bio-facts",1,"What must amphibians do to breed?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Amphibians must return to water to breed.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Their eggs have no shell and would dry out on land.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-insects-are-the-most-numer","bio-facts",1,"Most numerous group of animals on Earth?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Insects are the most numerous group of animals on Earth.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Success due to exoskeleton + tracheae (reduce water loss) and wings.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b1-fac-when-writing-a-binomial-by","bio-facts",1,"Writing a binomial name by hand?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">When writing a binomial by hand, underline both words.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>e.g. Homo sapiens, written with Homo capitalised.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-for-magnification","bio-form",2,"Magnification","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">magnification = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">image size</span><span class=\"de\">actual size</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>actual size = image size ÷ magnification</b> · <b>image size = actual size × magnification</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">Both sizes in the same units. Magnification has no units: write × before it (e.g. ×1200). Measure the image with a ruler in mm.</p>",0,""],["n-b2-for-converting-units","bio-form",2,"Converting units","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">1 mm = 1000 µm</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">mm → µm: × 1000. µm → mm: ÷ 1000. Also 1 m = 1000 mm; 1 µm = 10⁻⁶ m. Give cell sizes in µm.</p>",1,""],["n-b2-dif-light-microscope-vs-electr","bio-diff",2,"Light microscope vs electron microscope","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Light microscope</th><th>Electron microscope</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What passes through specimen</td><td>Light</td><td>Beam of electrons</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lenses</td><td>Glass</td><td>Electromagnets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Maximum magnification</td><td>About ×1500</td><td>Up to about ×500 000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Detail (resolution)</td><td>Lower — can see nucleus, cytoplasm, membrane, cell wall, chloroplasts, vacuole</td><td>Much higher — also shows ribosomes, mitochondria detail, membranes inside organelles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Specimens</td><td>Can be living</td><td>Must be dead (in a vacuum)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Image</td><td>Colour; called a <strong>photomicrograph</strong></td><td>Black and white (colour added later); called an <strong>electron micrograph</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost / size</td><td>Cheap, portable</td><td>Very expensive, large</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b2-dif-partially-permeable-vs-ful","bio-diff",2,"Partially permeable vs fully permeable","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Partially permeable</th><th>Fully permeable</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Lets <strong>some</strong> molecules and ions through but <strong>not others</strong></td><td>Lets <strong>all</strong> molecules and ions through</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td><strong>Cell membrane</strong>, dialysis (Visking) tubing</td><td><strong>Cellulose cell wall</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Role</td><td>Controls what enters/leaves the cell; needed for osmosis</td><td>Gives strength; does not control entry</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b2-dif-plant-cell-vs-animal-cell","bio-diff",2,"Plant cell vs animal cell vs bacterial cell","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Structure</th><th>Plant cell</th><th>Animal cell</th><th>Bacterial cell</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cell wall</td><td>✔ cellulose</td><td>✘</td><td>✔ (not cellulose)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cell membrane</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cytoplasm</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nucleus</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✘ — circular DNA instead</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chloroplasts</td><td>✔ (in some cells)</td><td>✘</td><td>✘</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ribosomes</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mitochondria</td><td>✔</td><td>✔</td><td>✘</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vacuole</td><td>Large, permanent, cell sap</td><td>Small, temporary</td><td>✘</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plasmids</td><td>✘</td><td>✘</td><td>✔</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shape</td><td>Regular (box-shaped)</td><td>Irregular</td><td>Rod, sphere, spiral</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Carbohydrate store</td><td>Starch grains</td><td>Glycogen granules</td><td>Glycogen granules</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-1-m-10-m-1-m-10-m","bio-facts",2,"1 µm in metres, and 1 m in µm?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">1 µm = 10⁻⁶ m; 1 m = 10⁶ µm</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Also <strong>1 mm = 1000 µm</strong> — this is the conversion used in the exam. A typical animal cell is 10–30 µm; a plant cell 40–100 µm; a bacterium 1–5 µm; a red blood cell ≈ 7 µm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-palisade-mesophyll-cell-le","bio-facts",2,"Palisade mesophyll cell – where is it found?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Palisade mesophyll cell – leaves of flowering plants</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Found just below the upper epidermis; tall cells packed with chloroplasts → main site of photosynthesis.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-bacteria-prokaryotic-cell","bio-facts",2,"Bacteria – what type of cell?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bacteria – prokaryotic cell</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>No nucleus (circular DNA), no mitochondria, no chloroplasts; has plasmids, ribosomes, cell wall.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-chloroplast-starch-grain","bio-facts",2,"Chloroplast – what storage product?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Chloroplast – starch grain</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Glucose made in photosynthesis is converted to <strong>starch</strong> and stored as starch grains inside chloroplasts. Animal cells never contain starch.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-mitochondrion-aerobic-resp","bio-facts",2,"Mitochondrion – which process?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Mitochondrion – aerobic respiration</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Number of mitochondria reflects energy demand (many in muscle, liver, sperm mid-piece, root hair cells).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-not-all-plant-cells-have-c","bio-facts",2,"Do all plant cells have chloroplasts?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Not all plant cells have chloroplasts.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Root cells and most epidermis cells have none; <strong>guard cells are the only epidermis cells with chloroplasts</strong>.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-not-all-cells-keep-all-the","bio-facts",2,"Do all cells keep all their parts?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Not all cells keep all their parts.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Red blood cells have no nucleus</strong> (more room for haemoglobin); <strong>xylem vessels have no cytoplasm or nucleus</strong> (dead, empty tubes); phloem sieve tubes have no nucleus.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-chloroplast-chlorophyll","bio-facts",2,"Chloroplast compared with chlorophyll?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Chloroplast ≠ chlorophyll.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Chloroplast = the organelle; chlorophyll = the green pigment inside it.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-cell-walls-are-fully-perme","bio-facts",2,"Permeability of cell walls and cell membranes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cell walls are fully permeable; cell membranes are partially permeable.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So it is the <strong>membrane</strong> that controls entry, not the wall.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-bacteria-do-not-divide-by","bio-facts",2,"Do bacteria divide by mitosis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bacteria do not divide by mitosis — they have no nucleus.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They reproduce by binary fission.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b2-fac-cytoplasm-is-70-water","bio-facts",2,"Percentage of water in cytoplasm?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cytoplasm is ≈ 70% water.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Water is the solvent in which metabolic reactions take place.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-exp-importance-of-diffusion","bio-expl",3,"Importance of diffusion","<p>Gases and solutes enter/leave organisms by diffusion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>O₂</strong> from alveoli → blood; <strong>CO₂</strong> blood → alveoli.</li>\n<li><strong>CO₂</strong> into leaves through stomata (photosynthesis); <strong>O₂</strong> out; <strong>water vapour</strong> out (transpiration).</li>\n<li><strong>Digested food</strong> (glucose, amino acids) from small intestine → blood (partly).</li>\n<li>Urea from cells → blood; O₂ and glucose from capillaries → tissues; across the placenta; neurotransmitter across a synapse.</li>\n</ul>",0,""],["n-b3-exp-factors-affecting-rate-of","bio-expl",3,"Factors affecting rate of diffusion","<ul>\n<li><strong>Surface area</strong> — larger area → faster (alveoli, villi, root hairs).</li>\n<li><strong>Temperature</strong> — higher → particles have more kinetic energy → faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Concentration gradient</strong> — steeper → faster (maintained by blood flow / ventilation).</li>\n<li><strong>Distance</strong> — shorter (thinner surface) → faster (alveolus wall one cell thick).</li>\n</ul>",0,""],["n-b3-exp-water-as-a-solvent","bio-expl",3,"Water as a solvent","<p>Water dissolves many substances, so:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Digestion</strong> — enzymes act on food in solution; products dissolve so they can be absorbed.</li>\n<li><strong>Excretion</strong> — urea and excess ions are removed dissolved in water (urine); water dilutes toxic urea.</li>\n<li><strong>Transport</strong> — blood plasma carries dissolved glucose, amino acids, urea, ions, hormones, CO₂; xylem carries dissolved mineral ions; phloem carries dissolved sucrose and amino acids.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Also: chemical reactions in cytoplasm happen in solution.</p>",0,""],["n-b3-exp-importance-of-water-potent","bio-expl",3,"Importance of water potential and osmosis to organisms","<ul>\n<li><strong>Root hair cells</strong> take in water by osmosis because the soil water has a <strong>higher water potential</strong> than the cell sap.</li>\n<li>Water moves cell-to-cell across the root cortex and from xylem into leaf mesophyll cells by osmosis.</li>\n<li>If the soil solution becomes more concentrated (e.g. too much fertiliser, salty soil), water <strong>leaves</strong> the roots → plant wilts and may die.</li>\n<li>Animal cells must be bathed in fluid (tissue fluid, plasma) of the <strong>same water potential</strong> as their cytoplasm; if the fluid is more dilute, cells swell and <strong>burst</strong>; if more concentrated, cells <strong>shrink</strong> — so blood water potential is controlled by the kidneys (homeostasis).</li>\n</ul>",1,""],["n-b3-for-percentage-change-in-mass","bio-form",3,"Percentage change in mass (osmosis practical)","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% change = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">final mass − initial mass</span><span class=\"de\">initial mass</span></span> × 100</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">A negative answer means the tissue lost water by osmosis. The same formula works for length.</p>",0,""],["n-b3-for-surface-area-to-volume-rat","bio-form",3,"Surface area to volume ratio","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">SA : V = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">surface area</span><span class=\"de\">volume</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">A smaller cube has a larger ratio, so substances diffuse into all of it faster.</p>",0,""],["n-b3-for-rate-of-diffusion","bio-form",3,"Rate of diffusion","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">rate = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">distance travelled</span><span class=\"de\">time</span></span></div>",0,""],["n-b3-dif-osmosis-in-animal-cells-vs","bio-diff",3,"Osmosis in animal cells vs plant cells","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Solution placed in</th><th>Animal cell (e.g. red blood cell)</th><th>Plant cell</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Pure water / dilute solution</strong> (higher water potential than cell)</td><td>Water enters by osmosis → cell <strong>swells and bursts</strong> (no cell wall)</td><td>Water enters by osmosis → vacuole swells → cell becomes <strong>turgid</strong>; the strong <strong>cell wall stops it bursting</strong> (turgor pressure)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Same water potential</strong></td><td>No net movement; no change</td><td>No net movement; no change</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Concentrated solution</strong> (lower water potential than cell)</td><td>Water leaves by osmosis → cell <strong>shrinks / shrivels (crenates)</strong></td><td>Water leaves → vacuole shrinks → cell becomes <strong>flaccid</strong>, then <strong>plasmolysed</strong> (membrane and cytoplasm pull away from wall)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b3-dif-turgid-vs-flaccid","bio-diff",3,"Turgid vs flaccid","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Turgid</th><th>Flaccid</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Water content</td><td>Full of water</td><td>Has lost water</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vacuole</td><td>Large, swollen</td><td>Smaller</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cytoplasm/membrane</td><td>Pressed firmly against cell wall</td><td>Not pressing on wall</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Turgor pressure</td><td>High</td><td>Low / none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cell feel</td><td>Firm, rigid</td><td>Soft, limp</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect on plant</td><td>Stems upright, leaves held out flat to catch light</td><td>Plant <strong>wilts</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Caused by</td><td>Solution of higher water potential outside</td><td>Solution of lower water potential outside</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b3-dif-diffusion-vs-osmosis-vs-ac","bio-diff",3,"Diffusion vs osmosis vs active transport","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Diffusion</th><th>Osmosis</th><th>Active transport</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What moves</td><td>Any particles (gases, solutes)</td><td><strong>Water only</strong></td><td>Molecules or ions (e.g. nitrate, glucose)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Direction</td><td>High → low concentration (<strong>down</strong> gradient)</td><td>High → low <strong>water potential</strong></td><td>Low → high concentration (<strong>against</strong> gradient)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Membrane needed?</td><td>No (but can cross one)</td><td><strong>Yes — partially permeable</strong></td><td>Yes — cell membrane with <strong>protein carriers</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy from respiration?</td><td><strong>No</strong> (kinetic energy of particles)</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>O₂ into blood at alveoli</td><td>Water into root hair cells</td><td>Mineral ions into root hairs</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-carrier-proteins-root-hair","bio-facts",3,"Carrier proteins – where, for example?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Carrier proteins – root hair cells, in cell membrane</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Protein carriers in the membrane of root hair cells pick up specific ions (e.g. nitrate, magnesium) and use energy to move them in against the gradient.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-concentrated-solution-wate","bio-facts",3,"Cell in a concentrated solution: what happens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Concentrated solution → water leaves → cell shrinks</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Because the solution has a lower water potential than the cell.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-pure-water-animal-cell-swe","bio-facts",3,"Animal cell in pure water: what happens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pure water → animal cell swells and bursts</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>No cell wall to resist the pressure. Plant cells become turgid instead.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-low-water-potential-concen","bio-facts",3,"Low water potential = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Low water potential = concentrated solution</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Few 'free' water molecules.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-high-water-potential-dilut","bio-facts",3,"High water potential = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">High water potential = dilute solution</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Pure water has the highest water potential.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-dialysis-tubing-partially","bio-facts",3,"Dialysis tubing: what does it let through?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Dialysis tubing – partially permeable membrane: lets water through, not sugar</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Visking tubing is used to model the cell membrane / the gut wall.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-sugar-molecules-are-larger","bio-facts",3,"Sugar molecules compared with water molecules: size?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sugar molecules are larger than water molecules</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>That is why they cannot pass through the pores of a partially permeable membrane while water can.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-solutes-in-the-cytoplasm-d","bio-facts",3,"How do solutes move within the cytoplasm?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Solutes in the cytoplasm diffuse through the cytoplasm</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Diffusion also moves substances <em>within</em> a cell, e.g. O₂ to mitochondria.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-higher-temperature-more-ki","bio-facts",3,"Effect of a higher temperature on particle movement?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Higher temperature → more kinetic energy → faster movement</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So diffusion and osmosis are faster when warm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-diffusion-and-osmosis-are","bio-facts",3,"Which transport processes are passive, and which needs energy?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Diffusion and osmosis are passive; active transport needs energy from respiration.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Cells doing a lot of active transport (root hairs, villi epithelium, kidney tubule cells) have many mitochondria.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-sugars-and-mineral-ions-do","bio-facts",3,"Do sugars and mineral ions move by osmosis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sugars and mineral ions do <strong>not</strong> move by osmosis.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Osmosis refers only to water.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b3-fac-surface-area-volume-ratio","bio-facts",3,"Surface area : volume ratio – small objects compared with large?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Surface area : volume ratio</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Small objects have a larger SA:V ratio, so diffusion can supply all of the inside quickly. Large organisms need special exchange surfaces (lungs, gills, villi, leaves) and transport systems.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-exp-ethanol-emulsion-test-fats","bio-expl",4,"Ethanol emulsion test (fats and oils)","<ol>\n<li>Crush/cut the food and shake it with <strong>ethanol</strong> (about 2 cm³) — fats dissolve in ethanol. 2. Pour the ethanol (the liquid only) into a test tube of <strong>water</strong>. <strong>Positive: a milky-white emulsion forms</strong>. Negative: stays clear. <em>Safety:</em> ethanol is <strong>flammable</strong> — no naked flames. No heating needed.</li>\n</ol>",0,""],["n-b4-exp-benedict-s-test-reducing-s","bio-expl",4,"Benedict's test (reducing sugars)","<ol>\n<li>Add an <strong>equal volume (e.g. 2 cm³) of blue Benedict's solution</strong> to a solution of the food. 2. <strong>Heat in a water bath at about 80 °C</strong> (or boiling) for a few minutes. <strong>Positive: colour changes blue → green → yellow → orange → brick-red</strong> (precipitate). The further along the series, the <strong>more reducing sugar</strong> — so it is semi-quantitative. Negative: stays <strong>blue</strong>. Reducing sugars: glucose, maltose, fructose (NOT sucrose). <em>Safety:</em> eye protection; use a water bath, not a flame directly; point tube away.</li>\n</ol>",0,""],["n-b4-exp-starch-test-iodine-test","bio-expl",4,"Starch test (iodine test)","<p>Add a few drops of <strong>orange-brown iodine solution</strong> (iodine in potassium iodide) to the food (solid or solution) on a white tile. <strong>Positive: blue-black</strong>. Negative: stays orange-brown. No heating.</p>",0,""],["n-b4-exp-biuret-test-proteins","bio-expl",4,"Biuret test (proteins)","<p>Add <strong>biuret reagent</strong> (blue) — or add dilute <strong>sodium hydroxide</strong> then a few drops of dilute <strong>copper(II) sulfate</strong> — to a solution of the food and shake gently. <strong>Positive: blue → purple/violet</strong> (lilac). Negative: stays blue. No heating. <em>Safety:</em> sodium hydroxide is corrosive — eye protection.</p>",0,""],["n-b4-exp-dcpip-test-vitamin-c","bio-expl",4,"DCPIP test (vitamin C)","<p>Put <strong>1 cm³ of blue DCPIP solution</strong> in a test tube; add the food solution (e.g. fruit juice) <strong>drop by drop</strong> from a pipette, shaking after each drop. <strong>Positive: DCPIP turns colourless</strong> (decolourises). Count the drops needed: <strong>fewer drops = more vitamin C</strong> in the juice. Use the same volume/concentration of DCPIP each time; rinse the pipette between juices. (Mary Jones describes DCPIP as purple; most mark schemes say <strong>blue → colourless</strong>.)</p>",0,""],["n-b4-exp-elements-in-biological-mol","bio-expl",4,"Elements in biological molecules","<p><strong>Carbohydrates: C, H, O</strong> (H:O = 2:1). <strong>Fats and oils: C, H, O</strong> (much less oxygen than carbohydrates). <strong>Proteins: C, H, O, N</strong> (and sometimes S). In an exam <em>name</em> the elements in words if asked to \"name\" — don't just write symbols.</p>",0,""],["n-b4-exp-large-molecules-from-small","bio-expl",4,"Large molecules from smaller molecules","<ul>\n<li><strong>Starch, glycogen and cellulose</strong> are made from many <strong>glucose</strong> molecules joined in long chains.</li>\n<li><strong>Proteins</strong> are made from <strong>amino acids</strong> (20 different types) joined in chains; the sequence of amino acids decides the protein and its shape.</li>\n<li><strong>Fats and oils</strong> are made from <strong>fatty acids and glycerol</strong> (three fatty acids + one glycerol).</li>\n</ul>",0,""],["n-b4-exp-structure-of-dna","bio-expl",4,"Structure of DNA","<p><strong>Two strands coiled together to form a double helix.</strong> <strong>Each strand contains chemicals called bases</strong> (four kinds: A, T, C, G). <strong>Bonds between pairs of bases hold the strands together.</strong> <strong>The bases always pair up in the same way: A with T, and C with G</strong> (full names not required). Each strand is a chain of <strong>nucleotides</strong>; the <strong>sequence of bases</strong> is the genetic code.</p>",1,""],["n-b4-dif-summary-of-food-tests","bio-diff",4,"Summary of food tests","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Test for</th><th>Reagent (colour before)</th><th>Method</th><th>Positive result</th><th>Negative</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Starch</strong></td><td>Iodine solution (orange-brown)</td><td>Add drops to food; no heat</td><td><strong>Blue-black</strong></td><td>Orange-brown</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Reducing sugar</strong></td><td>Benedict's solution (blue)</td><td>Equal volume; <strong>heat in water bath ~80 °C</strong></td><td><strong>Green → yellow → orange → brick-red</strong></td><td>Blue</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Protein</strong></td><td>Biuret reagent (blue)</td><td>Add, shake; no heat</td><td><strong>Purple / violet</strong></td><td>Blue</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Fats &amp; oils</strong></td><td>Ethanol, then water</td><td>Shake food with ethanol; pour into water</td><td><strong>Milky-white emulsion</strong></td><td>Clear</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Vitamin C</strong></td><td>DCPIP (blue)</td><td>Add food drop by drop to DCPIP</td><td><strong>DCPIP decolourises</strong> (colourless)</td><td>Stays blue</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b4-dif-carbohydrate-vs-fat-vs-pro","bio-diff",4,"Carbohydrate vs fat vs protein","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Carbohydrate</th><th>Fat / oil</th><th>Protein</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Elements</td><td>C, H, O</td><td>C, H, O (less O)</td><td>C, H, O, N (S)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sub-units</td><td>Glucose (simple sugars)</td><td>Fatty acids + glycerol</td><td>Amino acids</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Large molecules</td><td>Starch, glycogen, cellulose</td><td>Triglycerides (fats/oils)</td><td>Enzymes, haemoglobin, antibodies, keratin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main role</td><td>Energy (respiration); cellulose for walls</td><td>Energy store, insulation, membranes</td><td>Growth &amp; repair, enzymes, hormones, antibodies</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Test</td><td>Iodine (starch); Benedict's (reducing sugar)</td><td>Ethanol emulsion</td><td>Biuret</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-dna-is-made-of-2-strands-o","bio-facts",4,"Structure of DNA: strands and shape?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">DNA is made of 2 strands of nucleotides coiled to form a double helix</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Each nucleotide carries one base; the strands are held together by bonds between complementary bases.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-there-are-20-different-ami","bio-facts",4,"How many different amino acids are there?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">There are 20 different amino acids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Their order (sequence) in the chain determines which protein is made and its shape.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-dcpip-blue-purple-liquid-b","bio-facts",4,"Vitamin C test: what happens to DCPIP?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">DCPIP (blue/purple liquid) becomes colourless when mixed with vitamin C</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The fewer drops of juice needed to decolourise a fixed volume, the more vitamin C.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-biuret-reagent-blue-change","bio-facts",4,"Biuret test for protein: colour change?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Biuret reagent (blue) changes to violet/purple with proteins</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>No heating needed.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-fat-molecules-glycerol-fat","bio-facts",4,"A fat molecule is made of…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fat molecules = glycerol + fatty acids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>One glycerol joined to three fatty acids.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-lipids-fats-oils","bio-facts",4,"Lipids = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lipids = fats + oils</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Fats solid, oils liquid at room temperature.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-benedict-s-solution-blue-l","bio-facts",4,"Benedict&#x27;s test for reducing sugar: colour change?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Benedict's solution (blue liquid) turns orange-red when heated with reducing sugar</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Green/yellow = a little sugar; brick-red = lots.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-starch-iodine-solution-blu","bio-facts",4,"Starch with iodine solution: colour?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Starch + iodine solution → blue-black</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Iodine solution is orange-brown to start with.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-liver-helps-to-keep-the-co","bio-facts",4,"Liver – role in blood glucose?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Liver – helps to keep the concentration of glucose in the blood constant</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Stores excess glucose as glycogen (under insulin) and releases glucose (under glucagon) — Chapter 13.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-glucose-a-carbohydrate-c-h","bio-facts",4,"Formula of glucose?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Glucose (a carbohydrate) – C₆H₁₂O₆</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Glucose is the monomer of starch, glycogen and cellulose.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-sucrose-is-not-a-reducing","bio-facts",4,"Is sucrose a reducing sugar?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sucrose is <strong>not</strong> a reducing sugar.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It gives a negative Benedict's test unless first broken down (by acid or sucrase).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-haemoglobin-enzymes-antibo","bio-facts",4,"Haemoglobin, enzymes, antibodies, insulin and keratin are all…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Haemoglobin, enzymes, antibodies, insulin and keratin are all proteins.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Proteins are denatured by heat and extreme pH.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b4-fac-fats-give-about-twice-as-m","bio-facts",4,"Energy per gram: fats compared with carbohydrates?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fats give about twice as much energy per gram as carbohydrates.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>≈ 39 kJ g⁻¹ vs ≈ 17 kJ g⁻¹.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-exp-how-enzymes-work-lock-and","bio-expl",5,"How enzymes work (lock and key)","<p>Each enzyme has an <strong>active site</strong> with a specific shape that is <strong>complementary</strong> to the shape of its <strong>substrate</strong>. The substrate (the substance that the enzyme acts on) collides with and fits into the active site → an <strong>enzyme–substrate complex</strong> forms → the reaction happens → the <strong>product(s)</strong> are released → the enzyme is unchanged and can catalyse another reaction.</p>",0,""],["n-b5-exp-specificity-of-enzymes","bio-expl",5,"Specificity of enzymes","<p>Each enzyme catalyses <strong>only one reaction / acts on only one substrate</strong> because the <strong>shape of its active site is complementary only to that substrate</strong> — only that substrate can fit. e.g. amylase digests starch but not protein; protease digests protein but not starch.</p>",1,""],["n-b5-exp-factors-that-affect-enzyme","bio-expl",5,"Factors that affect enzymes — temperature and pH","<p><strong>Temperature:</strong> as temperature rises, enzyme and substrate molecules have <strong>more kinetic energy</strong>, move faster and <strong>collide more often</strong> (more <strong>effective collisions</strong>) → more enzyme–substrate complexes → <strong>rate increases</strong> up to the <strong>optimum temperature</strong> (≈ 37 °C for human enzymes). Above the optimum, the enzyme molecule vibrates so much that its <strong>shape changes</strong>; the <strong>active site loses its shape</strong> and is no longer complementary to the substrate → substrate no longer fits → rate falls rapidly; the enzyme is <strong>denatured</strong> (permanent). At low temperatures enzymes are <strong>inactive, not denatured</strong> — they work again if warmed. <strong>pH:</strong> each enzyme has an <strong>optimum pH</strong>; a pH far above or below it <strong>changes the shape of the active site</strong> → substrate no longer fits → <strong>denatured</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-b5-for-rate-of-an-enzyme-reaction","bio-form",5,"Rate of an enzyme reaction","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">rate = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">time taken</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Unit s⁻¹. Repeat each temperature or pH three times and calculate the mean.</p>",0,""],["n-b5-dif-effect-of-low-temperature","bio-diff",5,"Effect of low temperature vs high temperature on enzymes","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Low temperature (e.g. 5 °C)</th><th>High temperature (e.g. 70 °C)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Kinetic energy of molecules</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collisions</td><td>Few — slow rate</td><td>Many, but active site changes shape</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Active site</td><td>Shape unchanged</td><td>Shape changed — no longer complementary</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enzyme</td><td><strong>Inactive</strong> (not denatured)</td><td><strong>Denatured</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>If returned to optimum temperature</td><td>Works again</td><td>Does <strong>not</strong> work again (permanent)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-optimum-ph-for-most-enzyme","bio-facts",5,"Optimum pH for most enzymes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Optimum pH for most enzymes ≈ pH 7</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Exceptions: <strong>pepsin pH 2</strong> (stomach); <strong>pancreatic enzymes/trypsin pH ≈ 8</strong> (alkaline duodenum).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-optimum-temperature-for-hu","bio-facts",5,"Optimum temperature for human enzymes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Optimum temperature for human enzymes ≈ 37 °C</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Hot-spring bacteria have enzymes with optimum temperatures up to 80 °C</strong>; many plant enzymes have lower optima.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-carbohydrases-break-down-c","bio-facts",5,"Carbohydrases – break down what?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Carbohydrases – break down carbohydrates</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Amylase (starch → maltose), maltase (maltose → glucose), sucrase (sucrose → glucose + fructose), lactase (lactose → glucose + galactose).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-sucrase-sucrose","bio-facts",5,"Sucrase – acts on?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sucrase – sucrose</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Sucrose → glucose + fructose.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-maltase-maltose","bio-facts",5,"Maltase – acts on?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Maltase – maltose</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Maltose → glucose (on membranes of small intestine epithelium).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-lipase-lipids","bio-facts",5,"Lipase – acts on?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lipase – lipids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Fats and oils → fatty acids + glycerol.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-catalase-h-o-water-oxygen","bio-facts",5,"Catalase – which reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Catalase – H₂O₂ → water + oxygen</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Found in almost all living cells (liver, potato, celery); removes toxic hydrogen peroxide; the fastest enzyme known. Easy to measure: collect/count O₂ bubbles or time floating paper discs.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-protease-protein-amino-aci","bio-facts",5,"Protease – which reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Protease – protein → amino acids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>e.g. pepsin (stomach), trypsin (duodenum).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-amylase-starch-maltose-a-s","bio-facts",5,"Amylase – which reaction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Amylase – starch → maltose (a sugar)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Salivary glands and pancreas.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-enzymes-are-not-used-up-an","bio-facts",5,"Are enzymes used up? How much is needed?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Enzymes are not used up and are only needed in small amounts.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They are reusable catalysts.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b5-fac-all-enzymes-are-proteins","bio-facts",5,"What type of molecule are all enzymes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">All enzymes are proteins.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So they are denatured like other proteins by heat and extreme pH.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-exp-how-plants-use-the-carbohy","bio-expl",6,"How plants use the carbohydrate made","<p>Glucose from photosynthesis is used for:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Releasing useful energy</strong> — <strong>glucose used in respiration</strong> to provide energy (for active transport of ions, protein synthesis, cell division, growth).</li>\n<li><strong>Storing, to use later</strong> — converted to <strong>starch as an energy store</strong> (insoluble, so it does not affect osmosis; stored in chloroplasts, roots, tubers, seeds).</li>\n<li><strong>Making sucrose, for transport</strong> — <strong>sucrose is transported in the phloem</strong> to other parts of the plant.</li>\n<li><strong>Making cellulose, to build cell walls</strong> — for growth of new cells.</li>\n<li><strong>Making nectar</strong> (sugary liquid) <strong>to attract insects for pollination</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Making amino acids, to make proteins</strong> — glucose + <strong>nitrate ions</strong> (from soil) → amino acids → proteins.</li>\n<li><strong>Making other substances, e.g. chlorophyll</strong> — needs glucose + nitrogen + <strong>magnesium ions</strong>; also fats/oils (stored in seeds).</li>\n</ol>",0,""],["n-b6-exp-factors-affecting-needed-f","bio-expl",6,"Factors affecting / needed for photosynthesis","<p><strong>Needed:</strong> carbon dioxide, water, light (sunlight), chlorophyll, a suitable temperature (enzymes). Investigate the <strong>need for chlorophyll, light and carbon dioxide</strong> using a destarched plant and the starch test, with controls (see practicals). <strong>Rate is affected by:</strong> light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature (and stomata being open, water supply).</p>",0,""],["n-b6-exp-importance-of-nitrate-and","bio-expl",6,"Importance of nitrate and magnesium ions","<p><strong>Nitrate ions</strong> — needed to <strong>make amino acids</strong> (and so proteins) — glucose + nitrogen. Deficiency → poor/stunted growth, yellowing older leaves. <strong>Magnesium ions</strong> — needed to <strong>make chlorophyll</strong> (each chlorophyll molecule contains a magnesium atom). Deficiency → <strong>yellowing between the veins of leaves (chlorosis)</strong> → less photosynthesis. Both are absorbed by <strong>active transport</strong> through root hairs.</p>",0,""],["n-b6-exp-leaf-adaptations-for-photo","bio-expl",6,"Leaf adaptations for photosynthesis","<p><strong>Large surface area</strong> — captures more light and gives more area for CO₂ to diffuse in. <strong>Thin</strong> — short diffusion distance for CO₂ to reach all cells; light passes through to inner cells. Internal structures — see diagram and table below.</p>",0,""],["n-b6-exp-hydrogencarbonate-indicato","bio-expl",6,"Hydrogencarbonate indicator","<p>Indicator that shows CO₂ concentration: <strong>red/orange = atmospheric CO₂ (≈0.04%)</strong>; <strong>yellow = more CO₂</strong> (more acidic); <strong>purple/magenta = less CO₂</strong>. Used to show gas exchange of an aquatic plant in light and dark.</p>",0,""],["n-b6-dif-photosynthesis-vs-respirat","bio-diff",6,"Photosynthesis vs respiration (in a plant)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Photosynthesis</th><th>Aerobic respiration</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Chloroplasts (cells with chlorophyll)</td><td>Mitochondria (all living cells)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>When</td><td>Only in light</td><td>All the time (day and night)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Raw materials</td><td>Carbon dioxide + water</td><td>Glucose + oxygen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Products</td><td>Glucose + oxygen</td><td>Carbon dioxide + water</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy</td><td><strong>Absorbs</strong> light energy, stores it in glucose</td><td><strong>Releases</strong> energy from glucose</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect on dry mass</td><td>Increases</td><td>Decreases</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b6-dif-palisade-mesophyll-vs-spon","bio-diff",6,"Palisade mesophyll vs spongy mesophyll","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Palisade mesophyll</th><th>Spongy mesophyll</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Position</td><td>Just below upper epidermis</td><td>Below palisade, above lower epidermis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cell shape</td><td>Tall, column-shaped, tightly packed</td><td>Irregular/rounded, loosely packed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chloroplasts</td><td><strong>Many</strong> — main site of photosynthesis</td><td>Fewer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Air spaces</td><td>Few</td><td><strong>Many large air spaces</strong> (gas exchange; large internal surface area)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-leaf-heated-in-alcohol-eth","bio-facts",6,"Starch test on a leaf: why heat it in alcohol?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Leaf heated in alcohol (ethanol) — to remove chlorophyll</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Chlorophyll dissolves in hot alcohol; this decolourises the leaf so the iodine colour can be seen. Heat the alcohol in a <strong>water bath with the flame turned off</strong> — alcohol is flammable.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-boiling-the-leaf-breaks-do","bio-facts",6,"Starch test on a leaf: why boil it first?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Boiling the leaf — breaks down the cell membranes</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Kills the leaf, stops reactions, and lets iodine get into the cells to reach the starch. Then rinse in warm water to soften the brittle leaf before adding iodine.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-very-hot-very-bright-day-p","bio-facts",6,"Very hot, very bright day – effect on photosynthesis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Very hot / very bright day — photosynthesis may slow</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Stomata close to reduce water loss, so CO₂ cannot enter; very high temperatures also start to denature enzymes.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-warm-day-faster-photosynth","bio-facts",6,"Warm day – effect on photosynthesis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Warm day — faster photosynthesis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>More kinetic energy for the enzyme-controlled reactions (up to the optimum).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-low-temperature-slow-photo","bio-facts",6,"Low temperature – effect on photosynthesis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Low temperature — slow photosynthesis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Enzymes work slowly; temperature is the limiting factor.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-dim-light-slow-photosynthe","bio-facts",6,"Dim light – effect on photosynthesis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Dim light — slow photosynthesis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Light intensity is the limiting factor. (In bright light the rate stops increasing because another factor — CO₂ or temperature — becomes limiting.)</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-hydrogencarbonate-indicato","bio-facts",6,"Hydrogencarbonate indicator – what does it show?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Hydrogencarbonate indicator — effect of light and dark on gas exchange in an aquatic plant</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Light: purple (CO₂ used up by photosynthesis faster than produced by respiration). Dark: yellow (respiration only, CO₂ produced). No plant (control): stays red.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-potassium-hydroxide-or-sod","bio-facts",6,"Potassium hydroxide (or soda lime) – what does it do?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Potassium hydroxide (or soda lime) — removes CO₂ from around a leaf</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Used to show CO₂ is needed. ⚠ Sodium <strong>hydrogencarbonate</strong> does the opposite — it <strong>adds/supplies</strong> CO₂ (used to raise CO₂ for pondweed). Hodder wrongly lists it as a CO₂ absorber.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-guard-cells-gas-exchange-b","bio-facts",6,"Guard cells – role?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Guard cells — gas exchange by diffusion through stomata</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Pairs of guard cells open and close each stoma. They are the <strong>only epidermis cells with chloroplasts</strong>. CO₂ diffuses in, O₂ and water vapour diffuse out.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-spongy-mesophyll-air-space","bio-facts",6,"Spongy mesophyll – key feature?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Spongy mesophyll — air spaces</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Interconnecting air spaces give a large internal surface area for gas exchange.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-palisade-mesophyll-main-si","bio-facts",6,"Palisade mesophyll – role?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Palisade mesophyll — main site of photosynthesis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Most chloroplasts; near the upper surface where light is brightest.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-epidermis-no-chlorophyll-e","bio-facts",6,"Leaf epidermis – does it photosynthesise?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Epidermis — no chlorophyll (except guard cells) — no photosynthesis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Transparent, so light passes through to the mesophyll; protects the leaf.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-0-04-of-air-is-co-it-reach","bio-facts",6,"Percentage of CO₂ in air, and how it reaches leaf cells?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">0.04% of air is CO₂; it reaches cells by diffusion</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So CO₂ is very often the limiting factor; glasshouses add extra CO₂ (paraffin burners, CO₂ cylinders).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-chlorophyll-contains-nitro","bio-facts",6,"Which elements does chlorophyll contain?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Chlorophyll contains nitrogen and magnesium</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So plants need nitrate and magnesium ions to make chlorophyll.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-starch-not-glucose-is-test","bio-facts",6,"In leaves, do you test for starch or glucose?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Starch, not glucose, is tested for in leaves.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Glucose is quickly converted to starch for storage; starch present = photosynthesis has occurred.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-oxygen-produced-can-be-sho","bio-facts",6,"How can oxygen production be shown?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Oxygen produced can be shown with pondweed (Elodea/Cabomba).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Gas collected in an inverted test tube relights a glowing splint.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b6-fac-light-intensity-1-distance","bio-facts",6,"Light intensity and distance from the lamp?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Light intensity ∝ 1 / distance²</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Moving a lamp from 10 cm to 20 cm reduces light intensity to ¼.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-nutrients-sources-and-impo","bio-expl",7,"Nutrients — sources and importance","<ul>\n<li><strong>Carbohydrates</strong> (rice, bread, potatoes, pasta, cassava, sugar) — main <strong>source of energy</strong> (respiration).</li>\n<li><strong>Fats and oils</strong> (butter, oil, cheese, nuts, meat) — <strong>energy store</strong> (about twice the energy per gram of carbohydrate), <strong>insulation</strong> (under skin), protection of organs, cell membranes.</li>\n<li><strong>Proteins</strong> (meat, fish, eggs, milk, beans, lentils, soya) — <strong>growth and repair</strong> (making new cells), enzymes, antibodies, haemoglobin, some hormones (insulin), muscle.</li>\n<li><strong>Vitamin C</strong> (citrus fruits, guava, tomatoes, fresh vegetables) — needed to make <strong>collagen</strong> for healthy skin, gums and blood vessels; deficiency → <strong>scurvy</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Vitamin D</strong> (oily fish, egg yolk, butter; made in <strong>skin in sunlight</strong>) — helps <strong>absorb calcium</strong> for strong bones and teeth; deficiency → <strong>rickets</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Calcium</strong> (milk, cheese, dairy, fish with bones) — <strong>strong bones and teeth</strong>, <strong>blood clotting</strong>, muscle contraction; deficiency → brittle bones/teeth, poor clotting (rickets with vitamin D lack).</li>\n<li><strong>Iron</strong> (red meat, liver, egg yolk, dark green vegetables, beans) — making <strong>haemoglobin</strong> in red blood cells; deficiency → <strong>anaemia</strong> (tiredness, less oxygen carried).</li>\n<li><strong>Fibre (roughage)</strong> (wholemeal bread, bran, vegetables, fruit — cellulose) — adds <strong>bulk</strong> so muscles of the gut can push food along by <strong>peristalsis</strong>; prevents <strong>constipation</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Water</strong> (drinks, food) — solvent for reactions and transport (plasma), digestion, excretion (urine), temperature control (sweat).</li>\n</ul>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-alimentary-canal","bio-expl",7,"Alimentary canal","<p>The tube food passes through: <strong>mouth → oesophagus → stomach → small intestine (duodenum, ileum) → large intestine (colon, rectum) → anus</strong>. <strong>Associated organs</strong> (food does not pass through them): <strong>salivary glands, pancreas, liver, gall bladder</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-functions-of-the-parts-of","bio-expl",7,"Functions of the parts of the digestive system","<ul>\n<li><strong>Mouth</strong> — ingestion; teeth chew (physical digestion); saliva added; <strong>salivary amylase</strong> starts starch digestion; tongue rolls food into a <strong>bolus</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Salivary glands</strong> — secrete <strong>saliva</strong>: water + mucus (lubricates food) + amylase.</li>\n<li><strong>Oesophagus</strong> — muscular tube; carries bolus to the stomach by <strong>peristalsis</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stomach</strong> — muscular wall churns food (physical digestion); <strong>gastric juice</strong>: <strong>protease (pepsin)</strong> + <strong>hydrochloric acid</strong> + mucus; stores food 1–2 h; sphincter releases food into duodenum.</li>\n<li><strong>Duodenum</strong> (1st part of small intestine) — receives <strong>pancreatic juice</strong> (amylase, trypsin/protease, lipase + hydrogencarbonate) and <strong>bile</strong>; chemical digestion continues.</li>\n<li><strong>Ileum</strong> (2nd part of small intestine) — <strong>maltase</strong> and other enzymes on epithelium membranes finish digestion; <strong>absorption</strong> of nutrients and <strong>most water</strong> through <strong>villi</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pancreas</strong> — secretes pancreatic juice (amylase, protease, lipase) into the duodenum via the pancreatic duct (also hormones insulin and glucagon into blood).</li>\n<li><strong>Liver</strong> — makes bile; assimilation (glycogen storage, protein synthesis); deamination.</li>\n<li><strong>Gall bladder</strong> — stores bile and releases it into the duodenum.</li>\n<li><strong>Colon</strong> — absorbs <strong>some water</strong> (and mineral ions) from undigested food → faeces.</li>\n<li><strong>Rectum</strong> — stores faeces. <strong>Anus</strong> — egestion of faeces (controlled by sphincter).</li>\n</ul>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-hydrochloric-acid-in-gastr","bio-expl",7,"Hydrochloric acid in gastric juice","<p>(1) <strong>Kills harmful microorganisms in food</strong> (defence against disease); (2) <strong>provides an acidic pH (≈ pH 2) for optimum activity of the stomach protease (pepsin)</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-digestive-enzymes-function","bio-expl",7,"Digestive enzymes — functions","<p><strong>Amylase</strong> breaks down <strong>starch → simple reducing sugars (maltose)</strong>. <strong>Proteases</strong> break down <strong>protein → amino acids</strong>. <strong>Lipase</strong> breaks down <strong>fats and oils → fatty acids and glycerol</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-where-enzymes-are-secreted","bio-expl",7,"Where enzymes are secreted and act","<p><strong>Amylase:</strong> secreted by <strong>salivary glands</strong> (acts in <strong>mouth</strong>) and <strong>pancreas</strong> (acts in <strong>duodenum</strong>). <strong>Protease:</strong> secreted by <strong>stomach wall</strong> (pepsin, acts in <strong>stomach</strong>) and <strong>pancreas</strong> (trypsin, acts in <strong>duodenum</strong>). <strong>Lipase:</strong> secreted by <strong>pancreas</strong>, acts in <strong>duodenum</strong>. <strong>Maltase:</strong> made by <strong>small-intestine epithelium</strong>; acts <strong>on the membranes of the epithelium</strong> lining the small intestine.</p>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-digestion-of-starch","bio-expl",7,"Digestion of starch","<p><strong>Amylase breaks down starch to maltose</strong> (mouth and duodenum); <strong>maltase breaks down maltose to glucose on the membranes of the epithelium lining the small intestine</strong>. Glucose is then absorbed.</p>",1,""],["n-b7-exp-digestion-of-protein","bio-expl",7,"Digestion of protein","<p><strong>Pepsin breaks down protein in the acidic conditions of the stomach</strong>; <strong>trypsin breaks down protein in the alkaline conditions of the small intestine</strong>. Products: smaller peptides → amino acids (finished by other proteases in the small intestine).</p>",1,""],["n-b7-exp-absorption","bio-expl",7,"Absorption","<p><strong>The small intestine is the region where nutrients are absorbed</strong> (mainly the ileum). <strong>Most water is absorbed from the small intestine but some is also absorbed from the colon.</strong></p>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-villi-and-microvilli","bio-expl",7,"Villi and microvilli","<p><strong>Villi</strong> = finger-like projections (≈1 mm) of the small-intestine lining; <strong>microvilli</strong> = tiny folds of the cell membrane of each epithelial cell on a villus. Both <strong>greatly increase the internal surface area</strong>, so absorption is much faster.</p>",1,""],["n-b7-exp-structure-of-a-villus","bio-expl",7,"Structure of a villus","<p>Covered by a <strong>one-cell-thick epithelium</strong> (short diffusion distance) with <strong>microvilli</strong>; <strong>goblet cells</strong> secrete mucus; inside: a <strong>network of blood capillaries</strong> and a central <strong>lacteal</strong>; supplied by a small artery and drained to the hepatic portal vein. Epithelial cells have many <strong>mitochondria</strong> (energy for active transport of glucose).</p>",1,""],["n-b7-exp-roles-of-capillaries-and-l","bio-expl",7,"Roles of capillaries and lacteals","<p><strong>Capillaries</strong> absorb <strong>glucose, amino acids, water, vitamins and mineral ions</strong> → carried to the liver in the hepatic portal vein. <strong>Lacteals</strong> absorb <strong>fatty acids and glycerol</strong> → lymph → emptied into the blood. Blood flow carries absorbed nutrients away, <strong>maintaining the concentration gradient</strong> for diffusion.</p>",1,""],["n-b7-exp-teeth-types-and-functions","bio-expl",7,"Teeth — types and functions","<p><strong>Incisors</strong> (front, chisel-shaped) — <strong>biting/cutting</strong> off pieces of food. <strong>Canines</strong> (next to incisors, pointed) — <strong>gripping and tearing</strong> (similar to incisors in humans). <strong>Premolars</strong> (behind canines, broad ridged surface, 1–2 roots) — <strong>chewing and grinding</strong>. <strong>Molars</strong> (back, larger, broad with cusps, 2–3 roots) — <strong>chewing and grinding</strong>. Grinding increases surface area for enzymes.</p>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-tooth-structure","bio-expl",7,"Tooth structure","<p><strong>Enamel</strong> (hardest substance in body, covers crown), <strong>dentine</strong> (under enamel, hard, like bone, living channels), <strong>pulp cavity</strong> (contains <strong>nerves and blood vessels</strong>), <strong>cement</strong> (covers root, fibres attach tooth to <strong>jaw bone</strong>). Teeth are <strong>embedded in the bone of the jaw and the gums</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-b7-exp-scurvy-and-rickets","bio-expl",7,"Scurvy and rickets","<p><strong>Scurvy</strong> — caused by <strong>lack of vitamin C</strong>: bleeding/swollen gums, loose teeth, bleeding under skin, slow wound healing, tiredness. <strong>Rickets</strong> — caused by <strong>lack of vitamin D (or calcium)</strong>: soft, weak, deformed bones (bow legs) in children.</p>",0,""],["n-b7-dif-absorption-vs-assimilation","bio-diff",7,"Absorption vs assimilation","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Absorption</th><th>Assimilation</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Movement of nutrients from the intestines <strong>into the blood</strong></td><td>Uptake and <strong>use</strong> of nutrients by <strong>cells</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Small intestine (villi); some water in colon</td><td>Body cells, e.g. liver, muscle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>Glucose diffuses/actively transported into villus capillaries</td><td>Liver converts glucose to glycogen; cells make proteins from amino acids</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b7-dif-ingestion-vs-egestion","bio-diff",7,"Ingestion vs egestion","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Ingestion</th><th>Egestion</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Taking food and drink <strong>into</strong> the body</td><td>Removal of <strong>undigested food</strong> from the body as <strong>faeces</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Mouth</td><td>Anus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stage</td><td>First</td><td>Last</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Not to confuse with</td><td>—</td><td>Excretion (removal of metabolic waste, e.g. urea)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b7-dif-physical-digestion-vs-chem","bio-diff",7,"Physical digestion vs chemical digestion","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Physical digestion</th><th>Chemical digestion</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What happens</td><td>Food broken into <strong>smaller pieces</strong></td><td>Large insoluble molecules broken into <strong>small soluble molecules</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chemical change?</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — bonds broken</td></tr>\n<tr><td>By</td><td>Teeth, stomach churning, bile (emulsification)</td><td><strong>Enzymes</strong> (amylase, protease, lipase, maltase)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Increase <strong>surface area</strong> for enzymes</td><td>Make molecules small enough to be <strong>absorbed</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Mouth, stomach, duodenum</td><td>Mouth, stomach, duodenum, ileum</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b7-dif-pancreatic-duct-vs-bile-du","bio-diff",7,"Pancreatic duct vs bile duct","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Pancreatic duct</th><th>Bile duct</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Carries</td><td><strong>Pancreatic juice</strong> (amylase, trypsin/protease, lipase, hydrogencarbonate)</td><td><strong>Bile</strong> (bile salts, no enzymes)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>From</td><td>Pancreas</td><td>Gall bladder (bile made in liver)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>To</td><td>Duodenum</td><td>Duodenum</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Role</td><td>Chemical digestion of starch, protein, fat</td><td>Emulsification + neutralising stomach acid</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b7-dif-summary-of-digestive-enzym","bio-diff",7,"Summary of digestive enzymes","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Enzyme</th><th>Secreted by</th><th>Acts in</th><th>Substrate → products</th><th>Optimum pH</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Salivary amylase</td><td>Salivary glands</td><td>Mouth</td><td>Starch → maltose</td><td>≈ 7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pepsin (protease)</td><td>Stomach wall</td><td>Stomach</td><td>Protein → peptides/amino acids</td><td>≈ 2 (acid)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pancreatic amylase</td><td>Pancreas</td><td>Duodenum</td><td>Starch → maltose</td><td>≈ 7–8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trypsin (protease)</td><td>Pancreas</td><td>Duodenum</td><td>Protein → peptides/amino acids</td><td>≈ 8 (alkaline)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lipase</td><td>Pancreas</td><td>Duodenum</td><td>Fats/oils → fatty acids + glycerol</td><td>≈ 8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Maltase</td><td>Small-intestine epithelium</td><td>Membranes of ileum epithelium</td><td>Maltose → glucose</td><td>≈ 7–8</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-bile-emulsifies-fat","bio-facts",7,"What does bile do to fat?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bile emulsifies fat</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Physical, not chemical digestion — bile contains no enzymes.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-stomach-wall-pepsin-ph-2","bio-facts",7,"Stomach wall – enzyme and pH?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Stomach wall — pepsin, pH 2</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The stomach also secretes mucus to protect its wall from acid and pepsin.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-small-intestine-maltase","bio-facts",7,"Small intestine lining – which enzyme?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Small intestine — maltase</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Maltase is not secreted into the lumen; it stays on the membranes (microvilli) of the epithelial cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-duodenum-ph-7-so-enzymes-w","bio-facts",7,"pH in the duodenum, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Duodenum pH &gt; 7 so enzymes work fast</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Pancreatic juice (hydrogencarbonate) and bile neutralise stomach acid; pancreatic enzymes have optimum ≈ pH 8.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-pancreas-trypsin-protease","bio-facts",7,"Enzymes from the pancreas, and where they act?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pancreas → trypsin (protease), amylase, lipase — all act in the duodenum</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Also secretes hydrogencarbonate ions.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-walls-of-stomach-protease","bio-facts",7,"Walls of the stomach – which enzyme, acting where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Walls of stomach — protease (pepsin) acts in stomach</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-salivary-glands-amylase-ac","bio-facts",7,"Salivary glands – which enzyme, acting where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Salivary glands — amylase acts in mouth</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Starch digestion continues briefly in the bolus until stomach acid denatures the amylase.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-incisor-canine-premolar-mo","bio-facts",7,"The four types of teeth, front to back?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Incisor, canine, premolar, molar</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Adults: 8 incisors, 4 canines, 8 premolars, 12 molars (32 teeth).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-wisdom-teeth-third-molars","bio-facts",7,"Wisdom teeth = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Wisdom teeth = third molars</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Grow much later (late teens or after).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-dentine-quite-hard-rather","bio-facts",7,"Dentine – how hard?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Dentine — quite hard, rather like bone</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Living tissue with channels containing cytoplasm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-enamel-hardest-substance-i","bio-facts",7,"Enamel – how hard, and what damages it?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Enamel — hardest substance in body but dissolved by acids</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Bacteria in plaque feed on sugar and make acid → tooth decay. Calcium and vitamin D (and fluoride) help healthy enamel.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-liver-secretes-bile-bile-s","bio-facts",7,"Where is bile made, and where is it stored?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Liver secretes bile; bile stored in gall bladder</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-pancreas-and-liver-are-par","bio-facts",7,"Are the pancreas and liver part of the alimentary canal?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pancreas and liver are part of the digestive system but not of the alimentary canal</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Food never passes through them — they are associated organs.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-rectum-stores-undigested-f","bio-facts",7,"Rectum – function?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Rectum stores undigested food (faeces)</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-large-intestine-colon-rect","bio-facts",7,"Large intestine = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Large intestine = colon + rectum</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Colon absorbs some water.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-ileum-digested-nutrients-a","bio-facts",7,"Ileum – what is absorbed there?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Ileum — digested nutrients and water absorbed here</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Most absorption happens in the ileum.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-duodenum-first-part-of-sma","bio-facts",7,"Duodenum – which part of the gut?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Duodenum — first part of small intestine</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Pancreatic duct and bile duct empty into it.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-hcl-in-stomach-ph-2-optimu","bio-facts",7,"Hydrochloric acid in the stomach – pH and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">HCl in stomach — pH 2 (optimum for protease/pepsin)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>And kills microorganisms in food.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-goblet-cells-secrete-mucus","bio-facts",7,"Goblet cells – secrete what?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Goblet cells secrete mucus</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Found along the <strong>whole</strong> alimentary canal (including the stomach) and in the trachea/bronchi.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-lumen-the-hole-in-the-cent","bio-facts",7,"Lumen = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lumen — the hole in the centre of the oesophagus (any tube) through which food passes</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Blood vessels and the trachea also have a lumen.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-saliva-water-mucus-amylase","bio-facts",7,"Saliva = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Saliva = water + mucus + amylase</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Mucus lubricates the bolus for swallowing.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-alimentary-canal-has-sphin","bio-facts",7,"What are sphincter muscles?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alimentary canal has sphincter muscles</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Rings of muscle that contract to close the tube (e.g. top and bottom of stomach, anus).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-mucus-is-made-in-goblet-ce","bio-facts",7,"Where is mucus made?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Mucus is made in goblet cells</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Lubricates food; protects lining.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-cytoplasm-is-a-solution-of","bio-facts",7,"Cytoplasm is a solution of…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cytoplasm is a solution of many substances in water</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><blockquote>\n<p>60% of the body is water.</p>\n</blockquote></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-calcium-deficiency-brittle","bio-facts",7,"Calcium deficiency – effects?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Calcium deficiency → brittle bones and teeth, poor blood clotting</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-lack-of-vitamin-c-scurvy-l","bio-facts",7,"Lack of vitamin C and lack of vitamin D cause…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lack of vitamin C → scurvy; lack of vitamin D → rickets</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-protein-builds-new-cells-g","bio-facts",7,"Protein – uses in the body?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Protein — builds new cells (growth), makes proteins such as haemoglobin, antibodies, insulin, enzymes</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-fat-insulator-heat-mechani","bio-facts",7,"Fat – uses in the body?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fat — insulator (heat), mechanical protection of organs, energy store</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-villi-and-microvilli-incre","bio-facts",7,"Villi and microvilli – what do they do?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Villi and microvilli increase surface area of the small intestine, speeding up absorption</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Small intestine ≈ 5 m long — length also increases surface area and time for absorption.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-humans-cannot-digest-cellu","bio-facts",7,"Why does cellulose act as fibre?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Humans cannot digest cellulose — that is why it acts as fibre.</p>",0,""],["n-b7-fac-food-stays-in-the-stomach","bio-facts",7,"How long does food stay in the stomach?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Food stays in the stomach about 1–2 hours.</p>",0,""],["n-b8-exp-transpiration-pull","bio-expl",8,"Transpiration pull","<p>The 'pulling' force produced by the loss of water vapour from leaves. Water evaporating from mesophyll cells is replaced by water drawn from the xylem, which <strong>reduces the pressure at the top of the xylem vessels</strong>. Because <strong>water molecules are attracted to each other (cohesion)</strong>, the water in each xylem vessel forms a <strong>continuous column</strong> that is <strong>pulled up</strong> the stem from the roots, like drinking through a straw. (Exam wording: <em>\"a transpiration pull draws up a column of water molecules, held together by forces of attraction between water molecules.\"</em>)</p>",1,""],["n-b8-exp-conditions-affecting-trans","bio-expl",8,"Conditions affecting transpiration rate","<p>All are about <strong>evaporation and diffusion of water vapour</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Temperature ↑ → rate ↑</strong>: water molecules have more <strong>kinetic energy</strong>, so evaporate faster from mesophyll surfaces and diffuse out faster.</li>\n<li><strong>Wind speed ↑ → rate ↑</strong>: wind blows water vapour away from the leaf surface, keeping the <strong>concentration (water potential) gradient</strong> between the air spaces and the outside air steep.</li>\n<li><strong>Humidity ↑ → rate ↓</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: the air outside already contains a lot of water vapour, so the <strong>concentration gradient is shallower</strong> and less water vapour diffuses out.</li>\n<li>(Also: <strong>light intensity ↑ → rate ↑</strong> because stomata open in light.)</li>\n</ul>",0,""],["n-b8-exp-wilting","bio-expl",8,"Wilting","<p><strong>How:</strong> when a plant <strong>loses more water by transpiration than it takes up</strong> through its roots, its cells lose water by osmosis, become <strong>flaccid</strong>, no longer press against each other, so leaves and soft stems lose their support and <strong>droop</strong>. <strong>Why it happens:</strong> hot, dry, windy weather (high transpiration) or dry soil / damaged roots (low uptake). <strong>Advantage:</strong> wilted leaves fold and stomata close, reducing the surface area exposed to the sun → less water loss. <strong>Disadvantage:</strong> less CO₂ uptake → less photosynthesis.</p>",1,""],["n-b8-exp-structure-of-xylem-vessels","bio-expl",8,"Structure of xylem vessels related to function","<ul>\n<li><strong>Thick walls with lignin</strong> (strong, waterproof) → support the plant; stop vessels collapsing under the tension of transpiration pull.</li>\n<li><strong>No cell contents</strong> (dead, no cytoplasm or nucleus) → nothing to block the flow of water.</li>\n<li><strong>Cells joined end to end with no cross walls</strong>, forming a <strong>long continuous tube</strong> → water flows freely in an unbroken column from root to leaf.</li>\n</ul>",1,""],["n-b8-exp-root-hair-cells","bio-expl",8,"Root hair cells","<p>Long thin extensions of root epidermis cells just behind the root tip. Function: <strong>absorption of water (by osmosis) and mineral ions (by active transport)</strong>. Their <strong>large surface area increases the uptake of water and mineral ions</strong>. No chloroplasts (underground).</p>",0,""],["n-b8-exp-pathway-of-water","bio-expl",8,"Pathway of water","<p><strong>Root hair cells → root cortex cells → xylem → mesophyll cells</strong> (then evaporates into air spaces → out through stomata). Water moves from cell to cell across the root cortex by <strong>osmosis</strong> (each cell has a slightly lower water potential than the one before).</p>",0,""],["n-b8-exp-water-vapour-loss-and-leaf","bio-expl",8,"Water vapour loss and leaf structure","<p>The <strong>interconnecting air spaces between mesophyll cells give a very large internal surface area</strong> from which water evaporates, so lots of water vapour is lost. Water vapour diffuses out through the <strong>stomata</strong> — the <strong>more and larger the stomata (more open)</strong>, the more water vapour is lost. Stomata close in darkness and when the plant is short of water, reducing loss.</p>",1,""],["n-b8-exp-why-a-part-can-be-a-source","bio-expl",8,"Why a part can be a source and a sink at different times","<p>A <strong>potato tuber</strong> (or bulb) is a <strong>sink</strong> in summer when it is filling up with starch made from sucrose arriving from the leaves; it becomes a <strong>source</strong> in spring when its starch is broken down to sucrose and translocated to the growing shoots (before they have leaves to photosynthesise). A young leaf is a sink (growing); a mature leaf becomes a source.</p>",1,""],["n-b8-exp-potometer","bio-expl",8,"Potometer","<p>Apparatus that measures the <strong>rate of water uptake</strong> by a leafy shoot (approximately = rate of transpiration, since ≈99% of water taken up is lost). Rate = distance moved by the air bubble ÷ time (or volume = distance × cross-sectional area of capillary).</p>",0,""],["n-b8-for-rate-of-water-uptake-potom","bio-form",8,"Rate of water uptake (potometer)","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">rate = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">distance moved by the air bubble</span><span class=\"de\">time</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">This is approximately the rate of transpiration, because about 99% of the water taken up is lost. Volume taken up = distance × cross-sectional area of the capillary.</p>",0,""],["n-b8-dif-xylem-vs-phloem","bio-diff",8,"Xylem vs phloem","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Xylem</th><th>Phloem</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Transports</td><td><strong>Water and mineral ions</strong></td><td><strong>Sucrose and amino acids</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Direction</td><td><strong>Upwards only</strong> (roots → leaves)</td><td><strong>Up and down</strong> (source → sink)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cells</td><td><strong>Dead</strong>, hollow, no cytoplasm/nucleus</td><td><strong>Living</strong> cells (sieve tubes with cytoplasm, sieve plates; companion cells)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Walls</td><td>Thick, <strong>lignified</strong></td><td>Thin, cellulose, no lignin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>End walls</td><td><strong>None</strong> — continuous tube</td><td>Sieve plates (perforated cross walls)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Other function</td><td><strong>Support</strong></td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Driven by</td><td>Transpiration pull (no energy from plant)</td><td>Needs energy (living cells)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Position in stem (dicot)</td><td>Inner side of vascular bundle</td><td>Outer side of vascular bundle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Position in root</td><td><strong>Star/X-shape in centre</strong></td><td>Between the arms of the xylem</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Position in leaf vein</td><td>Upper side of vascular bundle</td><td>Lower side of vascular bundle</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b8-dif-source-vs-sink","bio-diff",8,"Source vs sink","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Source</th><th>Sink</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Part of plant that <strong>releases</strong> sucrose/amino acids</td><td>Part of plant that <strong>uses or stores</strong> them</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Photosynthesising leaves; storage organs in spring</td><td>Root tips, shoot tips, flowers, fruits, seeds, storage organs in summer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Process there</td><td>Photosynthesis / breakdown of stored starch</td><td>Respiration, growth, storage as starch</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b8-fac-high-temperature-high-wind","bio-facts",8,"Effect of high temperature and high wind speed on transpiration?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">High temperature, high wind speed → ↑ rate of transpiration</p>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-humidity-transpiration","bio-facts",8,"Effect of higher humidity on transpiration?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Humidity ↑ → transpiration ↓</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Shallower water-vapour concentration gradient.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-water-from-xylem-vessels-t","bio-facts",8,"How does water move from xylem to mesophyll cells?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Water from xylem vessels travels to mesophyll cells by osmosis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Then evaporates from their surfaces into the air spaces.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-vascular-bundles-xylem-ves","bio-facts",8,"Vascular bundles = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Vascular bundles = xylem vessels + phloem tubes</p>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-xylem-lignified-so-plants","bio-facts",8,"Xylem is lignified: what does that do?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Xylem — lignified, so plants stay upright</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Lignin makes xylem strong; wood is mostly xylem.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-phloem-living-cells-transp","bio-facts",8,"Phloem – living or dead? Transports what?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Phloem — living cells; transports sucrose and amino acids</p>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-xylem-dead-cells-transport","bio-facts",8,"Xylem – living or dead? Transports what?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Xylem — dead cells; transports water and mineral ions</p>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-branching-shape-of-plants","bio-facts",8,"Branching shape of plants – advantage?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Branching shape of plants — large surface area</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Most cells are near the surface, so gases can diffuse in/out of leaves directly; plants have no transport system for gases.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-water-does-not-move-up-xyl","bio-facts",8,"Does water move up the xylem by osmosis?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Water does <strong>not</strong> move up xylem by osmosis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Xylem cells have no membranes; water moves by mass flow due to transpiration pull. (Hodder also mentions capillarity and root pressure — these are minor; the mark scheme wants <strong>transpiration pull + cohesion</strong>.)</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-minerals-are-absorbed-by-a","bio-facts",8,"How are minerals and water absorbed by roots?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Minerals are absorbed by active transport, water by osmosis</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Both through root hair cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-stomata-are-mostly-on-the","bio-facts",8,"Where are most stomata on a dicot leaf, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Stomata are mostly on the lower surface of dicot leaves</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Cooler and shaded, so less evaporation.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b8-fac-dyes-e-g-eosin-methylene-b","bio-facts",8,"How can you show the pathway of water in a plant?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Dyes (e.g. eosin, methylene blue, food colouring) show the pathway of water</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Only the <strong>xylem</strong> is stained.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-exp-how-the-heart-beats-cardia","bio-expl",9,"How the heart beats (cardiac cycle)","<ol>\n<li>Heart muscle relaxed: blood flows from the <strong>vena cava</strong> (right) and <strong>pulmonary vein</strong> (left) into the <strong>atria</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Atria contract</strong> → pressure in atria rises → blood pushed through the open <strong>atrioventricular (AV) valves</strong> into the <strong>ventricles</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricles contract</strong> → pressure in ventricles rises → <strong>AV valves close</strong> (prevent backflow into atria; tendons stop them turning inside out) → <strong>semilunar valves open</strong> → blood forced into the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong> (right) and <strong>aorta</strong> (left).</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricles relax</strong> → pressure in ventricles falls → <strong>semilunar valves close</strong> (prevent backflow from arteries into ventricles) → cycle repeats. The \"lub\" sound = AV valves shutting; \"dub\" = semilunar valves shutting.</li>\n</ol>",1,""],["n-b9-exp-sequence-of-blood-clot-for","bio-expl",9,"Sequence of blood clot formation","<p><strong>Clotting is the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin to form a mesh.</strong> Sequence: 1. A blood vessel is damaged (cut). 2. <strong>Platelets</strong> stick to the damaged area, clump together and release a chemical (enzyme). 3. This causes soluble <strong>fibrinogen</strong> (a plasma protein) to be converted into insoluble <strong>fibrin</strong>. 4. Fibrin forms a <strong>mesh of fibres</strong> across the wound. 5. <strong>Red blood cells</strong> (and platelets) get trapped in the mesh → <strong>clot</strong>; it dries to a scab. <strong>Roles of clotting</strong>: <strong>prevents blood loss</strong> and <strong>prevents entry of pathogens</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-b9-exp-substances-transported-in","bio-expl",9,"Substances transported in blood plasma","<p>Plasma = pale yellow liquid, mostly <strong>water</strong>, transporting:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Water</strong> (solvent).</li>\n<li><strong>Proteins</strong> — <strong>fibrinogen</strong> (clotting), <strong>antibodies</strong>, albumin.</li>\n<li><strong>Lipids</strong> — <strong>cholesterol</strong>, fatty acids.</li>\n<li><strong>Carbohydrates</strong> — <strong>glucose</strong> (from small intestine/liver to all cells).</li>\n<li><strong>Excretory substances</strong> — <strong>urea</strong> (from liver to kidneys).</li>\n<li><strong>Mineral ions</strong> — e.g. <strong>Na⁺, Cl⁻</strong>, Ca²⁺.</li>\n<li><strong>Hormones</strong> — e.g. insulin, adrenaline (from glands to target organs).</li>\n<li><strong>Dissolved gases</strong> — <strong>carbon dioxide</strong> (mostly as hydrogencarbonate, from cells to lungs).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Also: amino acids, vitamins, <strong>heat</strong>, <strong>blood cells</strong> (syllabus: plasma transports <strong>blood cells, ions, nutrients, urea, hormones and carbon dioxide</strong>).</p>",0,""],["n-b9-exp-single-circulation-of-a-fi","bio-expl",9,"Single circulation of a fish","<p>Blood passes through the heart <strong>once</strong> in each complete circuit: <strong>heart → gills → body → heart</strong>. The heart has <strong>one atrium and one ventricle</strong>. Blood loses pressure passing through gill capillaries, so it flows slowly to the body.</p>",1,""],["n-b9-exp-double-circulation-of-a-ma","bio-expl",9,"Double circulation of a mammal","<p>Blood passes through the heart <strong>twice</strong> in each complete circuit: <strong>pulmonary circulation</strong> (heart → lungs → heart) and <strong>systemic circulation</strong> (heart → body → heart). Right side pumps deoxygenated blood to the lungs; left side pumps oxygenated blood to the body.</p>",1,""],["n-b9-exp-advantages-of-double-circu","bio-expl",9,"Advantages of double circulation","<p>Blood returning from the lungs (low pressure after the lung capillaries) is <strong>pumped again by the heart</strong>, so it reaches the body at <strong>high pressure</strong> → <strong>faster delivery of oxygen and glucose</strong> to respiring tissues (needed by active, warm-blooded mammals with high metabolic rates). Also <strong>lower pressure to the lungs</strong> prevents damage to delicate lung capillaries; oxygenated and deoxygenated blood are kept separate.</p>",1,""],["n-b9-exp-structure-of-the-heart","bio-expl",9,"Structure of the heart","<p><strong>Muscular wall</strong> (cardiac muscle), <strong>septum</strong>, <strong>left and right atria</strong>, <strong>left and right ventricles</strong>, <strong>one-way valves</strong> — <strong>atrioventricular</strong> (between atria and ventricles: bicuspid/mitral on left, tricuspid on right) and <strong>semilunar</strong> (at bases of aorta and pulmonary artery) — and <strong>coronary arteries</strong> on the outside. <strong>Blood is pumped away from the heart in arteries and returns to the heart in veins</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-b9-exp-relative-thickness-of-hear","bio-expl",9,"Relative thickness of heart walls","<p><strong>Left ventricle wall is thicker than right</strong>: it must create higher pressure to pump blood all round the body (long distance, high resistance); the right only pumps to the nearby lungs (and lower pressure protects lung capillaries). <strong>Atria walls are thinner than ventricles</strong>: atria only pump blood a short distance into the ventricles; ventricles pump out of the heart to lungs/body.</p>",1,""],["n-b9-exp-importance-of-the-septum","bio-expl",9,"Importance of the septum","<p>Separates the left and right sides so <strong>oxygenated and deoxygenated blood do not mix</strong> — so blood going to the body is fully oxygenated and carries as much oxygen as possible.</p>",1,""],["n-b9-exp-effect-of-physical-activit","bio-expl",9,"Effect of physical activity on heart rate","<p>Heart rate <strong>increases</strong> during exercise and stays raised for a while afterwards, then returns to resting. <strong>Explanation:</strong> contracting muscles respire faster, needing <strong>more oxygen and glucose</strong> and producing more <strong>CO₂</strong>; a faster heart rate increases blood flow to deliver O₂/glucose and remove CO₂ (and lactic acid). Adrenaline also raises heart rate. After exercise, heart rate stays high to repay the <strong>oxygen debt</strong> (carry lactic acid to the liver).</p>",1,""],["n-b9-exp-capillaries-functions","bio-expl",9,"Capillaries — functions","<p>Exchange of substances between blood and body cells: O₂, glucose, amino acids out to the cells (via tissue fluid); CO₂ and urea into the blood. Link arterioles to venules.</p>",0,""],["n-b9-exp-structure-related-to-press","bio-expl",9,"Structure related to pressure/function","<p><strong>Arteries:</strong> thick, muscular, <strong>elastic</strong> walls withstand and maintain <strong>high pressure</strong>; elastic tissue stretches and recoils to even out flow; narrow lumen keeps pressure high. <strong>Veins:</strong> thin walls (low pressure); <strong>wide lumen</strong> reduces resistance; <strong>valves</strong> prevent backflow; blood squeezed along by contraction of surrounding skeletal muscles. <strong>Capillaries:</strong> walls <strong>one cell thick</strong> → short diffusion distance; very narrow (red cells pass in single file, slowly) → time for exchange; <strong>leaky</strong> (gaps let plasma out as tissue fluid and let white cells out); huge numbers → large surface area; close to every cell.</p>",1,""],["n-b9-exp-main-blood-vessels","bio-expl",9,"Main blood vessels","<p><strong>Heart:</strong> vena cava (in, right atrium), pulmonary artery (out of right ventricle to lungs), pulmonary vein (lungs → left atrium), aorta (out of left ventricle to body). <strong>Lungs:</strong> pulmonary artery and pulmonary vein. <strong>Kidney:</strong> renal artery and renal vein. <strong>Liver</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: <strong>hepatic artery</strong> (oxygenated blood from aorta), <strong>hepatic portal vein</strong> (from small intestine, rich in absorbed food), <strong>hepatic veins</strong> (from liver to vena cava).</p>",0,""],["n-b9-exp-components-of-blood","bio-expl",9,"Components of blood","<p><strong>Red blood cells</strong> — transport oxygen (haemoglobin combines with O₂ → oxyhaemoglobin in lungs, releases it in tissues). <strong>White blood cells</strong> — phagocytosis and antibody production. <strong>Platelets</strong> — cell fragments, clotting (details not required for Core). <strong>Plasma</strong> — transport of blood cells, ions, nutrients, urea, hormones, CO₂.</p>",0,""],["n-b9-exp-lymphocytes-and-phagocytes","bio-expl",9,"Lymphocytes and phagocytes","<p><strong>Lymphocytes</strong> — antibody production; <strong>large round nucleus filling most of the cell</strong>, little cytoplasm. <strong>Phagocytes</strong> — engulfing pathogens by phagocytosis; <strong>lobed nucleus</strong>, granular cytoplasm, larger.</p>",1,""],["n-b9-for-heart-rate-from-an-ecg","bio-form",9,"Heart rate from an ECG","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">heart rate (beats per minute) = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">60</span><span class=\"de\">time between two R peaks (s)</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. peaks 0.8 s apart → 60 ÷ 0.8 = 75 beats per minute.</p>",0,""],["n-b9-dif-double-vs-single-circulato","bio-diff",9,"Double vs single circulatory system","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Single (fish)</th><th>Double (mammal)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Passes through heart per circuit</td><td>Once</td><td><strong>Twice</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Route</td><td>Heart → gills → body → heart</td><td>Heart → lungs → heart → body → heart</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heart chambers</td><td>1 atrium + 1 ventricle</td><td>2 atria + 2 ventricles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pressure to body</td><td><strong>Low</strong> (lost in gill capillaries)</td><td><strong>High</strong> (re-pumped after lungs)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed of O₂ delivery</td><td>Slower</td><td><strong>Faster</strong> — suits high metabolic rate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gas exchange organ</td><td>Gills</td><td>Lungs</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b9-dif-oxygenated-vs-deoxygenated","bio-diff",9,"Oxygenated vs deoxygenated blood","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Oxygenated</th><th>Deoxygenated</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Oxygen</td><td>High (lots of oxyhaemoglobin)</td><td>Low</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CO₂</td><td>Low</td><td>Higher</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colour</td><td>Bright red</td><td>Dark red (purplish)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Found in</td><td>Pulmonary vein, left atrium/ventricle, aorta, most arteries</td><td>Vena cava, right atrium/ventricle, pulmonary artery, most veins</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Exceptions</td><td><strong>Pulmonary vein</strong> = vein with oxygenated blood</td><td><strong>Pulmonary artery</strong> = artery with deoxygenated blood</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b9-dif-atrium-vs-ventricle","bio-diff",9,"Atrium vs ventricle","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Atrium</th><th>Ventricle</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Position</td><td>Top chambers</td><td>Bottom chambers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Receives blood from</td><td>Veins (vena cava / pulmonary vein)</td><td>Atria</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pumps blood to</td><td>Ventricles (short distance)</td><td>Arteries → lungs / whole body</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wall</td><td><strong>Thin</strong> muscle</td><td><strong>Thick</strong> muscle (left thickest)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Valve at exit</td><td>Atrioventricular valve</td><td>Semilunar valve</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b9-dif-pulmonary-artery-vs-pulmon","bio-diff",9,"Pulmonary artery vs pulmonary vein","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Pulmonary artery</th><th>Pulmonary vein</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Direction</td><td>Right ventricle → lungs</td><td>Lungs → left atrium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blood</td><td><strong>Deoxygenated</strong></td><td><strong>Oxygenated</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pressure / wall</td><td>Higher pressure, thicker wall</td><td>Lower pressure, thinner wall</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Valves</td><td>Semilunar valve at its base</td><td>—</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b9-dif-atrioventricular-av-valve","bio-diff",9,"Atrioventricular (AV) valve vs semilunar (SL) valve","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>AV valve</th><th>SL valve</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Position</td><td>Between each atrium and ventricle</td><td>At the base of the aorta and pulmonary artery</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Names</td><td>Bicuspid (mitral, left), tricuspid (right)</td><td>Aortic and pulmonary semilunar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Opens when</td><td>Atria contract (atrial pressure &gt; ventricular)</td><td>Ventricles contract (ventricular pressure &gt; arterial)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Closes when</td><td>Ventricles contract</td><td>Ventricles relax</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Prevents backflow from</td><td>Ventricle → atrium</td><td>Artery → ventricle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Held by</td><td>Tendons (cords)</td><td>No tendons; half-moon pockets</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b9-dif-artery-vs-vein-vs-capillar","bio-diff",9,"Artery vs vein vs capillary","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Artery</th><th>Vein</th><th>Capillary</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Direction</td><td><strong>Away from</strong> heart</td><td><strong>Towards</strong> heart</td><td>Links arteries to veins, through tissues</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pressure</td><td><strong>High</strong>, pulsing</td><td><strong>Low</strong>, steady</td><td>Falling</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wall</td><td><strong>Thick</strong>, muscle + elastic fibres</td><td><strong>Thin</strong>, little muscle/elastic</td><td><strong>One cell thick</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lumen</td><td>Narrow (relative to wall)</td><td><strong>Wide</strong></td><td>Very narrow (≈ one red cell wide)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Valves</td><td>None (except semilunar at heart)</td><td><strong>Present</strong></td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blood</td><td>Oxygenated (except pulmonary artery)</td><td>Deoxygenated (except pulmonary vein)</td><td>Exchange happens here</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b9-dif-hepatic-artery-vs-hepatic","bio-diff",9,"Hepatic artery vs hepatic vein (vs hepatic portal vein)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Hepatic artery</th><th>Hepatic portal vein</th><th>Hepatic vein</th></tr>\n<tr><td>From → to</td><td>Aorta → liver</td><td>Small intestine → liver</td><td>Liver → vena cava</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blood</td><td>Oxygenated</td><td>Deoxygenated, <strong>rich in absorbed nutrients</strong> (glucose, amino acids)</td><td>Deoxygenated; nutrient levels regulated; carries urea</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Function</td><td>Supplies liver cells with O₂</td><td>Brings food for assimilation/storage</td><td>Returns blood to the heart</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b9-dif-red-blood-cell-vs-white-bl","bio-diff",9,"Red blood cell vs white blood cell vs platelet","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Red blood cell</th><th>White blood cell</th><th>Platelet</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Shape</td><td>Biconcave disc</td><td>Irregular, larger</td><td>Small cell fragment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nucleus</td><td><strong>None</strong></td><td><strong>Present</strong> (lobed in phagocytes; large round in lymphocytes)</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Contains</td><td>Haemoglobin</td><td>Enzymes / makes antibodies</td><td>Clotting chemicals</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Function</td><td>Transport <strong>oxygen</strong></td><td><strong>Phagocytosis</strong>; <strong>antibody production</strong></td><td><strong>Blood clotting</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Number per mm³</td><td>≈ 5 million</td><td>≈ 7000</td><td>≈ 250 000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Made in</td><td>Red bone marrow</td><td>Bone marrow (lymphocytes also in lymph nodes)</td><td>Red bone marrow</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-lymphocytes-produce-antibo","bio-facts",9,"Which cells produce antibodies?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lymphocytes produce antibodies</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-haemoglobin-is-a-protein-c","bio-facts",9,"What is haemoglobin?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Haemoglobin is a protein containing iron</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It combines reversibly with oxygen (oxyhaemoglobin) in the lungs and releases it in respiring tissues. Iron deficiency → anaemia.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-vein-lumen-is-wider-than-a","bio-facts",9,"Vein lumen compared with artery lumen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Vein lumen is wider than artery lumen</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Reduces resistance to the low-pressure flow.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-artery-wall-has-elastic-ti","bio-facts",9,"Artery wall – which tissue, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Artery wall has elastic tissue</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Stretches as each surge of blood passes, then recoils — this is the pulse; evens out pressure.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-pulse-rate-heart-rate","bio-facts",9,"Pulse rate = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pulse rate = heart rate</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-blockage-of-coronary-arter","bio-facts",9,"Blockage of a coronary artery causes…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Blockage of coronary artery → CHD</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-coronary-arteries-run-on-t","bio-facts",9,"Coronary arteries – where are they, and what do they supply?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Coronary arteries run on the outside of the heart and supply the heart muscle</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-heart-muscle-is-thick","bio-facts",9,"Heart muscle – thick or thin?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Heart muscle is thick</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Especially the left ventricle; cardiac muscle never tires.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-largest-artery-in-body-aor","bio-facts",9,"Largest artery in the body?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Largest artery in body — aorta</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-metabolically-active-tissu","bio-facts",9,"What do metabolically active tissues need a lot of?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Metabolically active tissues need a lot of oxygen</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>e.g. muscles, brain, liver — which is why a fast, high-pressure supply (double circulation) matters.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-double-circulatory-system","bio-facts",9,"Why have a double circulatory system: where is pressure lost?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Double circulatory system — loses a lot of pressure</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It is <strong>in the capillaries of the lungs (or a fish's gills)</strong> that blood <strong>loses</strong> a lot of pressure. In a <strong>single</strong> circulation (fish) the blood stays at low pressure as it goes on to the body. In a <strong>double</strong> circulation the low-pressure blood <strong>returns to the heart and is pumped again</strong>, so blood reaches the body at <strong>high</strong> pressure — that is its advantage.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-right-side-of-heart-deoxyg","bio-facts",9,"Which side of the heart carries deoxygenated blood? (memory aid)","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Right side of heart = deoxygenated; left side = oxygenated (\"LORD\")</p>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-arteries-carry-blood-away","bio-facts",9,"Arteries carry blood which way? (memory aid)","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Arteries carry blood Away from the heart (\"AA\")</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Veins carry blood towards it.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b9-fac-exercise-heart-rate-it-the","bio-facts",9,"Effect of exercise on heart rate, and afterwards?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Exercise ↑ heart rate; it then takes time to return to resting rate</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Fitter people recover faster and have lower resting rates.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-exp-body-defences","bio-expl",10,"Body defences","<ul>\n<li><strong>Skin</strong> — tough outer layer of dead cells; a physical barrier that pathogens cannot easily cross; oily secretions; blood clotting seals cuts.</li>\n<li><strong>Hairs in the nose</strong> — trap dust and pathogens in the air breathed in.</li>\n<li><strong>Mucus</strong> — sticky, made by goblet cells in the airways (and gut); traps pathogens; <strong>cilia</strong> sweep it up to the throat to be swallowed.</li>\n<li><strong>Stomach acid</strong> (hydrochloric acid) — kills pathogens in food and drink.</li>\n<li><strong>White blood cells</strong> — <strong>phagocytes</strong> engulf and digest pathogens (phagocytosis); <strong>lymphocytes</strong> produce antibodies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(Also: tears contain lysozyme; vaccination boosts defence.)</p>",0,""],["n-b10-exp-immune-response","bio-expl",10,"Immune response","<p>The reaction of the body to the presence of a <strong>foreign antigen</strong>: <strong>lymphocytes</strong> with antibodies of the complementary shape are stimulated, <strong>divide</strong> rapidly by mitosis and <strong>secrete specific antibodies</strong>; some become <strong>memory cells</strong>. The <strong>first (primary)</strong> response is <strong>slow and small</strong> (you may become ill); a <strong>second (secondary)</strong> response to the same antigen is <strong>much faster and larger</strong> because memory cells are already present — you don't get ill.</p>",0,""],["n-b10-exp-how-pathogens-are-transmit","bio-expl",10,"How pathogens are transmitted","<p><strong>Direct contact</strong> — including <strong>through blood and other body fluids</strong> (e.g. HIV by unprotected sex, sharing needles, transfusion of unscreened blood, mother to baby; athlete's foot by touch). <strong>Indirect</strong> — from <strong>contaminated surfaces</strong> (door handles), <strong>food</strong> (food poisoning, <em>Salmonella</em>), <strong>water</strong> (cholera), <strong>animals/vectors</strong> (houseflies, mosquitoes carry malaria), <strong>air</strong> (droplets from coughs and sneezes: flu, colds, measles, COVID-19, TB).</p>",0,""],["n-b10-exp-controlling-the-spread-of","bio-expl",10,"Controlling the spread of disease","<ul>\n<li><strong>Clean water supply</strong> — water treated (filtered, chlorinated) so no pathogens from sewage (cholera, polio, typhoid); boil water if unsure.</li>\n<li><strong>Hygienic food preparation</strong> — wash hands; keep raw meat separate from cooked/ready-to-eat food; cook thoroughly; keep food covered (flies) and refrigerated; don't reheat more than once.</li>\n<li><strong>Good personal hygiene</strong> — wash hands after toilet and before handling food; wash body; cover coughs and sneezes.</li>\n<li><strong>Waste disposal</strong> — rubbish collected and buried in lined <strong>landfill</strong> or burned; stops breeding of flies and rats (vectors); landfill decomposers make <strong>methane</strong> (flammable — vent or collect as fuel).</li>\n<li><strong>Sewage treatment</strong> — sewage (waste liquid from homes, industry, streets) is treated before release so pathogens don't reach drinking water (stages not required).</li>\n</ul>",0,""],["n-b10-exp-importance-of-breast-feedi","bio-expl",10,"Importance of breast-feeding","<p>Breast milk (especially the first milk, colostrum) contains the <strong>mother's antibodies</strong>, giving the baby <strong>passive immunity</strong> to diseases the mother has met, at a time when the baby's own immune system is still developing. Also gives ideal nutrients, is sterile, at the right temperature and free.</p>",1,""],["n-b10-dif-direct-vs-indirect-transmi","bio-diff",10,"Direct vs indirect transmission","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Direct transmission</th><th>Indirect transmission</th></tr>\n<tr><td>How</td><td>Pathogen passes straight from an infected person to another</td><td>Pathogen passes via something in between</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples of routes</td><td>Blood, semen and other body fluids; touching infected skin</td><td>Contaminated surfaces, food, water, animals (vectors), air (droplets)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example diseases</td><td>HIV/AIDS, hepatitis B, athlete's foot</td><td>Cholera (water), food poisoning (food), malaria (mosquito), flu/measles/COVID (air)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Control</td><td>Condoms, sterile needles, screening blood</td><td>Clean water, hygiene, cooking food, vector control, covering coughs</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b10-dif-antigen-vs-antibody","bio-diff",10,"Antigen vs antibody","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Antigen</th><th>Antibody</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What</td><td>Chemical (usually protein) on the surface of a pathogen (or cell)</td><td>Y-shaped protein</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Made by</td><td>The pathogen (foreign to the body)</td><td><strong>Lymphocytes</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shape</td><td>Specific to each pathogen</td><td><strong>Complementary</strong> to one specific antigen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Role</td><td>Stimulates the immune response</td><td>Binds to antigen → destroys pathogen or marks it for phagocytes</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b10-dif-active-immunity-vs-passive","bio-diff",10,"Active immunity vs passive immunity","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Active immunity</th><th>Passive immunity</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Antibodies made by</td><td><strong>The person's own lymphocytes</strong></td><td><strong>Another individual</strong> (or made outside the body)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gained by</td><td>Infection, or <strong>vaccination</strong></td><td>Across the <strong>placenta</strong>; in <strong>breast milk</strong>; injection of antibodies</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Memory cells</td><td><strong>Produced</strong></td><td><strong>Not produced</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed of protection</td><td>Slow to develop (days–weeks)</td><td><strong>Immediate</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Duration</td><td><strong>Long-term</strong></td><td><strong>Short-term</strong> (weeks)</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b10-fac-coronaviruses-are-spherica","bio-facts",10,"Shape of coronaviruses?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Coronaviruses are spherical viruses (with protein spikes)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The spikes help the virus attach to human cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-covid-19-is-caused-by-the","bio-facts",10,"Cause of COVID-19?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">COVID-19 is caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2; spread by airborne droplets; controlled by masks, distancing, hand-washing, vaccines.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-measles-causes-a-skin-rash","bio-facts",10,"Symptoms of measles?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Measles causes a skin rash and fever</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Caused by a virus spread in airborne droplets; complications include blindness and brain damage; controlled by vaccination (herd immunity).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-long-lasting-immunity-acti","bio-facts",10,"Long-lasting immunity = which type?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Long-lasting immunity = active immunity</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Because of memory cells.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-serious-transmissible-dise","bio-facts",10,"Cholera is caused by…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Serious transmissible disease — cholera (<em>Vibrio cholerae</em> bacteria)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Spread in water contaminated with faeces/raw sewage.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-untreated-sewage-raw-sewag","bio-facts",10,"Untreated sewage = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Untreated sewage = raw sewage</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Contains pathogens (cholera, polio, typhoid) and causes eutrophication (Ch 19).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-waste-liquid-sewage","bio-facts",10,"Waste liquid = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Waste liquid = sewage</p>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-methane-is-a-flammable-gas","bio-facts",10,"Methane – key property?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Methane is a flammable gas</p>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-decomposers-produce-methan","bio-facts",10,"Which organisms produce methane in landfill?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Decomposers produce methane gas</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>In landfill (anaerobic decay); pipes vent it or it is collected as fuel; methane is also a greenhouse gas.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-antibiotics-do-not-work-ag","bio-facts",10,"Do antibiotics work against viruses?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Antibiotics do not work against viruses.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>See Chapter 17 (drugs).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-houseflies-carry-pathogens","bio-facts",10,"How do houseflies spread pathogens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Houseflies carry pathogens on their feet and in their saliva.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They feed on faeces and then on our food.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b10-fac-smallpox-was-eradicated-wo","bio-facts",10,"How and when was smallpox eradicated?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Smallpox was eradicated worldwide (1980) by vaccination.</p>",0,""],["n-b11-exp-characteristics-of-gas-exc","bio-expl",11,"Characteristics of gas exchange surfaces","<p><strong>Large surface area</strong> (≈ 300–700 million alveoli; &gt; 100 m² in total) → more gas diffuses at once. <strong>Thin surface</strong> (alveolus wall and capillary wall each <strong>one cell thick</strong>) → short diffusion distance. <strong>Good blood supply</strong> (dense capillary network) → blood carries O₂ away and brings CO₂, <strong>maintaining a steep concentration gradient</strong>. <strong>Good ventilation with air</strong> → keeps O₂ high and CO₂ low in the alveoli, maintaining the gradient. (Also <strong>moist</strong> — gases dissolve before diffusing.)</p>",0,""],["n-b11-exp-why-the-trachea-has-rings","bio-expl",11,"Why the trachea has rings of cartilage","<p>The C-shaped <strong>rings of cartilage support the trachea and keep it open</strong>, stopping it <strong>collapsing</strong> when air pressure inside falls during breathing in, so air can always flow freely. (Bronchi also have cartilage.) The rings are incomplete at the back so food can pass down the oesophagus behind.</p>",1,""],["n-b11-exp-oxygen-debt","bio-expl",11,"Oxygen debt","<p>During vigorous exercise muscles respire anaerobically, so <strong>lactic acid builds up in muscles and blood</strong>, causing an <strong>oxygen debt</strong> — the <strong>extra oxygen needed after exercise to break down the lactic acid</strong>. <strong>How it is removed:</strong> (a) <strong>heart rate stays fast</strong> to <strong>transport lactic acid in the blood from the muscles to the liver</strong>; (b) <strong>deeper and faster breathing continues</strong> to <strong>supply oxygen for aerobic respiration of lactic acid</strong>; (c) <strong>aerobic respiration of lactic acid in the liver</strong> (to CO₂ and water, or converted back to glucose/glycogen).</p>",1,""],["n-b11-exp-uses-of-energy-in-living-o","bio-expl",11,"Uses of energy in living organisms","<p><strong>Muscle contraction</strong>, <strong>protein synthesis</strong>, <strong>cell division</strong>, <strong>active transport</strong>, <strong>growth</strong>, <strong>the passage of nerve impulses</strong>, <strong>maintenance of a constant body temperature</strong> (in mammals and birds).</p>",0,""],["n-b11-exp-effect-of-temperature-on-r","bio-expl",11,"Effect of temperature on respiration in yeast","<p>Respiration is controlled by <strong>enzymes</strong>: rate (CO₂ bubbles per minute) <strong>increases</strong> with temperature up to an <strong>optimum (≈ 35–40 °C)</strong>, then <strong>decreases</strong> as the enzymes are <strong>denatured</strong> (no bubbles at ≈ 60–80 °C).</p>",0,""],["n-b11-exp-parts-of-the-breathing-sys","bio-expl",11,"Parts of the breathing system","<p>Air: <strong>nose/mouth → larynx (voice box) → trachea → bronchi (left and right) → bronchioles → alveoli</strong> (with associated capillaries). Also: <strong>lungs</strong>, <strong>diaphragm</strong>, <strong>ribs</strong>, <strong>intercostal muscles</strong> — <strong>internal and external</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>, pleural membranes/fluid (lubricate breathing movements).</p>",0,""],["n-b11-exp-composition-of-inspired-an","bio-expl",11,"Composition of inspired and expired air","<p><strong>Oxygen 21% → 16%</strong>; <strong>carbon dioxide 0.04% → 4%</strong>; <strong>water vapour: variable → saturated</strong> (more); nitrogen 78% → 78% (unchanged). Expired air is also warmer.</p>",0,""],["n-b11-exp-explaining-the-differences","bio-expl",11,"Explaining the differences","<p><strong>Less O₂ in expired air</strong>: O₂ diffuses from alveoli into the blood and is used by cells in aerobic respiration. <strong>More CO₂</strong>: CO₂ produced in respiration diffuses from the blood into the alveoli and is breathed out. <strong>More water vapour</strong>: water evaporates from the moist alveolar surfaces (and water is a product of respiration). <strong>Nitrogen unchanged</strong>: not used by the body.</p>",1,""],["n-b11-exp-protecting-the-breathing-s","bio-expl",11,"Protecting the breathing system","<p><strong>Goblet cells</strong> secrete <strong>mucus</strong>, which traps pathogens and dust particles; <strong>ciliated cells</strong> have <strong>cilia</strong> that <strong>beat</strong> in a co-ordinated way to <strong>sweep the mucus up</strong> the trachea to the throat, where it is swallowed (stomach acid kills pathogens). Nose hairs filter large particles. Smoking paralyses cilia → mucus builds up → coughing and infections.</p>",1,""],["n-b11-dif-aerobic-vs-anaerobic-respi","bio-diff",11,"Aerobic vs anaerobic respiration","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Aerobic respiration</th><th>Anaerobic respiration</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Oxygen</td><td><strong>Used</strong></td><td><strong>Not used</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where in cell</td><td><strong>Mitochondria</strong></td><td><strong>Cytoplasm</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Glucose breakdown</td><td>Complete</td><td>Incomplete</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Products</td><td><strong>Carbon dioxide + water</strong></td><td>Muscle: <strong>lactic acid</strong>; yeast/plants: <strong>alcohol (ethanol) + carbon dioxide</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy released per glucose</td><td><strong>Large</strong> amount</td><td><strong>Much less</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>When (in humans)</td><td>All the time</td><td>Vigorous exercise, when O₂ supply cannot keep up</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Oxygen debt</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (muscle)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b11-dif-inspired-air-vs-expired-ai","bio-diff",11,"Inspired air vs expired air","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Gas</th><th>Inspired air (%)</th><th>Expired air (%)</th><th>Reason for change</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Oxygen</td><td><strong>21</strong></td><td><strong>16</strong></td><td>Absorbed into blood, used in respiration</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Carbon dioxide</td><td><strong>0.04</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Produced by respiration, diffuses into alveoli</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Water vapour</td><td>Variable</td><td><strong>Saturated</strong></td><td>Evaporates from moist alveolar walls</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nitrogen</td><td>78</td><td>78</td><td>Not used by body</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Air temperature</td><td>Body temperature (warmer)</td><td>Warmed in lungs</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b11-dif-inspiration-vs-expiration","bio-diff",11,"Inspiration vs expiration","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Inspiration (breathing in)</th><th>Expiration (breathing out)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>External intercostal muscles</td><td><strong>Contract</strong></td><td><strong>Relax</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Internal intercostal muscles</td><td>Relax</td><td>Relax (normal) / <strong>contract</strong> (forced)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ribs / ribcage</td><td>Move <strong>up and out</strong></td><td>Move <strong>down and in</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diaphragm</td><td><strong>Contracts</strong> → <strong>flattens</strong> (moves down)</td><td><strong>Relaxes</strong> → <strong>domes up</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Volume of thorax</td><td><strong>Increases</strong></td><td><strong>Decreases</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pressure in thorax</td><td><strong>Decreases</strong> (below atmospheric)</td><td><strong>Increases</strong> (above atmospheric)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Air movement</td><td><strong>In</strong></td><td><strong>Out</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b11-dif-breathing-vs-respiration","bio-diff",11,"Breathing vs respiration","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Breathing (ventilation)</th><th>Respiration</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What it is</td><td>Physical movement of air in/out of lungs</td><td>Chemical reactions releasing energy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Lungs / thorax</td><td>Every living cell</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Uses muscles?</td><td>Yes — diaphragm and intercostals</td><td>No</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-lactic-acid-is-broken-down","bio-facts",11,"Where is lactic acid broken down?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Lactic acid is broken down in the liver</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>By aerobic respiration (repaying the oxygen debt).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-glucose-lactic-acid-a-litt","bio-facts",11,"Anaerobic respiration in muscle: product?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Glucose → lactic acid (+ a little energy)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Anaerobic respiration in muscles.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-total-surface-area-of-all","bio-facts",11,"Total surface area of the alveoli?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Total surface area of all the alveoli in the lungs &gt; 100 m²</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>About the size of a tennis court half.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-alveolus-wall-is-one-cell","bio-facts",11,"Thickness of the alveolus wall?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alveolus wall is one cell thick</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So is the capillary wall — total diffusion distance &lt; 1 µm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-cilia-are-found-in-the-tra","bio-facts",11,"Where are cilia found in the gas exchange system?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cilia are found in the trachea and bronchi</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They sweep mucus upwards to the throat.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-limewater-turns-milky-clou","bio-facts",11,"Limewater test for carbon dioxide?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Limewater turns milky/cloudy with carbon dioxide</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Expired air turns limewater milky much faster than inspired air. Hydrogencarbonate indicator turns yellow with more CO₂.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-respiration-happens-in-all","bio-facts",11,"Where and when does respiration happen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Respiration happens in all living cells all the time — in plants too.</p>",0,""],["n-b11-fac-the-brain-responds-to-co-c","bio-facts",11,"What does the brain detect to control the breathing rate?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The brain responds to CO₂ concentration (not O₂) to control breathing.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>CO₂ makes the blood slightly more acidic (lowers pH).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-exp-functions-of-hormones","bio-expl",12,"Functions of hormones","<ol>\n<li><strong>Adrenaline</strong> — 'fight or flight' hormone; prepares the body for action (see 103).</li>\n<li><strong>Insulin</strong> — <strong>decreases blood glucose concentration</strong> (liver and muscles take up glucose and store it as glycogen).</li>\n<li><strong>Glucagon</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> — <strong>increases blood glucose concentration</strong> (liver converts glycogen to glucose).</li>\n<li><strong>Testosterone</strong> — development and regulation of <strong>male secondary sexual characteristics</strong> at puberty.</li>\n<li><strong>Oestrogen</strong> — development of <strong>female secondary sexual characteristics</strong>; helps control the menstrual cycle (repairs/thickens uterus lining).</li>\n</ol>",0,""],["n-b12-exp-effects-of-adrenaline","bio-expl",12,"Effects of adrenaline","<p>Secreted in <strong>'fight or flight'</strong> situations (fear, excitement, stress):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Heart</strong> — <strong>increased heart rate</strong> (and force) → more O₂ and glucose to muscles and brain.</li>\n<li><strong>Muscles</strong> — receive more blood, O₂ and glucose → more respiration/energy (blood diverted from gut and skin).</li>\n<li><strong>Lungs</strong> — <strong>increased breathing rate</strong> (and depth; airways widen) → more O₂ into blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Eye</strong> — <strong>increased pupil diameter</strong> (dilates) → more light enters, see danger better.</li>\n<li><strong>Liver</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> — converts glycogen to glucose → <strong>increases blood glucose concentration</strong>. (<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> adrenaline controls metabolic activity: ↑ blood glucose, ↑ heart rate.)</li>\n</ol>",0,""],["n-b12-exp-auxin","bio-expl",12,"Auxin","<p>A <strong>plant hormone (growth substance)</strong> that controls shoot growth: (a) <strong>auxin is made in the shoot tip</strong>; (b) <strong>auxin diffuses through the plant from the shoot tip</strong>; (c) <strong>auxin is unequally distributed in response to light and gravity</strong>; (d) <strong>auxin stimulates cell elongation</strong> (in shoots). More auxin on one side → those cells elongate more → shoot bends.</p>",1,""],["n-b12-exp-mammalian-nervous-system","bio-expl",12,"Mammalian nervous system","<p><strong>Central nervous system (CNS)</strong> = <strong>brain and spinal cord</strong> (coordinates). <strong>Peripheral nervous system (PNS)</strong> = <strong>nerves outside the brain and spinal cord</strong> (connect receptors and effectors to the CNS). A <strong>nerve</strong> = a bundle of neurone axons.</p>",0,""],["n-b12-exp-neurone-types","bio-expl",12,"Neurone types","<p><strong>Sensory</strong> — receptor → CNS; cell body in a swelling (ganglion) just outside the spinal cord, part-way along the fibre. <strong>Relay</strong> (connector) — inside the CNS, links sensory to motor; short, many branches. <strong>Motor</strong> — CNS → effector; <strong>cell body in the CNS at one end</strong>, long axon to the muscle/gland.</p>",0,""],["n-b12-exp-parts-of-the-eye-and-funct","bio-expl",12,"Parts of the eye and functions","<p><strong>Cornea</strong> — transparent front; <strong>refracts light</strong>. <strong>Iris</strong> — coloured muscular ring; <strong>controls how much light enters the pupil</strong>. <strong>Pupil</strong> — hole in the iris through which light enters. <strong>Lens</strong> — transparent, flexible; <strong>focuses light onto the retina</strong>. <strong>Retina</strong> — <strong>contains light receptors (rods and cones), some sensitive to light of different colours</strong>. <strong>Optic nerve</strong> — <strong>carries impulses to the brain</strong>. <strong>Blind spot</strong> — no receptors. Also: sclera (tough white protective coat), choroid (black; stops reflection; blood vessels), ciliary muscle and suspensory ligaments <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>, aqueous and vitreous humour (keep shape), conjunctiva.</p>",0,""],["n-b12-exp-rods-and-cones","bio-expl",12,"Rods and cones","<p><strong>Distribution:</strong> <strong>cones concentrated in the fovea</strong> (only cones there); <strong>rods spread throughout the rest of the retina</strong> (most numerous at the edges); <strong>none at the blind spot</strong>. <strong>Function:</strong> <strong>rods — greater sensitivity, for night vision</strong> (black &amp; white, dim light); <strong>cones — three kinds absorbing different colours of light (red, green, blue), for colour vision</strong> (need bright light, detailed vision).</p>",1,""],["n-b12-exp-how-auxin-causes-phototrop","bio-expl",12,"How auxin causes phototropism and gravitropism","<p><strong>Light from one side:</strong> auxin made in the shoot tip moves (diffuses) to the <strong>shaded side</strong> → more auxin there → <strong>cells on the shaded side elongate more</strong> → shoot <strong>bends towards the light</strong> (positive phototropism) → leaves get more light for photosynthesis. <strong>Shoot placed horizontally:</strong> auxin accumulates on the <strong>lower side</strong> (gravity) → lower cells elongate more → shoot <strong>bends upwards</strong> (negative gravitropism).</p>",1,""],["n-b12-dif-receptor-vs-effector","bio-diff",12,"Receptor vs effector","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Receptor</th><th>Effector</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Function</td><td><strong>Detects a stimulus</strong> and starts an impulse</td><td><strong>Carries out the response</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Rods/cones (eye), touch/pain/temperature receptors (skin), taste buds</td><td><strong>Muscles</strong> (contract) and <strong>glands</strong> (secrete)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Linked to</td><td>Sensory neurone</td><td>Motor neurone</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b12-dif-sensory-vs-relay-vs-motor","bio-diff",12,"Sensory vs relay vs motor neurone","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Sensory neurone</th><th>Relay neurone</th><th>Motor neurone</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Carries impulse</td><td><strong>Receptor → CNS</strong></td><td><strong>Within CNS</strong> (sensory → motor)</td><td><strong>CNS → effector</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Location</td><td>Mostly in PNS (nerves)</td><td><strong>Brain and spinal cord</strong></td><td>Cell body in CNS; axon in nerves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cell body position</td><td>In a ganglion <strong>part-way along</strong>, just outside spinal cord</td><td>Central</td><td><strong>At one end</strong> (in CNS), dendrites around it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Length</td><td>Long dendron + short axon</td><td><strong>Short</strong>, many branches</td><td><strong>Long axon</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Myelin sheath</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b12-dif-cns-vs-pns","bio-diff",12,"CNS vs PNS","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Central nervous system</th><th>Peripheral nervous system</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Made of</td><td><strong>Brain + spinal cord</strong></td><td><strong>Nerves</strong> outside brain and spinal cord</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Role</td><td>Coordinates; processes information</td><td>Carries impulses between CNS and receptors/effectors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Protected by</td><td>Skull and vertebral column</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Neurones</td><td>Relay neurones; cell bodies of motor neurones</td><td>Sensory and motor neurone fibres</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b12-dif-rods-vs-cones","bio-diff",12,"Rods vs cones","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Rods</th><th>Cones</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sensitivity</td><td><strong>Very sensitive — work in dim light</strong></td><td>Need <strong>bright light</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vision</td><td><strong>Night vision</strong>, black and white (shades of grey)</td><td><strong>Colour vision</strong>, fine detail</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Types</td><td>One</td><td><strong>Three</strong> (absorb red, green or blue light)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Throughout retina, <strong>not in fovea</strong></td><td><strong>Concentrated in fovea</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>At blind spot</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b12-dif-circular-muscle-vs-radial","bio-diff",12,"Circular muscle vs radial muscle (of the iris)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Circular muscles</th><th>Radial muscles</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Arrangement</td><td>Rings around the pupil</td><td>Like spokes, from pupil outwards</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bright light</td><td><strong>Contract</strong></td><td><strong>Relax</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dim light</td><td><strong>Relax</strong></td><td><strong>Contract</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect when contracting</td><td>Pupil <strong>constricts</strong> (smaller)</td><td>Pupil <strong>dilates</strong> (larger)</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b12-dif-insulin-vs-glucagon-see-al","bio-diff",12,"Insulin vs glucagon (see also 40, Ch 13)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Insulin</th><th>Glucagon <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Made by</td><td>Pancreas</td><td>Pancreas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Released when blood glucose is</td><td><strong>High</strong> (e.g. after a meal)</td><td><strong>Low</strong> (e.g. fasting, exercise)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect on liver</td><td>Glucose → <strong>glycogen</strong> (stored)</td><td><strong>Glycogen → glucose</strong> (released)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect on blood glucose</td><td><strong>Decreases</strong></td><td><strong>Increases</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b12-dif-nervous-system-vs-endocrin","bio-diff",12,"Nervous system vs endocrine (hormonal) system","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Nervous system</th><th>Endocrine system</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Signal</td><td><strong>Electrical impulses</strong> (+ chemical at synapses)</td><td><strong>Chemicals (hormones)</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Carried by</td><td>Neurones (nerves)</td><td><strong>Blood</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Speed of action</strong></td><td><strong>Very fast</strong></td><td><strong>Slower</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Duration of effect</strong></td><td><strong>Short-lived</strong></td><td><strong>Longer-lasting</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Area affected</td><td>Very precise/localised (one muscle)</td><td>Can be widespread (target organs anywhere)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>Reflex withdrawal of hand</td><td>Insulin controlling blood glucose; puberty</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b12-dif-phototropism-vs-gravitropi","bio-diff",12,"Phototropism vs gravitropism","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Phototropism</th><th>Gravitropism</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Stimulus</td><td><strong>Light</strong> (one-sided)</td><td><strong>Gravity</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shoots</td><td><strong>Positive</strong> (grow towards light)</td><td><strong>Negative</strong> (grow up, away from gravity)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Roots</td><td>Negative (or none)</td><td><strong>Positive</strong> (grow down)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Auxin moves to</td><td><strong>Shaded side</strong></td><td><strong>Lower side</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Advantage</td><td>Leaves get more light for photosynthesis</td><td>Roots reach water and anchor plant; shoots reach light</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b12-fac-plants-respond-to-stimuli","bio-facts",12,"How do plants respond to stimuli?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Plants respond to stimuli by growth</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Responses (tropisms) are slow compared with animals' nervous responses.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-nervous-control-acts-more","bio-facts",12,"Nervous and hormonal control: speed and duration?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Nervous control acts more quickly but for a shorter period</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Hormonal control is slower but longer-lasting.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-auxin-concentrates-on-the","bio-facts",12,"Where does auxin collect in a shoot lit from one side?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Auxin concentrates on the shady side of a shoot</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Those cells elongate more → bends towards light.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-suspensory-ligaments-conne","bio-facts",12,"What do the suspensory ligaments connect?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Suspensory ligaments connect the lens to the ciliary muscles</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-suspensory-ligaments-tight","bio-facts",12,"Suspensory ligaments tight or slack: lens shape for distant and near vision?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Suspensory ligaments tight → lens thin (distant vision); slack → lens thick (near vision)</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-rods-dim-light-black-and-w","bio-facts",12,"Rods compared with cones?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Rods: dim light, black and white. Cones: colour, bright light</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-fovea-receptor-cells-cones","bio-facts",12,"Fovea – what is special about it?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fovea: receptor cells (cones) packed most closely</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Sharpest image.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-tears-contain-the-enzyme-l","bio-facts",12,"Which enzyme is in tears?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Tears contain the enzyme lysozyme</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Kills bacteria (a body defence).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-conjunctiva-protects-the-p","bio-facts",12,"Conjunctiva – function?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Conjunctiva protects the parts behind it</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Thin transparent membrane over the front of the eye; inflamed in conjunctivitis.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-myelin-insulates-the-nerve","bio-facts",12,"Myelin – what does it do?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Myelin insulates the nerve fibre, so impulses are carried faster</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Up to ~100 m/s vs ~5 m/s without myelin.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-the-brain-and-spinal-cord","bio-facts",12,"Which neurones make up most of the brain and spinal cord?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The brain and spinal cord are made mostly of relay neurones.</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-most-refraction-occurs-at","bio-facts",12,"Where does most refraction in the eye happen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Most refraction occurs at the cornea; the lens fine-tunes the focus.</p>",0,""],["n-b12-fac-glucagon-hormone-glycogen","bio-facts",12,"Glucagon compared with glycogen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Glucagon (hormone) ≠ glycogen (storage carbohydrate).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Spell both correctly.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-exp-how-urea-is-made-in-the-li","bio-expl",13,"How urea is made in the liver","<p><strong>Urea is formed in the liver from excess amino acids</strong>: excess amino acids (brought to the liver in the hepatic portal vein) → <strong>deamination</strong> removes the amino (–NH₂) group → it is converted to <strong>urea</strong> → urea passes into the blood (hepatic vein) → carried in the plasma to the <strong>kidneys</strong> → filtered out → urine. <strong>Why excretion matters</strong><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: <strong>urea is toxic</strong>; if it builds up in the blood it damages cells (kidney failure → dialysis/transplant needed).</p>",1,""],["n-b13-exp-how-urine-is-made-in-a-nep","bio-expl",13,"How urine is made in a nephron","<p>(1) <strong>Filtration</strong> — in the glomerulus, water, glucose, urea and ions are filtered from the blood into the capsule. (2) <strong>Reabsorption</strong> — as the filtrate flows along the tubule, <strong>all of the glucose, some of the ions and most of the water</strong> are reabsorbed <strong>back into the blood</strong> (capillaries around the tubule). (3) What is left — <strong>urea, excess water and excess ions</strong> — flows down the collecting duct as <strong>urine</strong> → pelvis → ureter → bladder. (Details of these processes are not required.)</p>",1,""],["n-b13-exp-how-blood-glucose-concentr","bio-expl",13,"How blood glucose concentration is regulated","<p>Monitored by the <strong>pancreas</strong>. <strong>Too high</strong> (after a meal): pancreas secretes <strong>insulin</strong> → liver (and muscle) cells take up glucose and convert it to <strong>glycogen</strong> (stored) → blood glucose <strong>falls</strong> back to the set point. <strong>Too low</strong> (fasting, exercise): pancreas secretes <strong>glucagon</strong> → liver converts <strong>glycogen → glucose</strong> and releases it → blood glucose <strong>rises</strong>. This is negative feedback. (<strong>Insulin decreases blood glucose</strong>; glucagon increases it.)</p>",1,""],["n-b13-exp-how-the-hypothalamus-acts","bio-expl",13,"How the hypothalamus acts like a thermostat","<p>The <strong>hypothalamus</strong> (part of the brain) contains <strong>temperature receptors that monitor the temperature of the blood</strong> flowing through it (and receives impulses from temperature receptors in the skin). It compares this with the <strong>set point (37 °C)</strong>; if too high or too low, it sends <strong>impulses along motor neurones</strong> to the effectors (sweat glands, arterioles in the skin, skeletal muscles, hair erector muscles) to bring the temperature back — just as a thermostat switches a heater on/off.</p>",0,""],["n-b13-exp-how-skin-helps-with-temper","bio-expl",13,"How skin helps with temperature regulation","<p>Skin structures: <strong>hairs and hair erector muscles</strong>, <strong>sweat glands</strong>, <strong>receptors</strong> (temperature), <strong>sensory neurones</strong>, <strong>blood vessels (arterioles + capillaries)</strong>, <strong>fatty tissue</strong> (insulation). The skin contains the effectors for: <strong>sweating</strong> (evaporation of sweat removes heat), <strong>vasodilation/vasoconstriction</strong> (changing blood flow near the surface), <strong>hair erection</strong> (trapping insulating air — small effect in humans), and the fat layer <strong>insulates</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-b13-exp-when-temperature-falls-res","bio-expl",13,"When temperature falls — responses","<ol>\n<li><strong>Shivering</strong> — muscles contract rapidly and involuntarily → respiration releases heat.</li>\n<li><strong>Metabolic rate may increase</strong> (e.g. liver) → more heat from respiration.</li>\n<li><strong>Hairs stand up</strong> (hair erector muscles contract) → trap a layer of insulating air ('goose bumps').</li>\n<li><strong>Vasoconstriction</strong> of skin arterioles → less blood to skin surface → less heat lost.</li>\n<li><strong>Sweat glands reduce secretion</strong> → less heat lost by evaporation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(Also behaviour: put on clothes, curl up; fat layer insulates.)</p>",0,""],["n-b13-exp-when-temperature-rises-res","bio-expl",13,"When temperature rises — responses","<ol>\n<li><strong>Hairs lie flat</strong> (erector muscles relax) → less air trapped, less insulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Vasodilation</strong> of skin arterioles → more blood near the surface → more heat lost.</li>\n<li><strong>Sweat production increases</strong> → water in sweat <strong>evaporates</strong>, taking <strong>heat</strong> (latent heat) from the skin → cooling.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(Also: no shivering; reduced metabolic rate; behaviour — remove clothes, seek shade.)</p>",0,""],["n-b13-exp-organs-of-the-urinary-syst","bio-expl",13,"Organs of the urinary system","<p><strong>Kidneys</strong> (two, at the back of the abdomen) → <strong>ureters</strong> (carry urine to bladder) → <strong>bladder</strong> (stores urine) → <strong>urethra</strong> (carries urine out; sphincter muscle controls release). Blood: renal artery in, renal vein out.</p>",0,""],["n-b13-exp-structure-of-the-kidney","bio-expl",13,"Structure of the kidney","<p><strong>Cortex</strong> (outer layer; contains glomeruli and capsules), <strong>medulla</strong> (inner layer; loops of tubules and collecting ducts), <strong>pelvis</strong> (collecting area leading to the ureter).</p>",1,""],["n-b13-exp-role-of-the-liver-in-assim","bio-expl",13,"Role of the liver in assimilation of amino acids","<p>Liver cells take up amino acids from the hepatic portal vein and <strong>convert them to proteins</strong> (e.g. <strong>plasma proteins</strong> such as fibrinogen and albumin) — assimilation. Excess amino acids are deaminated.</p>",1,""],["n-b13-exp-type-1-diabetes-and-its-tr","bio-expl",13,"Type 1 diabetes and its treatment","<p><strong>The pancreas does not secrete (enough) insulin</strong> (the insulin-making cells are destroyed by the immune system), so blood glucose is not controlled — it can rise very high (hyperglycaemia; glucose in urine, thirst, tiredness, weight loss) or fall too low (hypoglycaemia → coma). <strong>Treatment:</strong> <strong>regular insulin injections</strong> (or pump) matched to meals; <strong>monitor blood glucose</strong> frequently (glucose meter); <strong>controlled diet</strong> — regular meals, carbohydrates eaten in measured amounts, few sugary foods; exercise planned with extra carbohydrate; carry glucose for hypos.</p>",1,""],["n-b13-dif-insulin-vs-glucagon","bio-diff",13,"Insulin vs glucagon","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Insulin</th><th>Glucagon</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Secreted by</td><td>Pancreas</td><td>Pancreas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stimulus</td><td>Blood glucose <strong>above</strong> set point</td><td>Blood glucose <strong>below</strong> set point</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Target organ</td><td>Liver (and muscle)</td><td>Liver</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Action</td><td>Liver converts <strong>glucose → glycogen</strong>; cells take up more glucose</td><td>Liver converts <strong>glycogen → glucose</strong> and releases it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect on blood glucose</td><td><strong>Decreases</strong></td><td><strong>Increases</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b13-dif-type-1-vs-type-2-diabetes","bio-diff",13,"Type 1 vs Type 2 diabetes","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Type 1 diabetes</th><th>Type 2 diabetes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cause</td><td>Pancreas <strong>cannot make insulin</strong> (insulin-secreting cells destroyed)</td><td>Body cells <strong>stop responding to insulin</strong> properly (and/or less insulin made)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Usual age of onset</td><td>Childhood / young adults</td><td>Usually adults over 40 (now younger)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Risk factors</td><td>Genetic / autoimmune</td><td><strong>Obesity</strong>, poor diet, lack of exercise, family history</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Treatment</td><td><strong>Insulin injections</strong> + monitoring + diet</td><td><strong>Diet and exercise</strong>, weight loss, tablets; insulin later if needed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Syllabus</td><td>Treatment required</td><td>Background only</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b13-dif-vasoconstriction-vs-vasodi","bio-diff",13,"Vasoconstriction vs vasodilation","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Vasoconstriction</th><th>Vasodilation</th></tr>\n<tr><td>When</td><td><strong>Too cold</strong></td><td><strong>Too hot</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arteriole muscle</td><td><strong>Contracts</strong></td><td><strong>Relaxes</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arteriole lumen</td><td><strong>Narrower</strong></td><td><strong>Wider</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Blood to skin capillaries</td><td><strong>Less</strong></td><td><strong>More</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heat loss from skin</td><td><strong>Decreased</strong></td><td><strong>Increased</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Skin appearance</td><td>Pale</td><td>Red / flushed</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b13-fac-the-two-kidneys-filter-abo","bio-facts",13,"Water filtered by the kidneys each day compared with urine produced?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The two kidneys filter about 170 dm³ of water per day, but only about 1.5 dm³ of urine is produced in the same time</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So about 99% of the filtered water is reabsorbed.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-amino-acids-are-taken-to-t","bio-facts",13,"How do amino acids reach the liver?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Amino acids are taken to the liver in the hepatic portal vein (HPV)</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-amino-acids-are-absorbed-i","bio-facts",13,"Where are amino acids absorbed?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Amino acids are absorbed in the villi of the ileum</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-urea-is-made-from-excess-p","bio-facts",13,"Urea is made from…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Urea is made from excess protein (amino acids)</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-urethra-tube-from-bladder","bio-facts",13,"Urethra – from where to where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Urethra — tube from bladder to outside</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-ureter-tube-from-kidney-to","bio-facts",13,"Ureter – from where to where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Ureter — tube from kidney to bladder</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Spelling matters: ureter (×2, from kidneys) vs urethra (×1, to outside).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-co-lowers-the-ph-of-cells","bio-facts",13,"Effect of CO₂ on the pH of cells and blood?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">CO₂ lowers the pH of cells and blood</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It dissolves to form a weak acid — so it is toxic and must be excreted by the lungs.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-faeces-are-not-excretion-e","bio-facts",13,"Are faeces excretion?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Faeces are not excretion (egestion), except for bile pigments in them.</p>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-glucose-in-urine-is-a-sign","bio-facts",13,"Glucose in urine is a sign of…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Glucose in urine is a sign of diabetes.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Normally all glucose is reabsorbed.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b13-fac-body-temperature-is-kept-a","bio-facts",13,"Normal core body temperature?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Body temperature is kept at about 37 °C.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Enzymes' optimum; too high → denaturation; too low → slow reactions.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b14-exp-characteristics-of-a-wind","bio-expl",14,"Characteristics of a wind-pollinated flower","<p><strong>Petals</strong> small, dull green/brown or absent, no scent, no nectar (no need to attract insects). <strong>Anthers</strong> large, <strong>hang outside</strong> the flower on <strong>long thin filaments</strong>, loosely attached so they shake in the wind. <strong>Stigmas</strong> <strong>large and feathery</strong>, hanging outside the flower → large surface area to <strong>catch pollen from the air</strong>. <strong>Pollen</strong> — <strong>huge amounts</strong>; <strong>small, light, smooth</strong> grains (easily blown, most is wasted). e.g. grasses, maize, wheat.</p>",0,""],["n-b14-exp-characteristics-of-an-inse","bio-expl",14,"Characteristics of an insect-pollinated flower (PASF)","<p><strong>P</strong>etals — <strong>large, brightly coloured</strong>, scented, often with <strong>guide-lines</strong> to the nectar; <strong>nectaries</strong> secrete nectar (sugary reward). <strong>A</strong>nthers — <strong>inside the flower</strong>, firmly attached, positioned so insects brush against them. <strong>S</strong>tigma — <strong>inside the flower</strong>, small, <strong>sticky</strong> so pollen sticks to it. <strong>F</strong>ilaments — short/stiff (anthers inside). <strong>Pollen</strong> — <strong>less</strong>, <strong>larger</strong>, <strong>sticky or spiky/rough</strong> grains that stick to insects' hairy bodies. e.g. hibiscus, buttercup, lupin.</p>",0,""],["n-b14-exp-fertilisation-and-the-poll","bio-expl",14,"Fertilisation and the pollen tube","<p>After pollination (pollen of the same species on a ripe stigma): the pollen grain <strong>germinates</strong> — sugary fluid on the stigma stimulates it to grow a <strong>pollen tube</strong> <strong>down through the style</strong> (secreting enzymes to digest a path) → into the <strong>ovary</strong> → through the <strong>micropyle</strong> into an <strong>ovule</strong>. The <strong>male nucleus travels down the tube</strong> and <strong>fuses with the female (egg) nucleus</strong> in the ovule → <strong>zygote</strong>. One pollen grain fertilises one ovule. (Details of endosperm formation and development are not required.)</p>",1,""],["n-b14-exp-parts-of-an-insect-pollina","bio-expl",14,"Parts of an insect-pollinated flower and functions","<p><strong>Sepals</strong> — protect the flower in bud. <strong>Petals</strong> — large, coloured; attract insects. <strong>Stamen</strong> (male) = <strong>filament</strong> (stalk; holds anther up) + <strong>anther</strong> (makes pollen, which contains male gametes). <strong>Carpel</strong> (female) = <strong>stigma</strong> (sticky surface that receives pollen) + <strong>style</strong> (connects stigma to ovary; pollen tube grows through it) + <strong>ovary</strong> (contains ovules; becomes the fruit). <strong>Ovules</strong> — contain the female gamete; become <strong>seeds</strong>. Also nectary (nectar) and receptacle.</p>",0,""],["n-b14-exp-germination-conditions","bio-expl",14,"Germination conditions","<p>Seeds need: <strong>water</strong> (to activate enzymes that break down food stores; to swell and burst the testa; for transport), <strong>oxygen</strong> (for aerobic <strong>respiration</strong> to release energy for growth), and a <strong>suitable temperature</strong> (for <strong>enzyme</strong> activity — too cold = slow; too hot = denatured). Light is <strong>not</strong> needed by most seeds.</p>",0,""],["n-b14-exp-effects-of-self-and-cross","bio-expl",14,"Effects of self- and cross-pollination on a population","<p><strong>Self-pollination:</strong> less <strong>genetic variation</strong> (offspring very similar to parent) → population <strong>less able to respond/adapt to changes in the environment</strong> (e.g. a new disease could wipe it out); but <strong>does not rely on pollinators</strong> (useful if insects are rare) and fewer pollen grains are wasted. <strong>Cross-pollination:</strong> more <strong>genetic variation</strong> → better <strong>capacity to respond to environmental change</strong> (natural selection can act); but <strong>relies on pollinators</strong> (or wind) — if pollinators decline (e.g. bees killed by pesticides), seed production falls; much pollen wasted.</p>",1,""],["n-b14-dif-asexual-vs-sexual-reproduc","bio-diff",14,"Asexual vs sexual reproduction","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Asexual</th><th>Sexual</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Parents</td><td><strong>One</strong></td><td>Usually <strong>two</strong> (or one hermaphrodite)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gametes / fertilisation</td><td><strong>None</strong></td><td><strong>Gametes fuse</strong> (fertilisation)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cell division</td><td>Mitosis only</td><td>Meiosis (gametes) + mitosis (growth)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Offspring</td><td><strong>Genetically identical</strong> (clones)</td><td><strong>Genetically different</strong> (variation)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td>Fast; many offspring quickly</td><td>Slower</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Bacteria, potato tubers, <em>Hydra</em>, strawberry runners</td><td>Flowering plants (seeds), humans</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b14-dif-haploid-vs-diploid","bio-diff",14,"Haploid vs diploid","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Haploid</th><th>Diploid</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Chromosome sets</td><td><strong>One</strong> set (<em>n</em>)</td><td><strong>Two</strong> sets (2<em>n</em>) — pairs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Human number</td><td><strong>23</strong></td><td><strong>46</strong> (23 pairs)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Found in</td><td><strong>Gametes</strong> (sperm, egg, pollen nucleus, ovule nucleus)</td><td><strong>Zygote</strong> and all body cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Made by</td><td><strong>Meiosis</strong></td><td>Fertilisation; mitosis keeps it diploid</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b14-dif-male-gamete-vs-female-game","bio-diff",14,"Male gamete vs female gamete (flowering plant)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Male gamete</th><th>Female gamete</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Name</td><td>Pollen nucleus</td><td>Egg cell nucleus (in ovule)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Made in</td><td>Anther (pollen grains)</td><td>Ovule inside the ovary</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Movement</td><td>Carried by insects/wind; travels down pollen tube</td><td>Does not move</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Number</td><td>Very many</td><td>Few (one per ovule)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b14-dif-mitosis-vs-meiosis-see-als","bio-diff",14,"Mitosis vs meiosis (see also 56, Ch 16)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Mitosis</th><th>Meiosis</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Daughter cells</td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chromosome number</td><td><strong>Same</strong> as parent (diploid → diploid)</td><td><strong>Halved</strong> (diploid → haploid) — reduction division</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Genetically</td><td><strong>Identical</strong></td><td><strong>Different</strong> (variation)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Used for</td><td>Growth, repair, replacing cells, asexual reproduction</td><td><strong>Making gametes</strong> (anthers, ovules; testes, ovaries)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b14-dif-insect-pollinated-vs-wind","bio-diff",14,"Insect-pollinated vs wind-pollinated flowers","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Insect-pollinated</th><th>Wind-pollinated</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Petals</td><td><strong>Large, brightly coloured</strong>, scented, guide-lines</td><td><strong>Small/dull or absent</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nectar</td><td><strong>Present</strong> (nectaries)</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anthers</td><td><strong>Inside</strong> flower, fixed</td><td><strong>Hang outside</strong> on long filaments, loosely attached</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stigma</td><td><strong>Inside</strong>, small, <strong>sticky</strong></td><td><strong>Outside</strong>, <strong>large, feathery</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pollen amount</td><td>Less</td><td><strong>Very large</strong> amounts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pollen grains</td><td><strong>Larger, sticky/spiky</strong> — cling to insects</td><td><strong>Small, light, smooth</strong> — carried by wind</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Hibiscus, lupin, buttercup</td><td>Grasses, maize, wheat, rice</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b14-dif-self-pollination-vs-cross","bio-diff",14,"Self-pollination vs cross-pollination","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Self-pollination</th><th>Cross-pollination</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Pollen from</td><td>Same flower, or another flower on the <strong>same plant</strong></td><td>A flower on a <strong>different plant</strong> of the <strong>same species</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Genetic variation in offspring</td><td><strong>Low</strong></td><td><strong>High</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reliance on pollinators</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pollen wasted</td><td>Little</td><td>A lot</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b14-dif-advantages-of-sexual-vs-as","bio-diff",14,"Advantages of sexual vs asexual reproduction","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Asexual — advantages</th><th>Sexual — advantages</th></tr>\n<tr><td>In the wild</td><td>Fast; only one parent needed (no need to find a mate/pollinator); all offspring well adapted if environment stays the same; offspring often grow near parent in a good spot; food stores in tubers/bulbs give quick growth</td><td><strong>Variation</strong> → population can <strong>adapt</strong> to a changing environment; some may resist new diseases; seeds <strong>dispersed</strong> away from parent → less competition, colonise new areas; seeds survive harsh conditions (dormancy)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>In crop production</td><td><strong>Uniform</strong> crop (same flavour, size, ripening time — easier to harvest/sell); desirable features <strong>kept exactly</strong>; quick and can be cheap (cuttings, tissue culture)</td><td>New varieties with combined good features can be bred (selective breeding); seeds easy to store and transport</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b14-dif-disadvantages-of-sexual-vs","bio-diff",14,"Disadvantages of sexual vs asexual reproduction","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Asexual — disadvantages</th><th>Sexual — disadvantages</th></tr>\n<tr><td>In the wild</td><td><strong>No variation</strong> → whole population may be wiped out by a disease or environmental change; offspring crowd near parent → <strong>competition</strong> for light, water, nutrients</td><td>Slower; needs <strong>two parents</strong>/pollinators (may fail); lots of pollen/gametes wasted; offspring may lack the parent's good features</td></tr>\n<tr><td>In crop production</td><td>All plants equally <strong>susceptible to disease</strong>; can't adapt; no new varieties</td><td>Offspring vary (not uniform); slower to grow from seed; good features may be lost</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b14-dif-advantages-of-self-vs-cros","bio-diff",14,"Advantages of self- vs cross-pollination","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Self-pollination</th><th>Cross-pollination</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Advantages</td><td>Doesn't rely on pollinators or another plant; more certain; less pollen needed; keeps good features of a well-adapted plant</td><td>More <strong>genetic variation</strong> → better able to adapt to change; healthier offspring</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b14-dif-disadvantages-of-self-vs-c","bio-diff",14,"Disadvantages of self- vs cross-pollination","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Self-pollination</th><th>Cross-pollination</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Disadvantages</td><td>Little variation → less able to adapt to environmental change; harmful recessive features may show up</td><td>Depends on pollinators/wind and another plant nearby; lots of pollen wasted; less certain</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-eucryphia-hermaphrodite-re","bio-facts",14,"Eucryphia – type of flower and reproduction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Eucryphia — hermaphrodite — reproduces sexually</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Its flowers have both stamens and carpels.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-bacteria-hydra-potatoes-as","bio-facts",14,"Bacteria, Hydra, potatoes – which type of reproduction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bacteria, <em>Hydra</em>, potatoes — asexual reproduction</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Bacteria by binary fission; <em>Hydra</em> by budding; potato by stem tubers.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-after-fertilisation-ovule","bio-facts",14,"After fertilisation: what do the ovule and ovary become, and what withers?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">After fertilisation: ovule → seed; ovary → fruit; petals, sepals and stamens wither.</p>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-a-seed-contains-an-embryo","bio-facts",14,"What does a seed contain?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A seed contains an embryo plant (radicle, plumule) and food stores (cotyledons/endosperm), surrounded by a testa (seed coat).</p>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-pollination-seed-dispersal","bio-facts",14,"Pollination compared with seed dispersal?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pollination ≠ seed dispersal.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Insects carrying pollen = pollination; animals carrying seeds/fruits = dispersal.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b14-fac-many-crop-plants-are-grown","bio-facts",14,"Why are many crop plants grown from cuttings or tissue culture?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Many crop plants are grown from cuttings or tissue culture so they are genetically identical.</p>",0,""],["n-b15-exp-amniotic-sac-and-amniotic","bio-expl",15,"Amniotic sac and amniotic fluid","<p><strong>Amniotic sac</strong> — a tough <strong>membrane</strong> surrounding the fetus, containing <strong>amniotic fluid</strong>; protects against infection. <strong>Amniotic fluid</strong> — liquid secreted by the sac; <strong>supports and protects the fetus from physical damage (shock-absorber)</strong>, lets it move, keeps temperature steady. The sac breaks just before birth.</p>",0,""],["n-b15-exp-the-menstrual-cycle","bio-expl",15,"The menstrual cycle","<p>A cycle of about <strong>28 days</strong> of <strong>changes in the ovaries</strong> (follicle develops → ovulation → corpus luteum) and <strong>in the lining of the uterus</strong> (breaks down → rebuilds → maintained → breaks down), controlled by hormones: <strong>FSH, LH</strong> (pituitary gland), <strong>oestrogen</strong>, <strong>progesterone</strong> (ovary).</p>",0,""],["n-b15-exp-secondary-sexual-character","bio-expl",15,"Secondary sexual characteristics","<p><strong>Testosterone</strong> (from testes) — boys: voice deepens, facial/body/pubic hair, muscles develop, testes and penis enlarge, sperm production starts, broader shoulders. <strong>Oestrogen</strong> (from ovaries) — girls: breasts develop, hips widen, pubic and underarm hair, menstrual cycle starts (ovulation), uterus and vagina enlarge.</p>",0,""],["n-b15-exp-changes-in-hormone-levels","bio-expl",15,"Changes in hormone levels, ovary and uterus during the menstrual cycle","<p><strong>Week 1 (days 1–5):</strong> menstruation; low oestrogen and progesterone; <strong>FSH (pituitary) rises → a follicle develops in the ovary</strong>. <strong>Week 2:</strong> the follicle secretes <strong>oestrogen</strong> (rising) → <strong>uterus lining repairs and thickens</strong>; high oestrogen → <strong>LH surge</strong> (pituitary) → <strong>ovulation ≈ day 14</strong>. <strong>Week 3:</strong> the follicle becomes the <strong>corpus luteum</strong> → secretes <strong>progesterone</strong> (high) → <strong>maintains thick, blood-rich lining</strong>; progesterone (and oestrogen) <strong>inhibit FSH and LH</strong> so no new follicle develops. <strong>Week 4:</strong> if no fertilisation → corpus luteum degenerates → <strong>progesterone falls</strong> → lining breaks down → menstruation; FSH rises again.<br />\n<strong>Sites of production</strong>: <strong>oestrogen</strong> — ovary (developing follicle) in the cycle; <strong>placenta</strong> in pregnancy. <strong>Progesterone</strong> — <strong>corpus luteum</strong> in the cycle; <strong>placenta</strong> in pregnancy (from about 3 months). <strong>Pregnancy</strong>: progesterone (and oestrogen) <strong>stay high</strong> → lining maintained, no menstruation, FSH/LH inhibited (no ovulation).</p>",1,""],["n-b15-exp-preventing-hiv-transmissio","bio-expl",15,"Preventing HIV transmission","<p><strong>Methods of transmission:</strong> <strong>(1) sexual contact</strong> — unprotected sexual intercourse with an infected person; <strong>(2) blood contact</strong> — sharing needles (drug users), transfusion of unscreened blood, unsterilised medical instruments, cuts; <strong>(3) mother to baby</strong> — across the placenta, at birth, or in <strong>breast milk</strong>. <strong>Control of spread of STIs:</strong> use <strong>condoms</strong> (barrier); limit number of sexual partners / abstinence; <strong>testing</strong> and contact tracing; treatment (antibiotics for bacterial STIs; <strong>antiretrovirals</strong> for HIV); <strong>sterile needles</strong>, never share needles; <strong>screen blood</strong> for transfusion; HIV-positive mothers take antiretrovirals and may use bottle-feeding; education.</p>",0,""],["n-b15-exp-hiv-and-aids","bio-expl",15,"HIV and AIDS","<p><strong>HIV infection may lead to AIDS</strong> (acquired immune deficiency syndrome). HIV infects and destroys a type of <strong>lymphocyte</strong>, so over years the immune system fails and the person dies of other infections (e.g. TB, pneumonia) — this is AIDS.</p>",0,""],["n-b15-exp-male-reproductive-system-p","bio-expl",15,"Male reproductive system — parts and functions","<p><strong>Testes</strong> — make <strong>sperm</strong> (meiosis in tubules) and the hormone <strong>testosterone</strong>. <strong>Scrotum</strong> — sac that holds the testes <strong>outside the body</strong>, keeping them cooler. <strong>Sperm ducts</strong> — carry sperm from the testes to the urethra. <strong>Prostate gland</strong> — adds <strong>fluid</strong> (with nutrients) to the sperm to make <strong>semen</strong>. <strong>Urethra</strong> — carries semen (and, at other times, urine) through the penis to the outside. <strong>Penis</strong> — becomes erect to deposit semen in the vagina. (Epididymis stores sperm.)</p>",0,""],["n-b15-exp-female-reproductive-system","bio-expl",15,"Female reproductive system — parts and functions","<p><strong>Ovaries</strong> — contain follicles; <strong>make eggs</strong> (meiosis) and hormones oestrogen and progesterone. <strong>Oviducts (Fallopian tubes)</strong> — carry the egg from ovary to uterus (cilia); <strong>site of fertilisation</strong>. <strong>Uterus</strong> (womb) — thick muscular wall and blood-rich lining; <strong>where the embryo implants and the fetus develops</strong>. <strong>Cervix</strong> — ring of muscle at the base of the uterus; narrow opening to the vagina; holds fetus in until birth. <strong>Vagina</strong> — receives the penis/sperm during intercourse; birth canal.</p>",0,""],["n-b15-exp-adaptive-features-of-sperm","bio-expl",15,"Adaptive features of sperm","<p><strong>Flagellum</strong> (tail) — to <strong>swim</strong> to the egg. <strong>Mitochondria</strong> (many, in mid-piece) — release <strong>energy by respiration</strong> for swimming. <strong>Acrosome enzymes</strong> — <strong>digest the jelly coat</strong> so the sperm can enter the egg. (Also haploid nucleus; streamlined head.)</p>",0,""],["n-b15-exp-adaptive-features-of-egg-c","bio-expl",15,"Adaptive features of egg cells","<p><strong>Energy stores</strong> (cytoplasm with yolk/fat droplets) — nourish the zygote/embryo for its first divisions before implantation. <strong>Jelly coat</strong> — <strong>changes at fertilisation</strong> (hardens) so <strong>only one sperm can enter</strong>. (Large size; haploid nucleus.)</p>",0,""],["n-b15-dif-embryo-vs-zygote","bio-diff",15,"Embryo vs zygote","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Zygote</th><th>Embryo</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What</td><td><strong>Single</strong> diploid cell formed at fertilisation</td><td><strong>Ball of cells</strong> formed by repeated mitosis of the zygote</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Oviduct</td><td>Oviduct → implants in uterus lining</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Timing</td><td>Moment of fertilisation</td><td>From first division to about 8 weeks</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b15-dif-testosterone-vs-oestrogen","bio-diff",15,"Testosterone vs oestrogen","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Testosterone</th><th>Oestrogen</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Made in</td><td><strong>Testes</strong></td><td><strong>Ovaries</strong> (follicle); placenta in pregnancy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Secondary sexual characteristics</td><td>Deep voice, facial and body hair, muscle growth, broad shoulders, sperm production</td><td>Breasts, wider hips, pubic hair, start of menstrual cycle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Other role</td><td>—</td><td><strong>Repairs and thickens uterus lining</strong>; stimulates LH surge; inhibits FSH <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b15-dif-fetus-vs-embryo","bio-diff",15,"Fetus vs embryo","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Embryo</th><th>Fetus</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Stage</td><td>Fertilisation → ≈ 8 weeks</td><td>≈ 8 weeks → birth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Organs</td><td>Forming</td><td><strong>All organs formed</strong>; growing and maturing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Appearance</td><td>Ball of cells → tiny body</td><td>Recognisably human</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b15-dif-sperm-vs-egg-cell","bio-diff",15,"Sperm vs egg cell","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Sperm</th><th>Egg cell</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Size</strong></td><td><strong>Very small</strong> (≈ 50 µm long, head ≈ 5 µm)</td><td><strong>Large</strong> (≈ 100–150 µm)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Structure</strong></td><td>Head (haploid nucleus + acrosome), mid-piece (mitochondria), <strong>flagellum</strong></td><td>Large cytoplasm with <strong>energy stores</strong>, haploid nucleus, <strong>jelly coat</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Motility</strong></td><td><strong>Motile</strong> — swims (≈ 4 mm per minute)</td><td><strong>Not motile</strong> — moved along oviduct by cilia/muscle</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Numbers</strong></td><td><strong>Millions</strong> per ejaculation; made continuously from puberty</td><td><strong>One</strong> released per month</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b15-dif-fsh-vs-lh-vs-oestrogen-vs","bio-diff",15,"FSH vs LH vs oestrogen vs progesterone","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Hormone</th><th>Made by</th><th>Main role in the cycle</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>FSH</strong></td><td>Pituitary gland</td><td>Stimulates a <strong>follicle</strong> (egg) to develop; stimulates oestrogen secretion</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>LH</strong></td><td>Pituitary gland</td><td>Surge causes <strong>ovulation</strong>; follicle → corpus luteum</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Oestrogen</strong></td><td>Ovary (follicle); placenta</td><td><strong>Repairs/thickens uterus lining</strong>; inhibits FSH; triggers LH surge</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Progesterone</strong></td><td><strong>Corpus luteum</strong>; placenta</td><td><strong>Maintains</strong> the thick lining; inhibits FSH and LH; keeps pregnancy going</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b15-fac-aids-is-caused-by-hiv","bio-facts",15,"Cause of AIDS?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">AIDS is caused by HIV</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-if-the-egg-is-fertilised-p","bio-facts",15,"If the egg is fertilised: what happens to progesterone and FSH?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">If the egg is fertilised, progesterone levels stay high; FSH is inhibited (no new follicles develop)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So there is no menstruation and no ovulation during pregnancy.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-fsh-and-lh-are-made-contro","bio-facts",15,"Which gland makes FSH and LH?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">FSH and LH are made (controlled) by the pituitary gland</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Small endocrine gland at the base of the brain.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-sperm-swim-at-about-4-mm-p","bio-facts",15,"How fast do sperm swim?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sperm swim at about 4 mm per minute</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>About a million (hundreds of millions) are deposited, so a few reach the egg.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-semen-fluid-containing-spe","bio-facts",15,"Semen = ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Semen = fluid containing sperm</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Fluid from the prostate gland (and seminal vesicles) nourishes the sperm.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-acrosome-digestive-enzymes","bio-facts",15,"Acrosome – contains?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Acrosome — digestive enzymes</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-cells-in-ovaries-and-teste","bio-facts",15,"How do cells in the ovaries and testes divide to form gametes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cells in ovaries and testes divide by meiosis to form gametes</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-egg-producing-organ-ovary","bio-facts",15,"Egg-producing organ?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Egg-producing organ — ovary</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-other-name-for-oviduct-fal","bio-facts",15,"Other name for the oviduct?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Other name for oviduct — Fallopian tube</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-uterus-about-the-size-of-a","bio-facts",15,"Size of the uterus?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Uterus — about the size of a clenched fist</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It stretches enormously during pregnancy.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-sperm-are-stored-in-the-ep","bio-facts",15,"Where are sperm stored?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sperm are stored in the epididymis</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-fertilisation-happens-in-t","bio-facts",15,"Where do fertilisation and implantation happen?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fertilisation happens in the oviduct; implantation in the uterus.</p>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-mother-s-and-fetus-s-blood","bio-facts",15,"Do the mother&#x27;s and the fetus&#x27;s blood mix?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Mother's and fetus's blood never mix.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It would cause clotting/immune reactions (different blood groups) and high maternal blood pressure would damage the fetus.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b15-fac-birth-the-muscular-uterus","bio-facts",15,"What happens during birth?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Birth: the muscular uterus wall contracts, cervix widens, baby pushed out through vagina; then the placenta (afterbirth) is expelled.</p>",0,""],["n-b16-exp-test-cross","bio-expl",16,"Test cross","<p>Used <strong>to identify an unknown genotype</strong> of an organism showing the <strong>dominant</strong> phenotype (AA or Aa?). <strong>Cross it with a homozygous recessive (aa) individual.</strong> If <strong>all offspring show the dominant phenotype</strong> → unknown was <strong>homozygous AA</strong>. If about <strong>half show the recessive phenotype (1 : 1)</strong> → unknown was <strong>heterozygous Aa</strong>. (Even one recessive offspring proves Aa.)</p>",1,""],["n-b16-exp-how-proteins-are-made","bio-expl",16,"How proteins are made","<ol>\n<li><strong>In the nucleus:</strong> the <strong>gene coding for the protein remains in the nucleus</strong>; a copy of it — <strong>messenger RNA (mRNA)</strong> — is made (the DNA unwinds, the mRNA copies the base sequence).</li>\n<li><strong>mRNA moves out of the nucleus into the cytoplasm</strong> (through nuclear pores).</li>\n<li><strong>In the cytoplasm/inside the cell:</strong> <strong>the mRNA passes through a ribosome</strong>; <strong>the ribosome assembles amino acids into a protein molecule</strong>; <strong>the specific sequence of amino acids is determined by the sequence of bases in the mRNA</strong> (three bases code for one amino acid).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then the chain folds into its specific shape. (Details of transcription/translation not required.)</p>",1,""],["n-b16-exp-blood-grouping-abo","bio-expl",16,"Blood grouping (ABO)","<p>Controlled by <strong>one gene with three alleles: Iᴬ, Iᴮ and Iᴼ</strong>. <strong>Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant</strong>; <strong>Iᴼ is recessive</strong> to both. Phenotypes (4): <strong>A</strong> (IᴬIᴬ or IᴬIᴼ), <strong>B</strong> (IᴮIᴮ or IᴮIᴼ), <strong>AB</strong> (IᴬIᴮ — codominance), <strong>O</strong> (IᴼIᴼ). It is an example of <strong>discontinuous variation</strong>. e.g. IᴬIᴼ × IᴮIᴼ → children can be A, B, AB or O in ratio <strong>1 : 1 : 1 : 1</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-b16-exp-base-sequence-and-proteins","bio-expl",16,"Base sequence and proteins","<p><strong>The sequence of bases in a gene determines the sequence of amino acids used to make a specific protein.</strong> <strong>Different sequences of amino acids give different shapes to protein molecules</strong> (and so different functions — e.g. enzyme active site, antibody shape).</p>",1,""],["n-b16-exp-dna-controls-cell-function","bio-expl",16,"DNA controls cell function","<p><strong>DNA controls cell function by controlling the production of proteins, including enzymes, membrane carriers and receptors for neurotransmitters.</strong> Enzymes control all the cell's reactions.</p>",1,""],["n-b16-exp-gene-expression","bio-expl",16,"Gene expression","<p><strong>Most body cells in an organism contain the same genes, but many genes in a particular cell are not expressed because the cell only makes the specific proteins it needs.</strong> (e.g. only pancreas cells make insulin; only red cell precursors make haemoglobin).</p>",1,""],["n-b16-exp-pedigree-diagrams","bio-expl",16,"Pedigree diagrams","<p>Family trees (squares = male, circles = female; shaded = affected) showing how a characteristic is inherited. <strong>Clue for recessive:</strong> two unaffected parents have an affected child → both parents must be <strong>heterozygous carriers</strong>. Work out genotypes from affected (homozygous recessive) individuals first.</p>",0,""],["n-b16-for-probability-of-an-offsprin","bio-form",16,"Probability of an offspring's phenotype","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">probability = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">number of offspring with that outcome</span><span class=\"de\">total number of outcomes</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">A 3 : 1 ratio gives ¾ = 0.75 = 75%. A 1 : 1 ratio gives ½ = 0.5 = 50%.</p>",0,""],["n-b16-dif-mitosis-vs-meiosis","bio-diff",16,"Mitosis vs meiosis","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Mitosis</th><th>Meiosis</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Type</td><td>Nuclear division → <strong>identical</strong> cells</td><td><strong>Reduction division</strong> → <strong>genetically different</strong> cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Number of daughter cells</td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>4</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chromosome number</td><td><strong>Maintained</strong> (diploid → diploid, 46 → 46)</td><td><strong>Halved</strong> (diploid → haploid, 46 → 23)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Variation</td><td><strong>None</strong> — identical to parent</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — new combinations of alleles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where (humans)</td><td>All body cells (skin, bone marrow, growing tissues)</td><td><strong>Testes and ovaries</strong> only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where (plants)</td><td>Root and shoot tips (meristems)</td><td><strong>Anthers and ovules</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Role</td><td>Growth, repair, replacement, asexual reproduction, stem cells</td><td><strong>Making gametes</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b16-dif-homozygous-vs-heterozygous","bio-diff",16,"Homozygous vs heterozygous","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Homozygous</th><th>Heterozygous</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Alleles</td><td><strong>Two identical</strong> (TT or tt)</td><td><strong>Two different</strong> (Tt)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pure-breeding?</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (if bred with identical homozygote)</td><td><strong>No</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gametes made</td><td>One type</td><td>Two types (T and t, 50:50)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b16-dif-genotype-vs-phenotype","bio-diff",16,"Genotype vs phenotype","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Genotype</th><th>Phenotype</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Alleles present</td><td>Observable features</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Written as</td><td>Letters, e.g. Tt, IᴬIᴼ, XᴮXᵇ</td><td>Words, e.g. tall, blood group A, normal vision</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Affected by environment?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (often)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b16-dif-dominant-allele-vs-recessi","bio-diff",16,"Dominant allele vs recessive allele","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Dominant</th><th>Recessive</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Expressed when</td><td>Present at all (one or two copies)</td><td>Only when <strong>two</strong> copies (no dominant allele)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Symbol</td><td>Capital letter (T)</td><td>Small letter (t)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heterozygote shows</td><td>Dominant phenotype</td><td>Hidden (carrier)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b16-dif-x-chromosome-vs-y-chromoso","bio-diff",16,"X chromosome vs Y chromosome","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>X chromosome</th><th>Y chromosome</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Size</td><td><strong>Larger</strong></td><td><strong>Smaller</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Genes</td><td>Many (including colour vision, blood clotting)</td><td>Few (including gene for maleness)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Females</td><td>Two (XX)</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Males</td><td>One (from mother)</td><td>One (from father)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>In gametes</td><td>All eggs; half of sperm</td><td>Half of sperm</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-bases-in-dna-a-t-g-c","bio-facts",16,"The four bases in DNA?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bases in DNA: A, T, G, C</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Adenine, thymine, guanine, cytosine (full names not required).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-sex-linked-genes-are-found","bio-facts",16,"Where are sex-linked genes found?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sex-linked genes are found only on the non-homologous part of the X (or Y) chromosome</p>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-abo-blood-groups-codominan","bio-facts",16,"ABO blood groups – which kind of inheritance?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">ABO blood groups — codominance (Iᴬ and Iᴮ)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Iᴼ is recessive.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-human-body-cell-46-chromos","bio-facts",16,"Chromosome number in a human body cell and in a gamete?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Human body cell: 46 chromosomes (23 pairs); gamete: 23.</p>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-three-bases-code-for-one-a","bio-facts",16,"How many bases code for one amino acid?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Three bases code for one amino acid.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So a protein of 51 amino acids needs a gene of at least 153 bases.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-a-red-blood-cell-has-no-nu","bio-facts",16,"Does a red blood cell have chromosomes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A red blood cell has no nucleus, so no chromosomes.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Exam favourite.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b16-fac-ratios-are-only-probabilit","bio-facts",16,"Are genetic ratios exact?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Ratios are only probabilities.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>With small numbers of offspring the actual ratio may differ from 3:1 or 1:1.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b17-exp-causes-of-variation","bio-expl",17,"Causes of variation","<p><strong>Genetic (genotype-based)</strong> — different alleles inherited (caused by mutation, meiosis, random mating, random fertilisation). <strong>Environmental</strong> — effects of surroundings during life (diet, light, water, minerals, temperature, disease, exercise, training, accidents) — environmental variation is <strong>not inherited</strong>. <strong>Discontinuous variation is usually caused by genes only; continuous variation is caused by both genes and the environment.</strong></p>",0,""],["n-b17-exp-causes-of-genetic-variatio","bio-expl",17,"Causes of genetic variation","<p><strong>Mutation, meiosis, random mating and random fertilisation are sources of genetic variation in populations.</strong> 1. <strong>Mutation</strong> — <strong>genetic change</strong>; <strong>the way in which new alleles are formed</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Gene mutation = a random change in the base sequence of DNA</strong>. <strong>Ionising radiation</strong> (X-rays, gamma rays, UV, radioactive materials) and <strong>some chemicals</strong> (mutagens — e.g. tar in tobacco smoke, mustard gas, some heavy metals) <strong>increase the rate of mutation</strong>.</p>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>Meiosis</strong> — produces genetically different gametes (new combinations of alleles).</li>\n<li><strong>Random mating</strong> — any individual may mate with any other.</li>\n<li><strong>Random fertilisation</strong> — any one of millions of gametes may fuse with any other.</li>\n</ol>",1,""],["n-b17-exp-characteristics-of-xerophy","bio-expl",17,"Characteristics of xerophytes (and how they help)","<ol>\n<li><strong>Closing stomata</strong> (during the hottest part of the day; some open at night) → less water vapour lost.</li>\n<li><strong>Thick waxy cuticle</strong> → waterproof; reduces evaporation from the surface.</li>\n<li><strong>Hairy leaves</strong> → trap a layer of humid air → reduces the water vapour gradient → less diffusion out.</li>\n<li><strong>Sunken stomata, mostly on the underside of leaves</strong> (in pits/grooves; rolled leaves) → humid air trapped around stomata → less transpiration.</li>\n<li><strong>Reduced surface area of leaves</strong> (small, needle-like or spines) → less surface for water loss.</li>\n<li><strong>Deep or widespread (shallow, spreading) roots</strong> → reach deep water or absorb rain quickly over a wide area.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Also: <strong>swollen stems/leaves that store water</strong> (succulents); few stomata.</p>",1,""],["n-b17-exp-characteristics-of-hydroph","bio-expl",17,"Characteristics of hydrophytes (and how they help)","<ol>\n<li><strong>Roots float freely</strong> (water hyacinth) / few, small roots — water and minerals can be absorbed through the whole surface; roots not needed for support.</li>\n<li><strong>Stems with hollow air spaces</strong> → buoyancy, keep leaves at the surface for light; store O₂ for respiration.</li>\n<li><strong>Leaves with air spaces</strong>; submerged leaves <strong>thin and feathery</strong> (<em>Cabomba</em>) → large surface area for absorbing light, CO₂ and minerals; not damaged by water currents.</li>\n<li><strong>Stomata on the upper surface</strong> of floating leaves (none on the lower surface in contact with water) → gas exchange with air.</li>\n<li><strong>Roots penetrate into mud to hold the plant in position</strong> (rooted species, e.g. water lily).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Also: little or no cuticle (no need to save water); little xylem/support tissue (water supports them).</p>",1,""],["n-b17-exp-natural-selection","bio-expl",17,"Natural selection","<p>The process by which individuals with features that are better adapted to their environment are more likely to <strong>survive, reproduce and pass on their alleles</strong> to the next generation. It involves: (a) <strong>genetic variation within populations</strong>; (b) <strong>production of many offspring</strong> (over-production); (c) <strong>struggle for survival, including competition for resources</strong> (food, water, mates, space) and predation/disease; (d) <strong>a greater chance of reproduction by individuals that are better adapted to the environment than others</strong>; (e) <strong>these individuals pass on their alleles to the next generation</strong>. Over many generations the frequency of advantageous alleles increases.</p>",0,""],["n-b17-exp-mrsa","bio-expl",17,"MRSA","<p><strong>Methicillin-resistant <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em></strong> — a strain of bacteria <strong>resistant to the antibiotic methicillin (and many others)</strong> — a 'superbug', often found in hospitals; very hard to treat. <strong>Development of resistant strains = natural selection</strong>: in a population of bacteria, a random <strong>mutation</strong> gives one bacterium resistance → antibiotic kills the non-resistant bacteria → the <strong>resistant ones survive</strong> (no competition) → they <strong>reproduce rapidly</strong> by binary fission, passing on the resistance allele → whole population becomes resistant. <strong>Using antibiotics only when essential limits the development of resistant bacteria such as MRSA</strong> — fewer chances for resistant bacteria to be selected. Also: complete the full course; don't use antibiotics for viral infections; limit use in farm animals; good hospital hygiene.</p>",1,""],["n-b17-exp-artificial-selection-selec","bio-expl",17,"Artificial selection (selective breeding)","<p>Humans <strong>choose individuals with desirable features</strong>, <strong>cross them to produce the next generation</strong>, then <strong>select offspring showing the desirable features</strong> and breed from them — repeated <strong>over many generations</strong> to improve <strong>crop plants</strong> (higher yield, disease resistance, drought tolerance, flavour, uniform ripening) and <strong>domesticated animals</strong> (more milk/meat/eggs/wool, docile temperament, disease resistance). e.g. high-yielding wheat; Holstein dairy cattle; dog breeds.</p>",0,""],["n-b17-dif-continuous-vs-discontinuou","bio-diff",17,"Continuous vs discontinuous variation","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Continuous</th><th>Discontinuous</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Phenotypes</td><td><strong>Range between two extremes</strong> (any value)</td><td><strong>Limited number of distinct categories</strong>, no intermediates</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Caused by</td><td><strong>Genes (many) + environment</strong></td><td><strong>Genes only</strong> (usually one gene)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Height, body mass, body length, leaf length</td><td><strong>ABO blood group</strong>, pea seed shape/colour, tongue rolling</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Graph</td><td><strong>Histogram</strong> — bars touch; bell-shaped curve</td><td><strong>Bar chart</strong> — bars separate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Measured</td><td>Measured (quantitative)</td><td>Counted into categories</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b17-dif-xerophyte-vs-hydrophyte","bio-diff",17,"Xerophyte vs hydrophyte","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Xerophyte</th><th>Hydrophyte</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Habitat</td><td>Dry — water scarce</td><td>In water (floating/submerged)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cuticle</td><td><strong>Thick, waxy</strong></td><td><strong>Thin or absent</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stomata</td><td>Few, <strong>sunken</strong>, on lower surface, closed by day</td><td>On <strong>upper surface</strong> of floating leaves; none on submerged parts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Leaves</td><td>Small / spines / rolled / hairy (reduced surface area)</td><td>Large floating leaves or <strong>thin, feathery</strong> submerged leaves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Roots</td><td><strong>Deep or widespread</strong></td><td>Small or <strong>free-floating</strong>; some anchor in mud</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Air spaces</td><td>Few</td><td><strong>Many large air spaces</strong> (buoyancy)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Support tissue</td><td>Well developed</td><td>Little (water supports)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Cactus, <em>Sempervivum</em>, oleander, marram grass</td><td>Water hyacinth (<em>Eichhornia</em>), water lily, <em>Cabomba</em></td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b17-dif-natural-selection-advantag","bio-diff",17,"Natural selection — advantages and disadvantages","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>For the species/population</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Advantages</td><td>Populations become <strong>adapted</strong> to their environment and can <strong>change as the environment changes</strong>; keeps genetic variation; leads to evolution of new species</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disadvantages</td><td>Very <strong>slow</strong> (many generations); individuals less well adapted die; if the environment changes too fast, the species may become extinct; can produce harmful outcomes for humans (e.g. <strong>antibiotic-resistant bacteria</strong>, pesticide-resistant insects)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b17-dif-natural-selection-vs-artif","bio-diff",17,"Natural selection vs artificial selection","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Natural selection</th><th>Artificial selection</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Who/what selects</td><td><strong>The environment</strong> (selection pressures)</td><td><strong>Humans</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Features selected</td><td>Those that help <strong>survival and reproduction</strong></td><td>Those <strong>useful to humans</strong> (yield, appearance) — may not help survival</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td><strong>Slow</strong> — many generations</td><td><strong>Faster</strong> (strong, deliberate selection)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Genetic variation</td><td>Large amount kept</td><td><strong>Reduced</strong> (inbreeding)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Result</td><td>Populations adapted to environment; new species</td><td>New varieties/breeds of crops and livestock</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Antibiotic resistance, peppered moth</td><td>High-yield wheat, dairy cattle</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b17-dif-artificial-selection-advan","bio-diff",17,"Artificial selection — advantages and disadvantages","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Artificial selection (selective breeding)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Advantages</td><td>Higher <strong>yield</strong> (more food), better quality/flavour, <strong>disease resistance</strong>, drought tolerance, docile animals, uniform crops; faster than natural selection; no genes from other species (accepted by most people)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disadvantages</td><td><strong>Loss of genetic variation</strong> → whole crop/breed may be wiped out by a new disease or climate change; <strong>inbreeding</strong> problems (harmful recessive conditions); animal welfare problems (e.g. broiler chickens too heavy to walk); slow compared with GM (many generations); rare original breeds may become extinct</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-antibiotics-used-against-s","bio-facts",17,"Antibiotics used against Staphylococcus aureus?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Antibiotics used against <em>Staphylococcus aureus</em>: penicillin, oxacillin, amoxicillin, methicillin</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>MRSA is resistant to methicillin (and the related penicillin-type antibiotics).</p></div>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-natural-selection-example","bio-facts",17,"Classic example of natural selection (a moth)?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Natural selection example — peppered moth (<em>Biston betularia</em>)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Dark form increased in polluted areas (camouflaged on soot); selection pressure = bird predation.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-hydrophytes-eichhornia-cra","bio-facts",17,"Examples of hydrophytes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Hydrophytes — <em>Eichhornia crassipes</em> (water hyacinth), <em>Cabomba</em></p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-xerophytes-sempervivum-ole","bio-facts",17,"Examples of xerophytes?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Xerophytes — <em>Sempervivum</em>, oleander</p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-continuous-variation-is-ca","bio-facts",17,"Continuous variation is caused by…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Continuous variation is caused by genes + environment</p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-discontinuous-variation-is","bio-facts",17,"Discontinuous variation is caused by…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Discontinuous variation is caused by genes</p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-discontinuous-variation-ex","bio-facts",17,"Example of discontinuous variation?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Discontinuous variation example: ABO blood groups (A, B, AB, O)</p>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-most-mutations-are-harmful","bio-facts",17,"Are most mutations harmful, neutral or beneficial?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Most mutations are harmful or neutral; a few are beneficial.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Mutations in gametes can be inherited; in body cells they may cause cancer.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b17-fac-bacteria-can-evolve-resist","bio-facts",17,"Why can bacteria evolve resistance quickly?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Bacteria can evolve resistance quickly because they reproduce very fast (every ~20 min) and are very numerous.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-exp-energy-losses-between-trop","bio-expl",18,"Energy losses between trophic levels","<p>Energy transfer is <strong>inefficient</strong> — usually only <strong>≈ 10%</strong> passes to the next level. Energy is lost:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>As heat from respiration</strong> (the main loss) — used for movement, keeping warm (mammals/birds), active transport, etc.</li>\n<li><strong>Incomplete consumption</strong> — not all parts are eaten (roots, bones, shells, wood).</li>\n<li><strong>Not all eaten food is digested/absorbed</strong> — lost in <strong>faeces</strong> (egestion).</li>\n<li><strong>Excretory wastes</strong> — energy in urea/urine.</li>\n<li>Some organisms die without being eaten (energy passes to decomposers).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why food chains usually have fewer than five trophic levels</strong><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: so much energy is lost at each step that after 4–5 levels there is <strong>not enough energy left</strong> to support another population.</p>",1,""],["n-b18-exp-carbon-cycle","bio-expl",18,"Carbon cycle","<p>The cycling of carbon between the atmosphere and living things, limited to: <strong>photosynthesis</strong> (removes CO₂ from air → glucose in plants); <strong>feeding</strong> (carbon compounds pass along food chains); <strong>respiration</strong> (plants, animals and decomposers return CO₂ to the air); <strong>decomposition</strong> (decomposers respire the carbon compounds in dead organisms and waste → CO₂); <strong>formation of fossil fuels</strong> (dead organisms not decomposed, buried and compressed over millions of years → coal, oil, natural gas); <strong>combustion</strong> (burning fossil fuels and wood → CO₂). Deforestation and combustion increase atmospheric CO₂ → enhanced greenhouse effect.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-exp-nitrogen-cycle","bio-expl",18,"Nitrogen cycle","<ul>\n<li><strong>Decomposition of plant and animal protein (and urea) to ammonium ions</strong> by <strong>decomposers</strong> (bacteria and fungi).</li>\n<li><strong>Nitrification</strong> — <strong>nitrifying bacteria</strong> convert ammonium ions → nitrite → <strong>nitrate ions</strong> (needs O₂ — aerated soil).</li>\n<li><strong>Nitrogen fixation</strong> — N₂ gas converted to nitrogen compounds <strong>by lightning</strong> (N₂ + O₂ → oxides → nitrates in rain) and <strong>by nitrogen-fixing bacteria</strong> (free-living in soil, or in <strong>root nodules of legumes</strong> such as peas, beans, clover). (Industrially: the Haber process → <strong>fertilisers</strong>.)</li>\n<li><strong>Absorption of nitrate ions by plants</strong> — by <strong>active transport</strong> through root hairs.</li>\n<li><strong>Production of amino acids and proteins</strong> in plants (nitrate + glucose).</li>\n<li><strong>Feeding and digestion of proteins</strong> — animals eat plants; digest proteins to amino acids; make their own proteins.</li>\n<li><strong>Deamination</strong> — excess amino acids → <strong>urea</strong> (excreted) → decomposers → ammonium.</li>\n<li><strong>Denitrification</strong> — <strong>denitrifying bacteria</strong> convert <strong>nitrate ions → nitrogen gas</strong> (in <strong>waterlogged, anaerobic</strong> soils), returning N₂ to the air.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Roles of microorganisms</strong>: <strong>decomposition, nitrification, nitrogen fixation and denitrification</strong> (names like <em>Rhizobium</em> not required).</p>",1,""],["n-b18-exp-population-growth-phases-s","bio-expl",18,"Population growth phases (sigmoid growth curve)","<p>For a population in an environment with <strong>limited resources</strong>, numbers against time give an <strong>S-shaped (sigmoid) curve</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Lag phase</strong> — <strong>slow growth</strong>: few individuals; adjusting to the environment, maturing, finding mates; birth rate only slightly above death rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Log (exponential) phase</strong> — <strong>rapid growth</strong>, numbers doubling at a constant rate: plenty of food, space, few predators/disease — <strong>no limiting factors</strong>; birth rate ≫ death rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Stationary phase</strong> — numbers <strong>level off</strong>: <strong>limiting factors</strong> (food shortage, competition, predation, disease, toxic waste) increase; <strong>birth rate = death rate</strong>. Population at the carrying capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Death phase</strong> — numbers <strong>fall</strong>: food runs out, waste/toxins accumulate (e.g. ethanol for yeast), disease; <strong>death rate &gt; birth rate</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sigmoid growth curve</strong> = the S-shaped curve covering lag, log and stationary phases (death phase after, in closed cultures).</li>\n</ol>",1,""],["n-b18-exp-factors-affecting-populati","bio-expl",18,"Factors affecting population growth / limiting factors","<p><strong>Food supply</strong>, <strong>competition</strong> (for food, space, light, mates — within and between species), <strong>predation</strong>, <strong>disease</strong> (and infection/parasites); also environmental conditions (temperature, water, light, oxygen, space, accumulation of waste). Predator–prey numbers rise and fall in cycles (predator peaks lag behind prey peaks).</p>",0,""],["n-b18-exp-energy-flow","bio-expl",18,"Energy flow","<p><strong>The Sun is the principal source of energy input to biological systems.</strong> <strong>Light energy</strong> from the Sun is trapped by producers in photosynthesis → stored as <strong>chemical energy</strong> in organic molecules → passed along food chains by feeding → at every level some is released by respiration and <strong>eventually transferred to the environment as heat</strong>. <strong>Energy flows; it is not recycled</strong> (unlike carbon and nitrogen).</p>",0,""],["n-b18-exp-human-impact-via-food-chai","bio-expl",18,"Human impact via food chains and webs","<p><strong>Over-harvesting</strong> of a food species (e.g. overfishing cod, hunting) removes it from the web → its predators decline (food shortage) and its prey increase → can cause overgrazing etc. <strong>Introducing foreign (alien) species</strong> (e.g. rabbits/cane toads in Australia, water hyacinth) — they may have <strong>no natural predators</strong>, out-compete native species for food, or eat native species → native populations decline or become extinct.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-exp-why-eating-crop-plants-is","bio-expl",18,"Why eating crop plants is more energy-efficient","<p>Feeding crops to livestock and then eating the livestock <strong>adds an extra trophic level</strong>; ≈ 90% of the energy in the crop is lost by the livestock (respiration, heat, movement, faeces). So <strong>eating plants directly provides about 10× more energy</strong> per area of farmland — feeds more people.</p>",1,""],["n-b18-exp-interpreting-population-gr","bio-expl",18,"Interpreting population graphs","<p>Identify phases; quote figures; explain with births/deaths and limiting factors.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-for-efficiency-of-energy-trans","bio-form",18,"Efficiency of energy transfer","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% efficiency = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">energy passed to the next trophic level</span><span class=\"de\">energy in the previous trophic level</span></span> × 100</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Usually about 10%. The rest is lost as heat from respiration, in movement, and in parts not eaten or digested.</p>",1,""],["n-b18-dif-nitrification-vs-denitrifi","bio-diff",18,"Nitrification vs denitrification","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Nitrification</th><th>Denitrification</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Conversion</td><td><strong>Ammonium ions → nitrite → nitrate ions</strong></td><td><strong>Nitrate ions → nitrogen gas</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bacteria</td><td><strong>Nitrifying</strong> bacteria</td><td><strong>Denitrifying</strong> bacteria</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Soil conditions</td><td><strong>Aerated</strong> (needs oxygen)</td><td><strong>Waterlogged / anaerobic</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect on soil fertility</td><td><strong>Increases</strong> nitrate for plants</td><td><strong>Decreases</strong> — nitrogen lost to air</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Farmers encourage by</td><td>Ploughing, draining</td><td>(Avoid by draining)</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b18-dif-birth-rate-vs-death-rate","bio-diff",18,"Birth rate vs death rate","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Birth rate</th><th>Death rate</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>Number of new individuals born (per 1000 per year)</td><td>Number of individuals dying (per 1000 per year)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Population grows when</td><td>Birth rate <strong>&gt;</strong> death rate</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Stationary when</td><td>Birth rate <strong>=</strong> death rate</td><td>Birth rate <strong>=</strong> death rate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Population falls when</td><td>—</td><td>Death rate <strong>&gt;</strong> birth rate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Increased by</td><td>Plenty of food, few predators, mature population</td><td>Food shortage, disease, predation, competition, pollution</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b18-dif-lag-phase-vs-log-exponenti","bio-diff",18,"Lag phase vs log (exponential) phase","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Lag phase</th><th>Log (exponential) phase</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Position</td><td>Start of curve</td><td>After lag phase; steepest part</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Growth rate</td><td><strong>Slow</strong></td><td><strong>Fastest</strong> — numbers double each generation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reason</td><td>Few individuals; adjusting, maturing, finding mates</td><td>Plenty of resources; <strong>no limiting factors</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Birth vs death</td><td>Birth slightly &gt; death</td><td>Birth ≫ death</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b18-dif-pyramid-of-numbers-vs-biom","bio-diff",18,"Pyramid of numbers vs biomass vs energy","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Numbers</th><th>Biomass</th><th>Energy <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></th></tr>\n<tr><td>Shows</td><td>Number of organisms</td><td>Dry mass of organisms</td><td>Energy flowing through each level over time</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can be inverted?</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (large producers, parasites)</td><td>Rarely (e.g. plankton at one moment)</td><td><strong>Never</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Advantage</td><td>Easy to count</td><td>Allows for size</td><td>Allows for time/rate; shows efficiency; comparable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disadvantage</td><td>Ignores size</td><td>Must kill/dry organisms; snapshot only</td><td>Very difficult to measure</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b18-fac-about-90-of-energy-is-lost","bio-facts",18,"Energy lost and passed on at each trophic level?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">About 90% of energy is lost at each trophic level; ~10% passes on.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-fac-decomposers-are-essential","bio-facts",18,"Decomposers are essential to which cycles?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Decomposers are essential to both the carbon and nitrogen cycles.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-fac-legumes-peas-beans-clover","bio-facts",18,"Legumes – what do their root nodules contain, and why are they used?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Legumes (peas, beans, clover) have root nodules containing nitrogen-fixing bacteria — farmers use them in crop rotation.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-fac-food-chains-start-with-a-p","bio-facts",18,"Food chains: what do they start with, and which way do the arrows point?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Food chains start with a producer; arrows point to the eater.</p>",0,""],["n-b18-fac-yeast-in-a-flask-shows-a-s","bio-facts",18,"Yeast in a flask: shape of the population curve?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Yeast in a flask shows a sigmoid curve followed by a death phase (food used up; ethanol toxic).</p>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-food-production","bio-expl",19,"Food production","<p>Humans have increased food production by:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Agricultural machinery</strong> — to <strong>use larger areas of land and improve efficiency</strong> (ploughing, sowing, harvesting quickly with fewer workers).</li>\n<li><strong>Agricultural chemicals</strong> — <strong>chemical fertilisers</strong> to <strong>improve yields</strong> (supply nitrate, phosphate, potassium ions); <strong>insecticides</strong> to <strong>improve quality and yield</strong> (kill insect pests that damage crops); <strong>herbicides</strong> to <strong>reduce competition with weeds</strong> (for light, water, minerals).</li>\n<li><strong>Selective breeding</strong> — to <strong>improve production by crop plants</strong> (higher yield, disease resistance) and <strong>livestock/cattle</strong> (more milk, meat).</li>\n</ol>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-why-monoculture-is-more-ef","bio-expl",19,"Why monoculture is more efficient","<ol>\n<li><strong>Uninterrupted area</strong> → machinery works quickly. 2. <strong>Uniform crop</strong> → all ripens together, harvested at once. 3. <strong>Large area</strong> → efficiency of scale, cheaper per tonne. 4. <strong>Large quantities of produce</strong> → more profit; specialised equipment and knowledge.</li>\n</ol>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-disadvantages-of-monocultu","bio-expl",19,"Disadvantages of monoculture","<ol>\n<li><strong>Reduction in biodiversity</strong> — natural habitats cleared; hedgerows removed; only one plant species → few animal species. 2. <strong>Increase in pests</strong> — huge supply of one food → pest and disease populations explode and spread easily → more <strong>pesticides</strong> needed (which kill other species, including pollinators, and pests become resistant). 3. <strong>Reduction in soil fertility</strong> — same nutrients removed every year → more <strong>fertiliser</strong> needed (→ eutrophication); soil erosion when the field is bare. 4. Whole crop genetically uniform → vulnerable to one disease.</li>\n</ol>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-intensive-farming-intensiv","bio-expl",19,"Intensive farming (intensive livestock production)","<p>Keeping <strong>large numbers of animals in a small space</strong> (sheds, cages, feedlots) with controlled food, temperature and movement, so <strong>less energy is wasted</strong> (less movement and heat loss) → more meat/eggs/milk quickly and cheaply. See advantages/disadvantages (diff 70).</p>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-greenhouse-gases-co-and-ch","bio-expl",19,"Greenhouse gases — CO₂ and CH₄","<p>Gases that <strong>absorb infrared (heat) radiation</strong> and warm the atmosphere. <strong>Carbon dioxide</strong> — sources: <strong>combustion of fossil fuels</strong> (power stations, vehicles), burning forests, deforestation, respiration. <strong>Methane</strong> — sources: <strong>cattle and other ruminants</strong> (digestion), <strong>rice paddy fields</strong>, decomposition of waste in <strong>landfill sites</strong>, natural gas leaks, melting permafrost.</p>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-eutrophication","bio-expl",19,"Eutrophication","<p>A chain of events caused by <strong>extra nutrients (nitrate and phosphate ions) entering water</strong> — from <strong>leached fertilisers</strong>, <strong>untreated sewage</strong>, farm slurry, (food waste) — resulting in a <strong>decrease in dissolved oxygen</strong> and death of organisms. Sequence <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: <strong>increased availability of nitrate and other ions</strong> → <strong>increased growth of producers</strong> (algae/plants — algal bloom covers the surface, blocking light) → producers <strong>die</strong> (lack of light/nutrients) → <strong>increased decomposition</strong> after death of producers → <strong>increased aerobic respiration by decomposers</strong> (bacteria multiply) → <strong>reduction in dissolved oxygen</strong> → <strong>death of organisms requiring dissolved oxygen in water</strong> (fish, invertebrates).</p>",1,""],["n-b19-exp-methods-of-preventing-defo","bio-expl",19,"Methods of preventing deforestation / conserving forests","<p><strong>Education</strong> — teach local people and consumers the value of forests and sustainable timber. <strong>Protected areas</strong> — national parks/reserves where felling is banned or controlled. <strong>Quotas</strong> — legal limits on how many trees may be felled (certification schemes for sustainably harvested timber). <strong>Replanting</strong> — plant new trees to replace those felled (selective felling rather than clear-felling).</p>",1,""],["n-b19-exp-methods-of-conserving-fish","bio-expl",19,"Methods of conserving fish stocks","<p><strong>Education</strong> — of fishermen and consumers (choose sustainable fish). <strong>Closed seasons</strong> — no fishing during the breeding season so fish can spawn. <strong>Protected areas</strong> — marine reserves/no-take zones where fish can breed. <strong>Controlled net types and mesh size</strong> — large mesh lets <strong>young (undersized) fish escape</strong> to grow and breed; ban on harmful nets. <strong>Quotas</strong> — limits on the mass/number of each species caught. <strong>Monitoring</strong> — recording catches and surveying stocks so quotas can be adjusted.</p>",1,""],["n-b19-exp-why-species-become-endange","bio-expl",19,"Why species become endangered or extinct","<p><strong>Climate change</strong>, <strong>habitat destruction</strong>, <strong>hunting by humans</strong> (poaching), <strong>over-harvesting</strong> (overfishing, logging), <strong>pollution</strong>, <strong>introduction of new (alien) species</strong> (predators, competitors, diseases).</p>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-methods-of-conserving-enda","bio-expl",19,"Methods of conserving endangered species","<p>(a) <strong>Monitoring and protecting species and habitats</strong> (counting populations, legal protection, wardens, anti-poaching, protected reserves); (b) <strong>education</strong> (local communities, tourists); (c) <strong>captive breeding programmes</strong>; (d) <strong>seed banks</strong> (for plants).</p>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-maintaining-genetic-divers","bio-expl",19,"Maintaining genetic diversity","<p><strong>Risks to a species if its population size decreases, reducing genetic variation:</strong> less variation → <strong>less able to adapt</strong> to environmental change or a new disease (natural selection has little to act on) → more likely to become extinct; <strong>inbreeding</strong> → harmful recessive alleles expressed (e.g. infertility, disease). (Knowledge of genetic drift not required.) So conservation programmes swap breeding animals and use stored sperm/seeds.</p>",1,""],["n-b19-exp-reasons-for-conservation-p","bio-expl",19,"Reasons for conservation programmes","<p>(a) <strong>maintaining or increasing biodiversity</strong>; (b) <strong>reducing extinction</strong>; (c) <strong>protecting vulnerable ecosystems</strong> (e.g. coral reefs, rainforests, wetlands); (d) <strong>maintaining ecosystem functions — limited to nutrient cycling and resource provision, including food, drugs, fuel and genes</strong> (genes for crop breeding, e.g. disease resistance from wild relatives).</p>",1,""],["n-b19-exp-reasons-for-habitat-destru","bio-expl",19,"Reasons for habitat destruction","<p>(a) <strong>Increased area for housing, crop plant production and livestock production</strong> (growing human population); (b) <strong>extraction of natural resources</strong> (mining, quarrying, logging, oil); (c) <strong>freshwater and marine pollution</strong>. <strong>Through altering food webs and food chains, humans can have a negative impact on habitats</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-untreated-sewage-and-exces","bio-expl",19,"Untreated sewage and excess fertiliser in water","<p>Both add <strong>nutrients</strong> (nitrate, phosphate) → eutrophication → oxygen depletion → fish and invertebrates die. Untreated sewage also contains <strong>pathogens</strong> (cholera, typhoid) and organic matter that decomposers break down using oxygen.</p>",0,""],["n-b19-exp-non-biodegradable-plastics","bio-expl",19,"Non-biodegradable plastics","<p>Not broken down by decomposers → persist for hundreds of years. <strong>Aquatic:</strong> animals (turtles, seabirds, fish, whales) <strong>swallow</strong> plastic (blocks gut → starvation) or get <strong>entangled</strong> in nets/rings (drown, injuries); <strong>microplastics</strong> enter food chains; toxic chemicals released. <strong>Terrestrial:</strong> fill <strong>landfill</strong>, litter (visual pollution), animals eat or get trapped in it, toxins leach into soil and water; burning releases toxic gases.</p>",0,""],["n-b19-dif-monoculture-advantages-and","bio-diff",19,"Monoculture — advantages and disadvantages","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Large-scale monoculture</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Advantages</td><td>Efficient use of machinery; uniform crop harvested at once; specialised knowledge; high yield; cheaper food; more profit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disadvantages</td><td><strong>Loss of biodiversity</strong> (habitats destroyed, hedgerows removed); <strong>pests and diseases spread easily</strong> → more <strong>pesticides</strong> (harm other species/pollinators, resistance); <strong>soil fertility falls</strong> → more <strong>fertiliser</strong> (eutrophication); soil erosion; all plants susceptible to the same disease</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b19-dif-intensive-livestock-farmin","bio-diff",19,"Intensive livestock farming — advantages and disadvantages","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>Intensive livestock production</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Advantages</td><td><strong>High yield</strong> of meat/eggs/milk per unit area; <strong>cheap</strong> food; less energy wasted (less movement, kept warm); uses less land (more left wild); controlled conditions; easy to monitor animals</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disadvantages</td><td><strong>Disease spreads easily</strong> (crowding) → frequent <strong>antibiotics</strong> → antibiotic-resistant bacteria; <strong>animal welfare</strong> concerns (stress, little movement); large amounts of <strong>waste/slurry</strong> → <strong>water pollution (eutrophication)</strong>; <strong>methane</strong> release; high energy and water use; food crops/land used to grow animal feed (deforestation for soya)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b19-dif-intensive-vs-extensive-liv","bio-diff",19,"Intensive vs extensive livestock production","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Intensive</th><th>Extensive</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Animal density</td><td><strong>High</strong> — many animals in small space (indoors)</td><td><strong>Low</strong> — few animals on large area (outdoors)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Land needed</td><td>Little</td><td><strong>A lot</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Food supplied</td><td>Concentrated feed provided</td><td>Animals graze natural vegetation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Yield per hectare / cost</td><td><strong>High</strong> yield; <strong>cheap</strong></td><td>Lower yield; more expensive per kg</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disease / antibiotics</td><td>Spreads easily; antibiotics used routinely</td><td>Less disease</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Welfare</td><td>Often poor</td><td>Better (free to move)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pollution</td><td>Concentrated waste/slurry → eutrophication</td><td>Waste spread thinly; less pollution</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect on habitats</td><td>Less land cleared for the animals themselves (but land for feed crops)</td><td>More land cleared; risk of <strong>overgrazing</strong> → soil erosion</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-plastics-are-non-biodegrad","bio-facts",19,"Are plastics biodegradable?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Plastics are non-biodegradable</p>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-methane-is-produced-by-far","bio-facts",19,"Sources of methane from human activity?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Methane is produced by farming activities (cattle, rice fields) and by decomposers in rubbish (landfill)</p>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-without-the-greenhouse-eff","bio-facts",19,"Earth&#x27;s temperature without the greenhouse effect?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Without the greenhouse effect, Earth's average temperature would be about 33 °C lower than it is now</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The <em>natural</em> greenhouse effect is essential for life; the <em>enhanced</em> effect causes global warming.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-eutrophication-is-caused-b","bio-facts",19,"Eutrophication: cause, and why organisms die?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Eutrophication is caused by nutrients, but the organisms die from lack of oxygen.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Don't say fertiliser \"poisons\" the fish.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-deforestation-increases-co","bio-facts",19,"Two ways deforestation increases CO₂?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Deforestation increases CO₂ in two ways: less photosynthesis + burning/decomposition of the trees.</p>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-habitat-destruction-is-the","bio-facts",19,"Biggest threat to biodiversity?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Habitat destruction is the biggest threat to biodiversity.</p>",0,""],["n-b19-fac-seed-banks-keep-seeds-dry","bio-facts",19,"How do seed banks keep seeds?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Seed banks keep seeds dry and cold (about −20 °C) so they stay dormant for decades.</p>",0,""],["n-b20-exp-why-biotechnology-and-gm-u","bio-expl",20,"Why biotechnology and GM use bacteria","<p><strong>Rapid reproduction rate</strong> (divide every ~20 min → huge numbers quickly); <strong>ability to make complex molecules</strong> (e.g. proteins). <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Also: <strong>few ethical concerns over their manipulation and growth</strong>; <strong>presence of plasmids</strong> (small rings of DNA that are easy to remove, cut, insert genes into and put back). And: same genetic code as us; easy and cheap to grow in fermenters on simple nutrients.</p>",0,""],["n-b20-exp-how-genetic-modification-i","bio-expl",20,"How genetic modification is done (making human insulin in bacteria)","<p>(a) <strong>Isolation of the DNA making up a human gene</strong> (insulin gene) <strong>using restriction enzymes, forming sticky ends</strong>. (b) <strong>Cutting of bacterial plasmid DNA with the same restriction enzymes, forming complementary sticky ends</strong>. (c) <strong>Insertion of the human DNA into the bacterial plasmid DNA using DNA ligase to form a recombinant plasmid</strong>. (d) <strong>Insertion of recombinant plasmids into bacteria</strong> (specific details not required). (e) <strong>Multiplication of bacteria containing recombinant plasmids</strong> (in a fermenter). (f) <strong>Expression in bacteria of the human gene to make the human protein</strong> (insulin), which is extracted and purified.</p>",1,""],["n-b20-exp-anaerobic-respiration-of-y","bio-expl",20,"Anaerobic respiration of yeast in bread-making","<p>Yeast mixed with flour, water and sugar → dough left in a <strong>warm</strong> place → yeast respires (first aerobically, then <strong>anaerobically</strong>) → <strong>carbon dioxide</strong> bubbles are trapped in the dough → dough <strong>rises</strong> (light texture). Baking at high temperature <strong>kills the yeast</strong>, <strong>evaporates the ethanol</strong> and sets the bread; CO₂ bubbles expand.</p>",0,""],["n-b20-exp-pectinase-in-fruit-juice-p","bio-expl",20,"Pectinase in fruit juice production","<p><strong>Pectin</strong> is a substance that holds plant cell walls together. <strong>Pectinase</strong> breaks down pectin → <strong>more juice is extracted</strong> from fruit (cells break apart more easily; higher yield) and the juice is <strong>clearer</strong> (pectinase clarifies cloudy juice). Works faster when warm (enzyme).</p>",0,""],["n-b20-exp-biological-washing-powders","bio-expl",20,"Biological washing powders","<p>Contain enzymes: <strong>proteases</strong> (protein stains: blood, egg, grass), <strong>lipases</strong> (fat/grease stains: butter, oil) and <strong>amylases</strong> (starch stains). Enzymes break large insoluble stain molecules into <strong>small soluble molecules</strong> that wash out in water. Effective at <strong>lower temperatures</strong> (≈ 30–40 °C) → <strong>saves energy</strong>, and gentler on delicate fabrics; don't use very hot water (enzymes denatured). Not for silk/wool (proteins — digested by protease). Investigate: stained cloth squares washed with biological vs non-biological powder at different temperatures.</p>",0,""],["n-b20-exp-lactase-and-lactose-free-m","bio-expl",20,"Lactase and lactose-free milk","<p>Some people (lactose intolerant) cannot make <strong>lactase</strong>, so they can't digest <strong>lactose</strong> (milk sugar) → bacteria in the gut ferment it → wind, diarrhoea, pain. <strong>Lactase</strong> (made by microorganisms/yeast) is added to milk (or milk is passed over <strong>immobilised lactase</strong> on beads) → <strong>lactose → glucose + galactose</strong> → <strong>lactose-free milk</strong>. The glucose and galactose are sweeter and can also be used for making <strong>sweets</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-b20-exp-conditions-controlled-in-a","bio-expl",20,"Conditions controlled in a fermenter","<p><strong>Temperature</strong> — kept at the <strong>optimum</strong> for the microorganism's enzymes (respiration releases heat, so a <strong>water jacket</strong> cools it); too hot → enzymes denatured. <strong>pH</strong> — kept at the optimum (monitored by probe; acid/alkali added) because enzymes are pH-sensitive. <strong>Oxygen</strong> — sterile <strong>air bubbled in</strong> for aerobic respiration (for growth) — not for anaerobic processes; <strong>paddles stir</strong> to spread O₂, nutrients and heat. <strong>Nutrient supply</strong> — sugars, amino acids/nitrogen source, minerals added as needed for growth and product formation. <strong>Waste products</strong> — removed (e.g. CO₂ gas vented; toxic products) so they don't inhibit growth. Also <strong>sterile conditions</strong> throughout.</p>",1,""],["n-b20-exp-examples-of-genetic-modifi","bio-expl",20,"Examples of genetic modification","<p>(a) <strong>Insertion of human genes into bacteria to produce human proteins</strong> — <strong>insulin</strong> (for diabetics), human growth hormone. (b) <strong>Insertion of genes into crop plants to confer resistance to herbicides</strong> — e.g. herbicide-resistant <strong>soya</strong>: field can be sprayed to kill weeds without harming the crop. (c) <strong>Insertion of genes into crop plants to confer resistance to insect pests</strong> — e.g. <strong>Bt maize/cotton</strong>. (d) <strong>Insertion of genes into crop plants to improve nutritional qualities</strong> — e.g. <strong>Golden Rice</strong> (gene for <strong>beta-carotene</strong>, which the body converts to <strong>vitamin A</strong> → prevents blindness in countries where rice is the staple food).</p>",0,""],["n-b20-dif-advantages-and-disadvantag","bio-diff",20,"Advantages and disadvantages of GM crops (soya, maize, rice)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th></th><th>GM crops</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Advantages</td><td><strong>Higher yields</strong> (less damage from pests and weeds) → more food; <strong>less insecticide</strong> sprayed (Bt crops) → less harm to other insects and farmworkers; herbicide-resistant crops → weeds controlled easily; <strong>improved nutrition</strong> (Golden Rice — vitamin A); crops resistant to drought/salt can grow in poor land; longer shelf life; faster than selective breeding</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disadvantages</td><td>Genes may <strong>spread to wild relatives</strong> by pollen (cross-pollination) → 'superweeds' resistant to herbicide; insects may become <strong>resistant</strong> to Bt toxin; <strong>reduced biodiversity</strong> — fewer weeds/insects → less food for other species, and widespread use of a few GM varieties; <strong>increased herbicide use</strong> on herbicide-resistant crops; possible <strong>unknown health effects</strong> (e.g. allergies) worry some people; <strong>seeds expensive</strong>, patented by companies — poor farmers may become dependent; farmers can't save and replant seed; ethical objections</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b20-dif-bread-making-vs-biofuel-pr","bio-diff",20,"Bread-making vs biofuel production (both use yeast)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Bread-making</th><th>Ethanol (biofuel) production</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Product wanted</td><td><strong>Carbon dioxide</strong> (makes dough rise)</td><td><strong>Ethanol</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>By-product</td><td>Ethanol (evaporates in baking)</td><td>Carbon dioxide</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Conditions</td><td>Warm; sugar/flour; yeast respires aerobically then anaerobically</td><td>Warm; sugar; <strong>no oxygen</strong>; ethanol separated by distillation</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-b20-dif-penicillin-vs-insulin-prod","bio-diff",20,"Penicillin vs insulin production in fermenters","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Penicillin</th><th>Human insulin</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Organism</td><td>Fungus <em>Penicillium</em> (natural)</td><td><strong>GM bacterium</strong> (<em>E. coli</em>) with human insulin gene</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Product made</td><td>When nutrients run low (later in growth)</td><td>As bacteria grow and express the gene</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use</td><td>Antibiotic — kills bacteria</td><td>Treating type 1 diabetes</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-b20-fac-gm-bacteria-make-human-ins","bio-facts",20,"What do GM bacteria make (example)?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">GM bacteria make human insulin</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Identical to human insulin; cheap, plentiful, no animal ethical/religious issues, fewer allergic reactions than pig/cow insulin.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-sweets-glucose-and-galacto","bio-facts",20,"Lactase and sweets: which sugars?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sweets — glucose and galactose from lactose-free milk (lactase)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Treating milk with lactase gives sweeter sugars used in sweets.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-milk-lactase-lactose-free","bio-facts",20,"Lactase and milk?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Milk — lactase → lactose-free milk</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-biological-washing-powders","bio-facts",20,"Which enzymes are in biological washing powders?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Biological washing powders contain enzymes (protease, lipase, amylase)</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-fruit-juices-pectinase-mor","bio-facts",20,"Which enzyme is used for fruit juices, and why?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Fruit juices — pectinase (more juice, clearer)</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-biofuel-ethanol-from-yeast","bio-facts",20,"Which biofuel does yeast fermentation make?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Biofuel — ethanol (from yeast fermentation)</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-yeast-co-bread-rises-and-e","bio-facts",20,"Yeast: products used in baking and brewing?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Yeast — CO₂ (bread rises) and ethanol (alcohol, biofuel)</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Both by anaerobic respiration: glucose → ethanol + carbon dioxide.</p></div>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-mycoprotein-quorn-is-made","bio-facts",20,"What is mycoprotein (Quorn)?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Mycoprotein (Quorn) is made from the fungus <em>Fusarium</em> grown in fermenters — a high-protein, low-fat meat substitute.</p>",0,""],["n-b20-fac-golden-rice-contains-beta","bio-facts",20,"What does Golden Rice contain?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Golden Rice contains beta-carotene (vitamin A precursor).</p>",0,""]],"defs":[["d-b1-metabolism",1,"Metabolism","<p>All the chemical reactions that take place in a living organism (in its cells).</p>","<p>Respiration, photosynthesis, protein synthesis and digestion are all part of metabolism. The energy released by respiration is used to drive the other metabolic reactions.</p>",0],["d-b1-dry-mass",1,"Dry mass","<p>The mass of an organism (or tissue) after it has been killed and all the water has been removed from it.</p>","<p>(dried in an oven at about 100 °C until the mass stops changing). Used to measure <strong>growth</strong> because the water content of an organism changes from hour to hour, but dry mass only increases when new material (cells) is made. Growth = <strong>a permanent increase in size and dry mass</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b1-excretion",1,"Excretion","<p>The removal of the waste products of metabolism and substances in excess of requirements.</p>","<p>Examples: carbon dioxide (lungs), urea, excess water and excess ions (kidneys). <em>Faeces/egestion are NOT excretion</em> — faeces are undigested food that was never made by the body's metabolism.</p>",0],["d-b1-binomial-system",1,"Binomial system","<p>An internationally agreed system in which the scientific name of an organism is made up of two parts showing the genus and the species.</p>","<p>Genus first with a <strong>capital</strong> letter, species second with a <strong>small</strong> letter; printed in <em>italics</em>, <strong>underlined</strong> when handwritten. e.g. <em>Homo sapiens</em>, <em>Panthera leo</em> (lion), <em>Equus caballus</em> (horse), <em>Equus asinus</em> (donkey) — same genus, different species. Because the name is the same in every language, scientists worldwide know exactly which organism is meant.</p>",0],["d-b1-fronds",1,"Fronds","<p>The leaves of a fern.</p>","<p>Young fronds are tightly coiled and unroll as they grow. Spores are made in <strong>sporangia</strong> on the <strong>underside</strong> of the fronds.</p>",0],["d-b1-dichotomous-keys",1,"Dichotomous keys","<p>A way of identifying an organism by working through pairs of statements (each pair = two opposite choices) that lead you to its name.</p>","<p>'Dichotomous' = dividing into two. <em>Rules for constructing one:</em> use features that can be seen on <strong>one</strong> specimen; each pair must be <strong>opposites about the same feature</strong> (e.g. 'wings present / wings absent'); never use comparative or subjective words such as 'large', 'small', 'taller'; keep going until every organism is separated; the number of pairs = number of organisms − 1. <em>Rules for using one:</em> start at 1 every time and follow the instructions — never jump to a statement that 'looks right'.</p>",0],["d-b1-characteristics-of-living-orga",1,"Characteristics of living organisms (MRS GREN)","<p><strong>Movement</strong> — an action by an organism or part of an organism causing a change of position or place.</p><p><strong>Respiration</strong> — the chemical reactions in cells that break down nutrient molecules and release energy for metabolism.</p><p><strong>Sensitivity</strong> — the ability to detect and respond to changes in the internal or external environment.</p><p><strong>Growth</strong> — a permanent increase in size and dry mass.</p><p><strong>Reproduction</strong> — the processes that make more of the same kind of organism.</p><p><strong>Excretion</strong> — the removal of the waste products of metabolism and substances in excess of requirements.</p><p><strong>Nutrition</strong> — the taking in of materials for energy, growth and development.</p>","<p>All living things show all seven:<br />\nLearn these <strong>word-for-word</strong> (they are the syllabus definitions). A non-living thing (car, fire) may show <em>some</em> of them but never all seven. If asked what you could see by watching an organism for a few minutes, give movement, sensitivity, nutrition — not growth, respiration or reproduction.</p>",0],["d-b1-organism",1,"Organism","<p>A complete living thing (e.g. a bacterium, a mango tree, a human).</p>","",0],["d-b1-classification",1,"Classification","<p>Putting organisms into groups according to the features they share.</p>","<p>Makes identification easier, organises the huge number of species, and reflects how closely organisms are related.</p>",0],["d-b1-species",1,"Species","<p>A group of organisms that can reproduce to produce fertile offspring.</p>","<p>Horse × donkey → mule, which is <strong>infertile</strong>, so horses and donkeys are different species.</p>",0],["d-b1-genus",1,"Genus","<p>A group of closely related species that share similar features and a common ancestor (e.g. <em>Equus</em>).</p>","",0],["d-b1-kingdom",1,"Kingdom","<p>One of the largest groups into which organisms are classified.</p>","<p>Five kingdoms: <strong>animal, plant, fungus, prokaryote, protoctist</strong><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>. Order of groups (largest → smallest): kingdom → phylum → class → order → family → genus → species.</p>",0],["d-b1-arthropod",1,"Arthropod","<p>An invertebrate animal with jointed legs, a segmented body and a waterproof exoskeleton (supporting structure on the outside of the body).</p>","",0],["d-b1-vertebrate",1,"Vertebrate","<p>An animal with a backbone (vertebral column).</p>","<p>Five classes: mammals, birds, reptiles, amphibians, fish.</p>",0],["d-b1-hyphae-mycelium",1,"Hyphae / mycelium","<p>Hyphae: microscopic threads of cells joined end-to-end that make up the body of most fungi; a mass of hyphae = mycelium.</p>","",0],["d-b1-spores",1,"Spores","<p>Very small groups of cells (or single cells) surrounded by a protective wall, used in reproduction (fungi, ferns).</p>","",0],["d-b2-cells",2,"Cells","<p>The smallest units from which all organisms are made.</p><p>New cells are produced by division of existing cells.</p>","<p>Cells are the basic building blocks of life. New cells are made by mitosis in plants and animals, and by binary fission in bacteria. All cells contain cytoplasm, a cell membrane and DNA.</p>",0],["d-b2-tissues",2,"Tissues","<p>A group of cells with similar structures, working together to perform a shared function.</p>","<p>Examples: ciliated epithelium, muscle tissue, nervous tissue, blood (animals); palisade mesophyll, xylem, phloem, epidermis (plants).</p>",0],["d-b2-magnification",2,"Magnification","<p>How many times larger an image (drawing/photo) is than the real object.</p>","<p> Both sizes must be in the <strong>same units</strong> before dividing. Magnification has <strong>no units</strong> — write it as ×200.</p>",0],["d-b2-cell-levels-of-organisation",2,"Cell (levels of organisation)","<p><strong>Organ</strong> — a structure made of a group of <strong>tissues</strong> working together to perform a specific function (heart, stomach, brain, kidney, eye; leaf, stem, root, flower).</p><p><strong>Organ system</strong> — a group of <strong>organs</strong> with related functions working together to perform a body function (digestive, circulatory, nervous, respiratory, excretory, reproductive systems; plant shoot system).</p><p><strong>Organism</strong> — a complete living thing made of organ systems.</p>","<p><strong>Cell → tissue → organ → organ system → organism.</strong></p>",0],["d-b2-cell-membrane",2,"Cell membrane","<p>Partially permeable — controls what substances enter and leave the cell.</p>","<p>Very thin layer around the cytoplasm of <strong>every</strong> cell; <strong>partially permeable</strong> — <strong>controls what substances enter and leave the cell</strong>; holds the cell contents together.</p>",0],["d-b2-cell-wall",2,"Cell wall","<p>Fully permeable layer outside the cell membrane (cellulose in plants) — gives shape and strength and stops the cell bursting.</p>","<p>Tough layer <strong>outside</strong> the cell membrane in plant cells (made of <strong>cellulose</strong>), fungi and bacteria (not cellulose). <strong>Fully permeable</strong>. Gives the cell shape and strength, supports it and <strong>stops it bursting</strong> when it takes in water.</p>",0],["d-b2-cytoplasm",2,"Cytoplasm","<p>Where most of the cell's chemical (metabolic) reactions take place.</p>","<p>Clear jelly-like material (about 70% water) filling the cell; contains the organelles and dissolved substances; <strong>most metabolic (chemical) reactions happen here</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b2-nucleus",2,"Nucleus","<p>Contains the genetic material (DNA in chromosomes) and controls the activities of the cell.</p>","<p>Contains the genetic material (<strong>DNA in chromosomes</strong>); <strong>controls the activities of the cell</strong> (by controlling which proteins are made) and controls cell division.</p>",0],["d-b2-ribosomes",2,"Ribosomes","<p>Site of protein synthesis.</p>","<p>Tiny structures in the cytoplasm (free, or attached to membranes called <strong>rough endoplasmic reticulum</strong>) where <strong>proteins are made (protein synthesis)</strong> from amino acids. Found in all cells including bacteria. Seen only with an electron microscope.</p>",0],["d-b2-mitochondria",2,"Mitochondria","<p>Site of aerobic respiration, which releases energy.</p>","<p>Organelles where <strong>aerobic respiration</strong> happens, releasing energy. Have two membranes (inner one folded). Absent from bacteria and red blood cells.</p>",0],["d-b2-chloroplasts",2,"Chloroplasts","<p>Contain chlorophyll — the site of photosynthesis.</p>","<p>Organelles containing the green pigment <strong>chlorophyll</strong>; site of <strong>photosynthesis</strong>. Often contain <strong>starch grains</strong>. Only in some plant cells (e.g. palisade, spongy mesophyll, guard cells — <em>not</em> root cells or most epidermis cells).</p>",0],["d-b2-vacuole",2,"Vacuole","<p>Contains cell sap; keeps the plant cell turgid (firm).</p>","<p>Fluid-filled space surrounded by a membrane. Plant cells have a <strong>large permanent vacuole</strong> containing <strong>cell sap</strong> (water, sugars, mineral ions) which presses outwards and keeps the cell <strong>turgid</strong> (firm). Animal cells only have small, temporary vacuoles (<strong>vesicles</strong>).</p>",0],["d-b2-plasmids",2,"Plasmids","<p>Small circular loops of DNA in bacteria, in addition to the main circular DNA.</p>","<p>Small extra <strong>circular loops of DNA</strong> in bacteria (in addition to the main circular DNA); carry a few genes (e.g. antibiotic resistance); used as vectors in genetic modification.</p>",0],["d-b2-specialised-cells",2,"Specialised cells","<p><strong>Ciliated cells</strong> — movement of mucus in the trachea and bronchi.</p><p><strong>Root hair cells</strong> — absorption (of water and mineral ions).</p><p><strong>Palisade mesophyll cells</strong> — photosynthesis.</p><p><strong>Neurones</strong> — conduction of electrical impulses.</p><p><strong>Red blood cells</strong> — transport of oxygen.</p><p><strong>Sperm and egg cells (gametes)</strong> — reproduction.</p>","<p>Cells with a particular structure suited to a <strong>specific function</strong>:</p>",0],["d-b3-diffusion",3,"Diffusion","<p>The net movement of particles from a region of their higher concentration to a region of their lower concentration (i.e. down a concentration gradient), as a result of their random movement.</p>","<p>The <strong>energy for diffusion comes from the kinetic energy of random movement of molecules and ions</strong> — it is a passive process; the cell supplies no energy. <strong>Some substances move into and out of cells by diffusion through the cell membrane</strong> (e.g. O₂, CO₂, small solutes). <em>All three marking points are needed: net movement + high→low concentration + random movement.</em></p>",0],["d-b3-osmosis",3,"Osmosis","<p>The net movement of water molecules from a region of higher water potential (dilute solution) to a region of lower water potential (concentrated solution), through a partially permeable membrane.</p>","<p>Core: <strong>water diffuses through partially permeable membranes by osmosis</strong>; water moves into and out of cells by osmosis through the cell membrane. Osmosis is a special case of diffusion — <strong>only water</strong> moves, and it <strong>must</strong> cross a partially permeable membrane.</p>",0],["d-b3-water-potential-gradient",3,"Water potential gradient","<p>A difference in water potential between two areas.</p>","<p>Water potential = the tendency of water to move; <strong>dilute solution / pure water = high water potential</strong>; <strong>concentrated solution = low water potential</strong>. Water always moves <strong>down</strong> a water potential gradient (high → low) by osmosis. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",0],["d-b3-turgor-pressure",3,"Turgor pressure","<p>The pressure of water inside a plant cell pushing outwards on the cell wall.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>","<p>When a plant cell takes in water, the vacuole swells and presses the cytoplasm against the inelastic cell wall; the wall resists, preventing the cell bursting and stopping further net entry of water. <strong>Plants are supported by this pressure of water pressing outwards on the cell walls</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b3-plasmolysed",3,"Plasmolysed","<p>Describes a plant cell that has lost so much water by osmosis that the cytoplasm and cell membrane have pulled away (torn away) from the cell wall.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>","<p>Happens in a concentrated solution (lower water potential than the cell sap). The gap between wall and membrane fills with the outside solution (the wall is fully permeable). Animal cells cannot plasmolyse (they have no wall) — they <strong>shrink and crenate (shrivel)</strong> instead.</p>",0],["d-b3-active-transport",3,"Active transport","<p>The movement of particles through a cell membrane from a region of lower concentration to a region of higher concentration (i.e. against a concentration gradient), using energy from respiration.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Protein carriers</strong> in the membrane move the molecules or ions across, using energy from <strong>aerobic respiration</strong>. Importance: <strong>ion uptake by root hairs</strong> (e.g. <strong>nitrate ions</strong> absorbed from soil where they are less concentrated than in the root cell), glucose absorption in villi, reabsorption of glucose in kidney tubules. Anything that stops respiration (no oxygen, cyanide, low temperature) stops active transport.</p>",0],["d-b3-concentration-gradient",3,"Concentration gradient","<p>An imaginary 'slope' from a high concentration to a low concentration.</p>","<p>The steeper the gradient, the faster diffusion.</p>",0],["d-b3-net-movement",3,"Net movement","<p>Overall/average movement — particles move randomly in both directions, but more move from high to low.</p>","",0],["d-b3-flaccid",3,"Flaccid","<p>A plant cell that has lost water and is soft, no longer pressing against its wall (not fully plasmolysed).</p>","<p>Flaccid cells → plant wilts.</p>",0],["d-b3-turgid",3,"Turgid","<p>A plant cell that is swollen and firm, with the cytoplasm pressed against the cell wall by turgor pressure.</p>","<p>Turgid cells support young stems and leaves.</p>",0],["d-b4-complementary-base-pairing",4,"Complementary base pairing<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The way the bases on the two strands of DNA always pair: A with T, and C with G.</p>","<p>So if one strand reads A T T C G A, the other must read <strong>T A A G C T</strong>. Bonds between the paired bases hold the two strands together.</p>",1],["d-b5-catalyst",5,"Catalyst","<p>A substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction and is not changed by the reaction.</p>","<p>Because it is unchanged, it can be used again and again.</p>",0],["d-b5-enzymes",5,"Enzymes","<p>Proteins that are involved in all metabolic reactions, where they function as biological catalysts.</p>","<p><strong>Importance</strong>: without enzymes, reactions at body temperature would be far too slow to sustain life — enzymes make them fast enough. They work in breakdown reactions (digestion: amylase, protease, lipase; catalase) and building-up reactions (making proteins, starch, DNA). Names usually end in <strong>-ase</strong>, named after the substrate (maltose → maltase).</p>",0],["d-b5-enzyme-substrate-complex",5,"Enzyme–substrate complex<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The short-lived structure formed when the substrate binds temporarily to the active site of the enzyme.</p>","<p>The reaction takes place while the complex exists; then the products leave the active site.</p>",1],["d-b5-denaturation",5,"Denaturation","<p>A permanent change in the shape of an enzyme's active site (caused by high temperature or extreme pH) so it is no longer complementary to the substrate and the enzyme can no longer catalyse the reaction.</p>","<p>Most human enzymes are denatured above about <strong>50–60 °C</strong>; at a pH far from their optimum. <em>Never say the enzyme is 'killed' — enzymes are not alive.</em></p>",0],["d-b5-active-site",5,"Active site","<p>The part of the enzyme molecule (a dent/groove) to which the substrate temporarily binds; its shape is complementary to the substrate.</p>","",0],["d-b5-substrate-product",5,"Substrate / product","<p>Substrate: the substance the enzyme acts on.</p><p>Product: the new substance formed by the reaction.</p>","",0],["d-b5-optimum",5,"Optimum","<p>The temperature or pH at which the enzyme's activity is greatest (fastest rate).</p>","<p>Human enzymes ≈ 37 °C; pepsin pH 2; salivary amylase ≈ pH 7; trypsin and pancreatic lipase ≈ pH 8; hot-spring bacteria enzymes up to 80 °C.</p>",0],["d-b5-effective-collision",5,"Effective collision","<p>A collision between substrate and enzyme in which the substrate enters the active site and reacts.</p>","",0],["d-b6-photosynthesis-and-its-equatio",6,"Photosynthesis and its equation","<p>The process by which plants synthesise carbohydrates from raw materials using energy from light.</p><p>Word equation: carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen (in the presence of light and chlorophyll)</p><p>Balanced equation: 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>","<p>(light, chlorophyll written over the arrow).<br />\n<strong>Chlorophyll</strong> is a <strong>green pigment found in chloroplasts</strong>; it <strong>transfers energy from light into energy in chemicals, for the synthesis of carbohydrates</strong>. Raw materials: CO₂ (diffuses in through stomata) and water (from roots via xylem). Products: glucose (used/stored) and oxygen (diffuses out as a by-product, or used in respiration).</p>",0],["d-b6-vascular-bundles",6,"Vascular bundles","<p>Collections of xylem vessels and phloem tubes running side by side, forming the veins of a leaf.</p>","<p>(and found in the stem). <strong>Xylem</strong> brings water and mineral ions <strong>to</strong> the leaf; <strong>phloem</strong> carries sucrose and amino acids <strong>away</strong> from the leaf. The branching network of veins also <strong>supports</strong> the leaf (holds the thin lamina out flat). In a leaf, xylem is on the upper side of the bundle, phloem below.</p>",0],["d-b6-limiting-factors",6,"Limiting factors<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A factor that is in short supply and so stops an activity (photosynthesis) happening at a faster rate.</p>","<p>Whichever factor is <strong>nearest its minimum</strong> limits the rate. You recognise a limiting factor because <strong>if you give more of it, the rate increases</strong>. Limiting factors for photosynthesis: <strong>light intensity, CO₂ concentration, temperature</strong> (also stomata closing on hot/very bright days, which cuts off CO₂). On a graph: where the line is <strong>rising</strong>, the factor on the x-axis is limiting; where it has <strong>levelled off (plateau)</strong>, something else is limiting.</p>",1],["d-b6-destarching",6,"Destarching","<p>Leaving a plant in the dark for 48 hours so it uses up its stored starch.</p>","<p>Makes sure any starch found at the end was made <strong>during</strong> the experiment.</p>",0],["d-b6-control-experimental",6,"Control (experimental)","<p>A comparison set-up identical except for the factor being tested (e.g. a leaf given CO₂ vs one with CO₂ removed), so you can be sure the result is caused by that factor.</p>","",0],["d-b7-emulsification",7,"Emulsification<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Bile breaks large drops of fat/oil into many tiny droplets.</p><p>This increases the surface area of fat for lipase to act on.</p>","<p>The tiny droplets spread out (disperse) in the watery contents of the small intestine, forming an emulsion, so lipase can digest the fat faster. It is <strong>physical digestion</strong> — the fat molecules are not chemically changed.</p>",1],["d-b7-balanced-diet",7,"Balanced diet","<p>A diet that contains all the nutrients (carbohydrates, fats/oils, proteins, vitamins, mineral ions, fibre and water) in the correct proportions, and the right amount of energy, to maintain good health.</p>","<p>Energy needs depend on <strong>age, sex, activity level</strong> and pregnancy/breast-feeding.</p>",0],["d-b7-the-digestive-process-5-stages",7,"The digestive process (5 stages)","<p><strong>Ingestion</strong> — the taking of substances (food and drink) into the body (through the mouth).</p><p><strong>Digestion</strong> — the breakdown of food (physical and chemical).</p><p><strong>Absorption</strong> — the movement of nutrients from the intestines into the blood.</p><p><strong>Assimilation</strong> — the uptake and use of nutrients by cells.</p><p><strong>Egestion</strong> — the removal of undigested food from the body as faeces.</p>","",0],["d-b7-bile",7,"Bile","<p>An alkaline, greenish liquid made in the liver, stored in the gall bladder and released along the bile duct into the duodenum.</p>","<p>Contains <strong>bile salts</strong> (no enzymes). Functions: (1) <strong>emulsifies fats</strong>; (2) <strong>neutralises the acidic mixture of food and gastric juice entering the duodenum from the stomach, giving a suitable (alkaline) pH for the enzymes in the small intestine</strong><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>. Also carries bile pigments (excretion of broken-down haemoglobin).</p>",0],["d-b7-hepatic-portal-vein-function",7,"Hepatic portal vein — function","<p>The vein that carries blood from the small intestine (villi capillaries) to the liver.</p>","<p>It brings absorbed <strong>glucose, amino acids, vitamins, mineral ions and water</strong> to the liver, which <strong>assimilates</strong> them (stores glucose as glycogen, uses amino acids to make plasma proteins, <strong>deaminates</strong> excess amino acids) before the blood goes on round the body.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",0],["d-b7-lacteals",7,"Lacteals","<p>Small lymph vessels in the centre of each villus that absorb fatty acids and glycerol (fats).</p>","<p>Their contents (lymph) are eventually emptied into the blood.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",0],["d-b7-physical-digestion",7,"Physical digestion","<p>The breakdown of food into smaller pieces without chemical change to the food molecules.</p>","<p>Done by teeth (chewing), the stomach (churning) and bile (emulsifying). It <strong>increases the surface area of food for the action of enzymes</strong> in chemical digestion.</p>",0],["d-b7-chemical-digestion",7,"Chemical digestion","<p>The breakdown of large insoluble molecules into small soluble molecules.</p>","<p>(by enzymes). Its role: to <strong>produce small soluble molecules that can be absorbed</strong> into the blood.</p>",0],["d-b8-transpiration",8,"Transpiration","<p>The loss of water vapour from leaves.</p><p>Water evaporates from the surfaces of the mesophyll cells into the air spaces and then diffuses out of the leaves through the stomata as water vapour.</p>","<p>Uses of transpiration: pulls water and mineral ions up the plant; cools the leaf (evaporation); keeps cells turgid (supply of water). Most transpiration happens through stomata on the lower surface.</p>",0],["d-b8-translocation",8,"Translocation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The movement of sucrose and amino acids in the phloem from sources to sinks.</p>","<p>It can occur <strong>up or down</strong> the plant. Uses energy (phloem cells are living).</p>",1],["d-b8-xylem",8,"Xylem","<p>Transports water and mineral ions (from roots → stem → leaves) and provides support to the plant</p>","<p>Made of dead cells.</p>",0],["d-b8-phloem",8,"Phloem","<p>Transports sucrose and amino acids (from where they are made/stored to where they are needed).</p>","<p>Made of living cells (sieve tubes).</p>",0],["d-b8-source-and-sink",8,"Source and sink<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Source = a part of a plant that releases sucrose or amino acids.</p><p>Sink = a part of the plant that uses or stores sucrose or amino acids.</p>","<p>Sources: e.g. photosynthesising leaves, or a storage organ when its stores are being used. Sinks: e.g. growing roots and shoot tips, developing fruits and seeds, storage organs being filled.</p>",1],["d-b9-circulatory-system",9,"Circulatory system","<p>A system of blood vessels with a pump (the heart) and valves to ensure one-way flow of blood.</p>","<p>It transports oxygen, nutrients, CO₂, urea, hormones, antibodies and heat around the body.</p>",0],["d-b9-coronary-artery",9,"Coronary artery","<p>Arteries that branch off the aorta and run over the surface of the heart, supplying the heart (cardiac) muscle with oxygenated blood.</p>","<p>(oxygen and glucose for respiration). The heart cannot use the blood inside its chambers — it is moving too fast and the walls are too thick.</p>",0],["d-b9-coronary-heart-disease-chd",9,"Coronary heart disease (CHD)","<p>Blockage of the coronary arteries.</p>","<p>(by fatty/cholesterol deposits — atheroma — and blood clots), so the heart muscle beyond the blockage gets <strong>no oxygen</strong>, cannot respire aerobically and <strong>dies</strong> → heart attack. <strong>Possible risk factors:</strong> <strong>diet</strong> (high in saturated (animal) fat/cholesterol, salt), <strong>lack of exercise</strong>, <strong>stress</strong>, <strong>smoking</strong>, <strong>genetic predisposition</strong> (family history), <strong>age</strong> (risk rises), <strong>sex</strong> (males more at risk); also obesity, high blood pressure.<br />\n<strong>Roles of diet and exercise in reducing risk</strong>: eat less saturated/animal fat and cholesterol (use plant oils), less salt, more fibre/fruit/vegetables, avoid obesity → fewer deposits in arteries, lower blood pressure. Regular exercise strengthens heart muscle, lowers blood pressure and cholesterol, helps keep weight down, reduces stress.</p>",0],["d-b9-pulse-rate",9,"Pulse rate","<p>The number of times an artery expands and recoils in one minute.</p>","<p>= <strong>a measure of heart rate</strong> (beats per minute). Felt where an artery is near the skin — wrist (radial artery) or neck (carotid artery). Resting ≈ 60–80 bpm in adults; lower in fitter people.</p>",0],["d-b9-electrocardiograph-ecg",9,"Electrocardiograph (ECG)","<p>A record (graph) of the electrical activity of the heart against time.</p>","<p>Made using electrodes stuck on the skin of the chest, arms and legs. Each repeating pattern (P, QRS, T waves) = one heartbeat; heart rate = 60 ÷ time between two beats (s). Used to diagnose heart problems. <strong>Heart activity can be monitored by: ECG, pulse rate, and listening to the sounds of valves closing</strong> (stethoscope — \"lub-dub\").</p>",0],["d-b9-phagocytosis",9,"Phagocytosis<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The process by which phagocytes engulf pathogens (bacteria) by flowing around them, taking them into a vacuole in the cytoplasm, and digesting them with enzymes.</p>","<p>Phagocytes have a <strong>lobed nucleus</strong> and can squeeze out of capillaries to reach infected tissue.</p>",1],["d-b10-pathogen",10,"Pathogen","<p>A disease-causing organism.</p>","<p>(microorganism). Types: <strong>viruses</strong> (flu, cold, measles, polio, HIV/AIDS, COVID-19), <strong>bacteria</strong> (cholera, TB, tetanus, whooping cough), <strong>protoctists</strong> (malaria, amoebic dysentery), <strong>fungi</strong> (athlete's foot, ringworm).</p>",0],["d-b10-transmissible-diseases",10,"Transmissible diseases","<p>A disease in which the pathogen can be passed from one host to another.</p>","<p>(Non-transmissible diseases — e.g. CHD, scurvy, diabetes — cannot be passed on.) <strong>Host</strong> = the organism in which a pathogen lives and reproduces.</p>",0],["d-b10-infection",10,"Infection","<p>The entry of a pathogen into the body of a host.</p>","<p>(and its reproduction there).</p>",0],["d-b10-memory-cells",10,"Memory cells","<p>Long-lived lymphocytes produced during an immune response that remain in the blood after the infection.</p>","<p>On a second contact with the <strong>same antigen</strong> they <strong>divide quickly and produce large amounts of the specific antibody</strong> before the pathogen can cause illness → <strong>long-term immunity</strong>. <strong>Memory cells are not produced in passive immunity</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b10-vaccine",10,"Vaccine","<p>A harmless preparation of weakened (attenuated) or dead pathogens, or just their antigens (or mRNA coding for them), injected or swallowed to stimulate an immune response without causing the disease.</p>","<p><strong>Process of vaccination</strong><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: (a) <strong>weakened pathogens or their antigens are put into the body</strong>; (b) <strong>the antigens stimulate an immune response by lymphocytes which produce antibodies</strong>; (c) <strong>memory cells are produced that give long-term immunity</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b10-herd-immunity",10,"Herd immunity","<p>When a large proportion of a population is vaccinated, the pathogen cannot spread, so the unvaccinated are protected too.</p>","<p>When a <strong>large proportion of a population is vaccinated</strong> (often &gt; 90%), the pathogen has very few people to infect and reproduce in, so it <strong>cannot spread</strong> — this <strong>protects the unvaccinated</strong> too (babies, people who cannot be vaccinated). <strong>Role of vaccination in controlling spread</strong><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: fewer people can catch and pass on the disease → outbreaks/epidemics prevented; mass vaccination eradicated <strong>smallpox</strong> and has nearly eradicated polio; measles cases fell sharply after vaccination began.</p>",0],["d-b10-active-immunity",10,"Active immunity<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Defence against a pathogen by antibody production in the body.</p><p>Gained after an infection by a pathogen or by vaccination.</p>","<p><strong>Long-term</strong> because <strong>memory cells</strong> are made.</p>",1],["d-b10-passive-immunity",10,"Passive immunity<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A short-term defence against a pathogen by antibodies acquired from another individual, including across the placenta and in breast milk.</p>","<p>(or by injection of antibodies, e.g. anti-tetanus or anti-venom). Short-term because the antibodies are broken down and <strong>no memory cells are made</strong>.</p>",1],["d-b10-antigens",10,"Antigens<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Each pathogen has its own antigens, which have specific shapes.</p>","<p>Chemicals (usually proteins) on the surface of the pathogen that the body recognises as foreign and that stimulate antibody production.</p>",1],["d-b10-antibodies",10,"Antibodies<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Proteins that bind to antigens, leading to direct destruction of pathogens or marking of pathogens for destruction by phagocytes.</p><p>Specific antibodies have complementary shapes which fit specific antigens.</p>","<p>Ways they work: clump pathogens together (agglutination), burst them, neutralise their toxins, label them so phagocytes find and engulf them.</p>",1],["d-b10-cholera",10,"Cholera<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A disease caused by a bacterium (<em>Vibrio cholerae</em>), transmitted in contaminated water.</p>","<p>(and food) — water polluted with raw sewage. <strong>Mechanism:</strong> the bacterium multiplies in the <strong>small intestine</strong> and produces a <strong>toxin</strong> → the toxin causes <strong>secretion of chloride ions into the small intestine</strong> → this <strong>lowers the water potential</strong> of the gut contents → <strong>water moves by osmosis into the gut</strong> → <strong>diarrhoea</strong>, <strong>dehydration</strong> and <strong>loss of ions from the blood</strong> (can be fatal). <strong>Treatment:</strong> oral rehydration therapy (water + salts + glucose); antibiotics. <strong>Prevention:</strong> clean water, sewage treatment, hygiene, vaccine.</p>",1],["d-b11-aerobic-respiration",11,"Aerobic respiration","<p>The chemical reactions in cells that use oxygen to break down nutrient molecules to release energy.</p><p>Word equation: glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water</p><p>Balanced equation: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>","<p>Happens in the <strong>mitochondria</strong>; releases a <strong>large amount</strong> of energy per glucose molecule.</p>",0],["d-b11-anaerobic-respiration",11,"Anaerobic respiration","<p>The chemical reactions in cells that break down nutrient molecules to release energy without using oxygen.</p><p>Yeast: glucose → alcohol (ethanol) + carbon dioxide</p><p>Yeast (balanced): C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><p>Muscles: glucose → lactic acid</p>","<p>Happens in the <strong>cytoplasm</strong>. <strong>Releases much less energy per glucose molecule than aerobic respiration</strong> (glucose is only partly broken down).<br />\n(no CO₂ made)</p>",0],["d-b11-goblet-cells",11,"Goblet cells<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Cells in the lining (epithelium) of the trachea, bronchi (and alimentary canal) that secrete mucus.</p>","<p>The sticky mucus <strong>traps pathogens (bacteria, viruses) and dust particles</strong> so they don't reach the alveoli.</p>",1],["d-b11-alveoli",11,"Alveoli","<p>Tiny air-filled sacs at the ends of the bronchioles where gas exchange takes place.</p>","<p>O₂ diffuses from the air in the alveolus into the blood in the capillaries; CO₂ diffuses from the blood into the alveolus.</p>",0],["d-b11-ventilation",11,"Ventilation","<p>The movement of air into and out of the lungs by breathing movements.</p>","<p>Keeps a steep concentration gradient of O₂ and CO₂ at the alveoli. <strong>Mechanism</strong><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>:<br />\n<strong>Inspiration (breathing in):</strong> <strong>external intercostal muscles contract</strong> (internal relax) → <strong>ribs move up and out</strong>; <strong>diaphragm muscles contract</strong> → diaphragm <strong>flattens (moves down)</strong> → <strong>volume of the thorax increases</strong> → <strong>pressure inside the thorax/lungs decreases</strong> below atmospheric → <strong>air moves in</strong>.<br />\n<strong>Expiration (breathing out):</strong> <strong>external intercostals relax</strong> → ribs move <strong>down and in</strong> (forced expiration: <strong>internal intercostals contract</strong>); <strong>diaphragm relaxes</strong> → returns to <strong>dome shape</strong> (pushed up by abdominal organs) → <strong>volume of thorax decreases</strong> → <strong>pressure increases</strong> above atmospheric → <strong>air moves out</strong>. The <strong>internal and external intercostal muscles are antagonistic</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b11-breathing-rate",11,"Breathing rate","<p>The number of breaths taken per minute.</p>","<p>(≈ 12–16 at rest in an adult). <strong>Depth of breathing</strong> = volume of air taken in per breath (≈ 0.5 dm³ at rest — tidal volume; up to ≈ 3–4 dm³ in deep exercise breathing).<br />\n<strong>Effect of physical activity</strong><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: muscles respire faster → <strong>more CO₂ is produced</strong> → <strong>CO₂ concentration in the blood increases</strong> → this is <strong>detected by the brain</strong> (breathing centre) → brain sends impulses to the intercostal muscles and diaphragm → <strong>increased rate and greater depth of breathing</strong> → more O₂ supplied, more CO₂ removed. Breathing stays fast and deep after exercise to repay the oxygen debt.</p>",0],["d-b12-coordination",12,"Coordination","<p>Ensuring that the actions of different parts of the body work together.</p>","<p>(in response to stimuli). In animals by the <strong>nervous system</strong> (fast, electrical) and the <strong>endocrine (hormonal) system</strong> (slower, chemical). <strong>Role of the nervous system</strong>: <strong>coordination and regulation of body functions</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b12-reflex-arc",12,"Reflex arc","<p>Stimulus → receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone (in CNS — spinal cord/brain) → motor neurone → effector (muscle or gland) → response</p>","<p>Example: touching a hot object → pain/temperature receptors in skin → sensory neurone → relay neurone in spinal cord → motor neurone → <strong>biceps contracts</strong> → hand pulled away. The impulse does not have to go to the conscious part of the brain first, so it is very fast.</p>",0],["d-b12-nerve-impulse",12,"Nerve impulse","<p>An electrical signal that passes rapidly along a neurone (axon).</p>","<p>Speed up to ≈ 100 m s⁻¹ in myelinated fibres.</p>",0],["d-b12-reflex-action",12,"Reflex action","<p>A means of automatically and rapidly integrating and coordinating stimuli with the responses of effectors (muscles and glands).</p>","<p>Reflexes are <strong>automatic (involuntary)</strong>, <strong>rapid</strong>, and usually <strong>protective</strong> (e.g. withdrawal from pain, blinking, pupil reflex, coughing, knee-jerk).</p>",0],["d-b12-synapse",12,"Synapse","<p>A junction between two neurones.</p>","<p>Consisting of a tiny <strong>synaptic gap</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Structure</strong>: the end of the first neurone contains <strong>vesicles containing neurotransmitter molecules</strong>; then the <strong>synaptic gap</strong>; then the membrane of the next neurone with <strong>receptor proteins</strong>. <strong>Events</strong>: (a) <strong>an impulse stimulates the release of neurotransmitter molecules from vesicles into the synaptic gap</strong>; (b) <strong>the neurotransmitter molecules diffuse across the gap</strong>; (c) <strong>neurotransmitter molecules bind with receptor proteins on the next neurone</strong>; (d) <strong>an impulse is then stimulated in the next neurone</strong>. <strong>Synapses ensure that impulses travel in one direction only</strong> — neurotransmitter is only released on one side and receptors are only on the other.</p>",0],["d-b12-neurotransmitter",12,"Neurotransmitter <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A chemical stored in vesicles at the end of a neurone; released into the synaptic gap when an impulse arrives; diffuses across and binds to receptors on the next neurone, starting an impulse there.</p>","<p>Then quickly broken down/removed so the signal stops.</p>",1],["d-b12-receptor-protein",12,"Receptor protein <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Protein molecules in the membrane of the neurone after the synapse, with a shape complementary to the neurotransmitter molecule, so the neurotransmitter can bind to them.</p>","<p>(DNA codes for these receptor proteins — Ch 16.) Many drugs act by binding to these receptors.</p>",1],["d-b12-blind-spot",12,"Blind spot","<p>The part of the retina where the optic nerve leaves the eye; it has no receptor cells (no rods or cones), so light falling on it is not detected.</p>","",0],["d-b12-fovea",12,"Fovea<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A small region of the retina directly opposite the lens, where cones are packed most closely (no rods).</p>","<p>Light is focused here when we look directly at an object; gives the <strong>sharpest, most detailed colour vision</strong> (in bright light).</p>",1],["d-b12-iris-reflex-pupil-reflex",12,"Iris reflex / pupil reflex","<p>An automatic change in pupil diameter in response to light intensity.</p>","<p>Protecting the retina from damage by bright light and letting in more light when dim. Receptor: rods/cones in retina; effector: muscles of the iris.<br />\n<strong>Bright light:</strong> <strong>circular muscles contract, radial muscles relax</strong> → <strong>pupil constricts (smaller)</strong> → less light enters.<br />\n<strong>Dim light:</strong> <strong>radial muscles contract, circular muscles relax</strong> → <strong>pupil dilates (larger)</strong> → more light enters.</p>",0],["d-b12-antagonistic-muscles",12,"Antagonistic muscles <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A pair of muscles whose contractions have opposite effects — when one contracts, the other relaxes.</p>","<p>In the iris: <strong>circular and radial muscles</strong>. Other examples: biceps/triceps; internal/external intercostals.</p>",1],["d-b12-accommodation",12,"Accommodation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Changing the shape of the lens to focus on objects at different distances.</p>","<p><strong>Near object:</strong> <strong>ciliary muscles contract</strong> → <strong>suspensory ligaments slacken (lose tension)</strong> → lens becomes <strong>fatter / more convex</strong> → <strong>refracts light more strongly</strong> → image focused on retina.<br />\n<strong>Distant object:</strong> <strong>ciliary muscles relax</strong> → <strong>suspensory ligaments pulled tight (tension)</strong> → lens pulled <strong>thinner / less convex</strong> → <strong>refracts light less</strong>.</p>",1],["d-b12-refraction",12,"Refraction","<p>The bending of light rays.</p>","<p>As they pass from one medium to another. In the eye, most refraction happens at the <strong>cornea</strong>; the <strong>lens</strong> does the fine adjustment (focusing).</p>",0],["d-b12-endocrine-glands",12,"Endocrine glands","<p>Glands that secrete hormones directly into the blood.</p>","<p>(ductless). <strong>Adrenal glands → adrenaline</strong>; <strong>pancreas → insulin</strong> (and <strong>glucagon</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>); <strong>testes → testosterone</strong>; <strong>ovaries → oestrogen</strong> (and progesterone). Also pituitary gland (FSH, LH, ADH).</p>",0],["d-b12-target-organs",12,"Target organs","<p>Organs whose activity is altered by a hormone.</p>","<p>Their cells have receptors for that hormone. e.g. insulin → liver and muscle; adrenaline → heart, liver, eye, lungs; FSH → ovaries.</p>",0],["d-b12-tropism",12,"Tropism","<p>A growth response by a plant in which the direction of growth is related to the direction of the stimulus.</p>","<p>(towards = positive, away = negative). Plants respond to stimuli by growth, slowly.</p>",0],["d-b12-sense-organs",12,"Sense organs","<p>Groups of receptor cells responding to specific stimuli: light (eye), sound (ear), touch (skin), temperature (skin), chemicals (nose, tongue).</p>","<p>Receptors convert the stimulus into electrical impulses.</p>",0],["d-b12-hormone",12,"Hormone","<p>A chemical substance, produced by a gland and carried by the blood, which alters the activity of one or more specific target organs.</p>","",0],["d-b12-gravitropism-phototropism",12,"Gravitropism / phototropism","<p>Gravitropism — a response in which parts of a plant grow towards or away from gravity.</p><p>Phototropism — a response in which parts of a plant grow towards or away from the direction of the light source.</p>","<p>(roots positive, shoots negative). (shoots positive, roots negative). Both are examples of the <strong>chemical control of plant growth</strong> by auxin<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>.</p>",0],["d-b13-urine",13,"Urine","<p>A solution of urea, excess water and excess ions (and other wastes) in water, produced by the kidneys.</p>","<p>Stored in the <strong>bladder</strong>, released through the <strong>urethra</strong>. Its volume and concentration depend on how much water the body has (hydration).</p>",0],["d-b13-excretion",13,"Excretion","<p>The removal of the waste products of metabolism and substances in excess of requirements.</p>","<p><strong>CO₂ is excreted through the lungs</strong>; <strong>the kidneys excrete urea and excess water and ions</strong> (skin also loses some urea, salts and water in sweat).</p>",0],["d-b13-deamination",13,"Deamination<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The removal of the nitrogen-containing part of amino acids to form urea.</p>","<p>Happens in the <strong>liver</strong> to <strong>excess amino acids</strong> (they cannot be stored). The rest of the molecule is respired for energy or converted to carbohydrate/fat.</p>",1],["d-b13-nephron",13,"Nephron<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>One of about a million microscopic tubules in each kidney where urine is made.</p>","<p>Parts: <strong>renal (Bowman's) capsule</strong> surrounding the <strong>glomerulus</strong> (in the <strong>cortex</strong>), the <strong>tubule</strong> (loops into the <strong>medulla</strong>), and the <strong>collecting duct</strong>, with associated blood vessels (arteriole in → glomerulus → arteriole out → capillaries around the tubule → renal vein).</p>",1],["d-b13-glomeruli",13,"Glomeruli","<p>Knots of blood capillaries inside each renal capsule where blood is filtered.</p>","<p>Blood arrives under <strong>high pressure</strong>, which forces <strong>water, glucose, urea and ions</strong> (small molecules) out of the blood into the capsule — the <strong>filtrate</strong>. <strong>Blood cells and proteins are too large</strong> and stay in the blood.</p>",0],["d-b13-reabsorption",13,"Reabsorption <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Taking back useful substances from the filtrate into the blood.</p>","<p>In the kidney tubule: <strong>all glucose</strong>, <strong>most water</strong>, <strong>some ions</strong> (by diffusion, active transport and osmosis). If glucose appears in urine, blood glucose was too high for all of it to be reabsorbed (a sign of diabetes).</p>",1],["d-b13-homeostasis",13,"Homeostasis","<p>The maintenance of a constant internal environment.</p>","<p>Internal environment = the blood and tissue fluid around cells. Factors kept constant: <strong>body temperature</strong>, <strong>blood glucose concentration</strong>, <strong>water content (water potential)</strong> of blood, CO₂ concentration/pH. Cells (and their enzymes) work best in constant conditions.</p>",0],["d-b13-set-point",13,"Set point <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The normal value (or range) around which a factor is kept.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>37 °C</strong> body temperature; blood glucose ≈ <strong>90 mg per 100 cm³</strong> (≈ 5 mmol dm⁻³).</p>",1],["d-b13-negative-feedback",13,"Negative feedback<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A control mechanism in which a change away from the set point is detected and triggers responses that reverse the change, bringing the value back towards the set point.</p>","<p>Needs: a <strong>receptor/sensor</strong> that detects the change, a <strong>coordinator</strong> (brain/pancreas), and <strong>effectors</strong> that make the correction. e.g. temperature rises → sweating and vasodilation → temperature falls → responses switched off.</p>",1],["d-b13-vasoconstriction",13,"Vasoconstriction<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Narrowing of the arterioles supplying the skin surface capillaries.</p>","<p>(their muscle walls <strong>contract</strong>) → <strong>less blood flows</strong> through skin capillaries → <strong>less heat lost</strong> by radiation from the skin (skin looks pale). Happens when cold.</p>",1],["d-b13-vasodilation",13,"Vasodilation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Widening of the arterioles supplying the skin surface capillaries.</p>","<p>(their muscle walls <strong>relax</strong>) → <strong>more blood flows</strong> through skin capillaries near the surface → <strong>more heat lost</strong> by radiation (skin looks red/flushed). Happens when hot. <em>Arterioles widen/narrow — capillaries do not move up or down.</em></p>",1],["d-b14-asexual-reproduction",14,"Asexual reproduction","<p>A process resulting in the production of genetically identical offspring from one parent.</p>","<p>No gametes, no fertilisation; cells divide by <strong>mitosis</strong>. Offspring = a <strong>clone</strong>. Examples: bacteria (binary fission), fungi (spores, e.g. <em>Mucor</em>), yeast (budding), <em>Hydra</em> (budding), potato (stem tubers), onion/daffodil (bulbs), strawberry (runners), ginger (rhizomes), cuttings and tissue culture.</p>",0],["d-b14-sexual-reproduction",14,"Sexual reproduction","<p>A process involving the fusion of the nuclei of two gametes to form a zygote and the production of offspring that are genetically different from each other.</p>","<p>Gametes are made by <strong>meiosis</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b14-gametes",14,"Gametes","<p>Sex cells with half the normal number of chromosomes (haploid).</p>","<p>Whose nucleus fuses with another gamete's nucleus at fertilisation. Plants: <strong>pollen nucleus</strong> (male, inside pollen grain) and <strong>egg/ovule nucleus</strong> (female, inside ovule). Animals: sperm and egg.</p>",0],["d-b14-fertilisation",14,"Fertilisation","<p>The fusion of the nuclei of gametes.</p>","<p>In flowering plants, <strong>fertilisation occurs when a pollen nucleus fuses with a nucleus in an ovule</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b14-zygote",14,"Zygote","<p>The cell formed by fertilisation.</p>","<p>(fusion of two gamete nuclei). <strong>Diploid</strong>. Divides by mitosis to form the <strong>embryo</strong>. In plants the zygote is inside the ovule, which becomes the <strong>seed</strong>; the ovary becomes the <strong>fruit</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b14-pollination",14,"Pollination","<p>The transfer of pollen grains from an anther to a stigma.</p><p>Self-pollination: transfer of pollen from the anther of a flower to the stigma of the same flower or a different flower on the same plant.</p><p>Cross-pollination: transfer of pollen from the anther of a flower to the stigma of a flower on a different plant of the same species.</p>","<p>By <strong>insects</strong> (or other animals) or <strong>wind</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b14-haploid-and-diploid",14,"Haploid and diploid<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Nuclei of gametes are haploid.</p><p>The nucleus of a zygote is diploid.</p>","<p>Haploid = one set of chromosomes (<em>n</em>); diploid = two sets (2<em>n</em>). Fertilisation restores the diploid number.</p>",1],["d-b15-ovulation",15,"Ovulation","<p>The release of an egg from an ovary.</p>","<p>(from a mature <strong>follicle</strong>), about <strong>day 14</strong> of a 28-day menstrual cycle; triggered by <strong>LH</strong>. The egg enters the <strong>oviduct</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b15-acrosome",15,"Acrosome","<p>A structure (a sac/vesicle) in the tip of the sperm head containing digestive enzymes.</p>","<p>When the sperm reaches the egg, the acrosome releases enzymes that <strong>digest the jelly coat</strong> (and cells around the egg) so the sperm head can <strong>penetrate</strong> the egg.</p>",0],["d-b15-fertilisation",15,"Fertilisation","<p>The fusion of the nuclei from a male gamete (sperm) and a female gamete (egg cell).</p>","<p>In the <strong>oviduct</strong>. Once one sperm enters, the <strong>jelly coat changes</strong> to stop any more sperm entering. The result is a <strong>zygote</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b15-embryo",15,"Embryo","<p>In early development, the zygote forms an embryo, which is a ball of cells that implants into the lining of the uterus.</p>","<p>(From fertilisation until about 8 weeks, when organs have formed.)</p>",0],["d-b15-fetus",15,"Fetus","<p>An unborn mammal in which all the organs have formed.</p>","<p>From about <strong>8 weeks</strong> after fertilisation until birth (≈ 40 weeks/9 months of gestation in total).</p>",0],["d-b15-umbilical-cord",15,"Umbilical cord","<p>Cord containing blood vessels (umbilical artery and vein) that connect the fetus to the placenta.</p>","<p>The <strong>umbilical artery</strong> carries <strong>deoxygenated blood with CO₂ and urea</strong> from the fetus to the placenta; the <strong>umbilical vein</strong> carries <strong>oxygenated blood with nutrients</strong> (glucose, amino acids) back to the fetus.</p>",0],["d-b15-placenta",15,"Placenta","<p>An organ that connects the growing fetus to its mother.</p>","<p>Attached to the uterus wall; the <strong>blood of the fetus and mother are brought very close together but do not mix</strong>. <strong>Function</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>: <strong>exchange of dissolved nutrients (glucose, amino acids), gases (O₂ to fetus, CO₂ to mother) and excretory products (urea to mother)</strong> between mother's and fetus's blood, by diffusion across a thin barrier with a large surface area (villi). It also acts as a <strong>barrier</strong> to many pathogens and toxins, and makes hormones (<strong>progesterone and oestrogen</strong>) in pregnancy. However, <strong>some pathogens and toxins can pass across the placenta and affect the fetus</strong>: e.g. <strong>viruses (rubella, HIV, Zika)</strong>, <strong>nicotine, carbon monoxide</strong> (smoking), <strong>alcohol</strong>, some <strong>drugs</strong> (e.g. heroin, some medicines).</p>",0],["d-b15-puberty",15,"Puberty","<p>The time when the sex organs mature and sexual maturity is reached.</p>","<p>(about 10–14 years in girls, 12–16 in boys) — the ovaries/testes start producing gametes and secreting sex hormones (<strong>oestrogen/testosterone</strong>), which cause <strong>secondary sexual characteristics</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b15-menstruation",15,"Menstruation","<p>The loss of the broken-down uterus lining (blood and cells) through the vagina.</p>","<p>Days <strong>1–5</strong> of the cycle. The lining then thickens again (days 5–14); <strong>ovulation ≈ day 14</strong>; the empty follicle becomes the <strong>corpus luteum</strong>; if no fertilisation, the corpus luteum breaks down, progesterone falls and the lining breaks down → menstruation again ≈ day 28.</p>",0],["d-b15-corpus-luteum",15,"Corpus luteum","<p>'Yellow body' — the structure that develops from the empty follicle in the ovary after ovulation.</p>","<p>It <strong>secretes progesterone</strong> (and some oestrogen), which maintains the uterus lining. If pregnancy occurs it persists (until the placenta takes over); if not, it breaks down after ≈ 10–14 days.</p>",0],["d-b15-sexually-transmitted-infection",15,"Sexually transmitted infection (STI)","<p>An infection that is transmitted through sexual contact.</p>","<p>(via body fluids). Examples: <strong>HIV</strong> (a virus — <strong>a pathogen that causes an STI</strong>), gonorrhoea, syphilis, chlamydia (bacteria).</p>",0],["d-b16-chromosomes",16,"Chromosomes","<p>Thread-like structures in the nucleus, made of DNA, which contains genetic information in the form of genes.</p>","<p>(Also protein.) Visible only when a cell divides (short and thick, made of two identical chromatids before division). Humans: <strong>46 chromosomes = 23 pairs</strong> in body cells; one of each pair from each parent.</p>",0],["d-b16-gene",16,"Gene","<p>A length of DNA that codes for a protein.</p>","<p>Each gene has a fixed position (locus) on a chromosome.</p>",0],["d-b16-allele",16,"Allele","<p>An alternative form of a gene.</p>","<p>e.g. the gene for pea height has alleles <strong>T</strong> (tall) and <strong>t</strong> (dwarf). A diploid cell has two alleles of each gene (one on each chromosome of a pair).</p>",0],["d-b16-reduction-division-meiosis",16,"Reduction division (meiosis)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Meiosis is a reduction division in which the chromosome number is halved from diploid to haploid, resulting in genetically different cells.</p>","<p>(4 haploid nuclei from 1 diploid). <strong>Meiosis is involved in the production of gametes</strong>. (Details of the stages are not required.)</p>",1],["d-b16-codominance",16,"Codominance<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A situation in which both alleles in a heterozygous organism contribute to the phenotype.</p>","<p>(neither is dominant). Written with a capital letter for the gene and superscripts for alleles, e.g. <strong>Iᴬ Iᴮ</strong> → blood group <strong>AB</strong>; flower colour <strong>Cᴿ Cᵂ</strong>. Heterozygotes have a third, distinct phenotype.</p>",1],["d-b16-inheritance",16,"Inheritance","<p>The transmission of genetic information from generation to generation.</p>","",0],["d-b16-monohybrid-crosses",16,"Monohybrid crosses","<p>A genetic cross in which we follow the inheritance of only one gene (one characteristic).</p>","<p>Typical ratios: <strong>3 : 1</strong> (Aa × Aa) and <strong>1 : 1</strong> (Aa × aa).</p>",0],["d-b16-genetic-diagram",16,"Genetic diagram","<p>A standard way of showing all the steps in predicting the genotypes and phenotypes of offspring from two parents.</p>","<p>(1) state the symbols (key); (2) parental phenotypes; (3) parental genotypes; (4) <strong>gametes</strong> (circled); (5) offspring genotypes (Punnett square); (6) offspring phenotypes and <strong>ratio</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b16-punnett-square",16,"Punnett square","<p>The grid part of a genetic diagram showing the possible combinations of gametes at fertilisation.</p>","<p>Gametes of one parent across the top, the other down the side; fill each box. It shows all possible offspring genotypes and their probabilities.</p>",0],["d-b16-pure-breeding-homozygous",16,"Pure breeding = homozygous","<p>Two identical homozygous individuals that breed together will be pure-breeding.</p><p>A heterozygous individual will not be pure-breeding.</p>","<p>Always producing offspring like themselves.</p>",0],["d-b16-sex-chromosomes",16,"Sex chromosomes","<p>The pair of chromosomes that determines sex: females XX, males XY.</p>","<p>Inheritance of sex: mother's eggs all carry <strong>X</strong>; father's sperm carry <strong>X or Y</strong> (50:50) → <strong>50% chance</strong> of boy (XY) or girl (XX) each time. The other 22 pairs are autosomes.</p>",0],["d-b16-sex-linkage",16,"Sex linkage<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A sex-linked characteristic is a feature in which the gene responsible is located on a sex chromosome (usually the X), which makes the characteristic more common in one sex than in the other.</p>","<p>Males have only one X, so a single recessive allele is always expressed in a male (no second X to mask it).</p>",1],["d-b16-sex-linked-genes",16,"Sex-linked genes","<p>Genes found on a part of one sex chromosome (usually X) that has no matching part on the other.</p>","<p>(non-homologous region). Example: <strong>red-green colour blindness</strong> — alleles <strong>Xᴮ</strong> (normal) and <strong>Xᵇ</strong> (colour blind). Females: XᴮXᴮ normal, <strong>XᴮXᵇ carrier</strong> (normal vision), XᵇXᵇ colour blind. Males: XᴮY normal, <strong>XᵇY colour blind</strong>. Males cannot be carriers. Haemophilia is another example.</p>",0],["d-b16-messenger-rna-mrna",16,"Messenger RNA (mRNA)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A copy of a gene made in the nucleus; a single-stranded molecule that carries the code from the DNA in the nucleus out to the ribosomes.</p>","<p>In the cytoplasm, where it is used to make a protein.</p>",1],["d-b16-stem-cells",16,"Stem cells<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Unspecialised cells that divide by mitosis to produce daughter cells that can become specialised for specific functions.</p>","<p>e.g. embryonic stem cells (can become any cell type), adult stem cells in <strong>bone marrow</strong> (→ blood cells), skin.</p>",1],["d-b16-haploid-diploid-nucleus",16,"Haploid / diploid nucleus<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Haploid nucleus — contains a single set of chromosomes.</p><p>Diploid nucleus — contains two sets of chromosomes.</p>","<p>Human gametes have 23 chromosomes (haploid). In a diploid cell there is a <strong>pair of each type</strong> of chromosome; <strong>human body cells: 23 pairs = 46</strong>.</p>",1],["d-b16-mitosis",16,"Mitosis<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Nuclear division giving rise to genetically identical cells.</p>","<p><strong>Exact replication (copying) of the chromosomes occurs before mitosis</strong>; <strong>during mitosis the copies of chromosomes separate, maintaining the chromosome number in each daughter cell</strong>. <strong>Roles:</strong> <strong>growth</strong>, <strong>repair of damaged tissues</strong>, <strong>replacement of cells</strong> (worn-out skin, blood), <strong>asexual reproduction</strong>. (Stages not required.)</p>",1],["d-b16-genotype-phenotype",16,"Genotype / phenotype","<p>Genotype — the genetic make-up of an organism in terms of the alleles present.</p><p>Phenotype — the observable features of an organism.</p>","<p>e.g. genotype <strong>Tt</strong> → phenotype <strong>tall</strong>. The phenotype is the result of the genotype <strong>and</strong> the environment.</p>",0],["d-b16-homozygous-heterozygous",16,"Homozygous / heterozygous","<p>Homozygous — having two identical alleles of a particular gene.</p><p>Heterozygous — having two different alleles of a particular gene.</p>","<p>Homozygous: <strong>TT</strong> or <strong>tt</strong>. Heterozygous: <strong>Tt</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b16-dominant-recessive",16,"Dominant / recessive","<p>Dominant allele — an allele that is expressed if it is present in the genotype.</p><p>Recessive allele — an allele that is only expressed when there is no dominant allele of the gene present in the genotype.</p>","<p>Dominant allele = <strong>capital</strong> letter (T); recessive = <strong>small</strong> letter (t) — a recessive characteristic only shows when the genotype is homozygous recessive (tt).</p>",0],["d-b17-variation",17,"Variation","<p>Differences between individuals of the same species.</p>","<p>Two types: <strong>continuous</strong> and <strong>discontinuous</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b17-population",17,"Population","<p>A group of organisms of one species, living in the same area, at the same time.</p>","<p>Variation exists within every population.</p>",0],["d-b17-adaptive-feature",17,"Adaptive feature","<p>An inherited feature that helps an organism to survive and reproduce in its environment.</p>","<p>You must be able to interpret images of a species and describe its adaptive features — state the feature <strong>and</strong> how it helps (e.g. \"thick white fur — insulation and camouflage in snow\").</p>",0],["d-b17-xerophytes",17,"Xerophytes<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Plants with adaptive features that help them to survive in an environment where water is scarce.</p>","<p>(deserts, dry/windy places). e.g. cacti, <em>Sempervivum</em>, oleander, marram grass, pine.</p>",1],["d-b17-hydrophytes",17,"Hydrophytes<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Plants with adaptive features that help them to survive in water.</p>","<p>(floating or submerged). e.g. water hyacinth (<em>Eichhornia crassipes</em>), water lily, <em>Cabomba</em>.</p>",1],["d-b17-adaptation",17,"Adaptation<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The process, resulting from natural selection, by which populations become more suited to their environment over many generations.</p>","<p>(Different from 'adaptive feature', which is the feature itself.)</p>",1],["d-b17-drug",17,"Drug","<p>Any substance taken into the body that modifies or affects chemical reactions in the body.</p>","<p>Medicinal drugs (antibiotics, painkillers) and recreational drugs (caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, heroin).</p>",0],["d-b17-antibiotic",17,"Antibiotic","<p>A drug (substance) that kills bacteria (or stops their growth) but does not harm human cells.</p>","<p><strong>Used to treat bacterial infections.</strong> Many come from fungi or bacteria, e.g. <strong>penicillin</strong> (from the fungus <em>Penicillium</em>). <strong>Antibiotics kill bacteria but do not affect viruses</strong> — viruses have no cell wall/metabolism of their own and reproduce inside our cells (so antibiotics are useless for colds and flu). <strong>Some bacteria are resistant to antibiotics, which reduces the effectiveness of antibiotics.</strong></p>",0],["d-b17-continuous-variation",17,"Continuous variation","<p>Results in a range of phenotypes between two extremes.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>body length/height, body mass</strong>, leaf length, hand span. Caused by <strong>genes (many) and the environment</strong>. Shown by a <strong>histogram</strong> (bars touching; normal / bell-shaped distribution).</p>",0],["d-b17-discontinuous-variation",17,"Discontinuous variation","<p>Results in a limited number of phenotypes with no intermediates.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>ABO blood groups, seed shape in peas (round/wrinkled), seed colour in peas (yellow/green)</strong>, tongue rolling. Usually caused by <strong>genes only</strong> (one or two genes). Shown by a <strong>bar chart</strong> (bars separate).</p>",0],["d-b18-habitat",18,"Habitat","<p>The place where an organism lives.</p>","<p>(e.g. a pond, a rotting log, the rocky shore).</p>",0],["d-b18-community",18,"Community","<p>All of the populations of different species in an ecosystem.</p>","",0],["d-b18-ecosystem",18,"Ecosystem","<p>A unit containing the community of organisms and their environment, interacting together.</p>","<p>(e.g. a lake, a forest, a coral reef). Includes the non-living parts: water, soil, light, temperature, minerals, oxygen.</p>",0],["d-b18-food-chain",18,"Food chain","<p>A diagram showing the transfer of energy from one organism to the next, beginning with a producer.</p>","<p>Arrows point in the direction of energy flow (from the eaten to the eater). e.g. grass → grasshopper → frog → snake → eagle. Don't include the Sun.</p>",0],["d-b18-food-web",18,"Food web","<p>A network of interconnected food chains.</p>","<p>More realistic, because most animals eat more than one type of food and are eaten by more than one predator.</p>",0],["d-b18-producer",18,"Producer","<p>An organism that makes its own organic nutrients, usually using energy from sunlight, through photosynthesis.</p>","<p>Green <strong>plants</strong> (and algae, some bacteria). First trophic level.</p>",0],["d-b18-consumer",18,"Consumer","<p>An organism that gets its energy by feeding on other organisms.</p><p>Herbivore — an animal that gets its energy by eating plants.</p><p>Carnivore — an animal that gets its energy by eating other animals.</p>","<p>Classed as <strong>primary (1°), secondary (2°), tertiary (3°) and quaternary (4°)</strong> according to position in the food chain. Herbivores are <strong>primary consumers</strong>; carnivores are <strong>secondary or higher</strong> consumers. Omnivores eat both.</p>",0],["d-b18-decomposer",18,"Decomposer","<p>An organism that gets its energy from dead or waste organic material.</p>","<p>Mainly <strong>bacteria and fungi</strong>. They secrete enzymes onto dead material, absorb the products, and <strong>release CO₂ and mineral ions</strong> (e.g. ammonium) back to the environment — essential for nutrient cycles.</p>",0],["d-b18-pyramid-of-numbers",18,"Pyramid of numbers","<p>A diagram in which the width of each bar represents the number of organisms at each trophic level.</p>","<p>(producers at the bottom). Can be <strong>inverted/odd-shaped</strong> — e.g. one large oak tree supporting thousands of caterpillars; or parasites (many fleas on one bird).</p>",0],["d-b18-trophic-level",18,"Trophic level","<p>The position of an organism in a food chain, food web or ecological pyramid.</p>","<p><strong>producers</strong> (1st), <strong>primary consumers</strong> (2nd), <strong>secondary consumers</strong> (3rd), <strong>tertiary consumers</strong> (4th), <strong>quaternary consumers</strong> (5th). One organism may be at more than one trophic level in a web.</p>",0],["d-b18-pyramid-of-biomass",18,"Pyramid of biomass","<p>A diagram in which each bar shows the total dry mass of living material (biomass) at each trophic level.</p>","<p>Almost always <strong>pyramid-shaped</strong>. <strong>Advantage over pyramid of numbers</strong>: takes account of the <strong>size</strong> of organisms (one tree vs one aphid), so it is rarely inverted and shows the amount of material available more accurately. (Disadvantages: organisms must be killed and dried; biomass changes with season.)</p>",0],["d-b18-pyramid-of-energy",18,"Pyramid of energy<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A diagram showing the energy (kJ per m² per year) passing through each trophic level over a period of time.</p>","<p><strong>Always pyramid-shaped (never inverted)</strong> — energy decreases at every level. <strong>Advantages over numbers/biomass:</strong> takes account of the <strong>rate of energy transfer</strong> (production over time), not just a snapshot; allows <strong>comparison of efficiency</strong> between levels and between ecosystems; size and energy content of different tissues are both accounted for. (Disadvantage: data very hard to collect.)</p>",1],["d-b18-nitrogen-fixation",18,"Nitrogen fixation","<p>Converting unreactive nitrogen gas (N₂) into a more reactive form such as ammonia/ammonium or nitrate.</p>","<p>Which plants can use. By: <strong>1. lightning</strong>; <strong>2. artificial fertilisers</strong> (industrial Haber process); <strong>3. nitrogen-fixing bacteria</strong> (soil and legume root nodules).</p>",0],["d-b18-nitrification",18,"Nitrification","<p>Converting ammonium ions into nitrate ions.</p>","<p>(via nitrite) by <strong>nitrifying bacteria</strong> in well-aerated soil.</p>",0],["d-b18-denitrification",18,"Denitrification","<p>Converting nitrate ions into nitrogen gas.</p>","<p>By <strong>denitrifying bacteria</strong>, mainly in <strong>waterlogged (anaerobic)</strong> soil — reduces soil fertility (so farmers drain and plough soil).</p>",0],["d-b18-limiting-factor",18,"Limiting factor","<p>A factor that is in short supply and so restricts (limits) a process or the growth of a population.</p>","<p>e.g. food, water, light, space, mates, oxygen; for photosynthesis: light, CO₂, temperature.</p>",0],["d-b19-monoculture",19,"Monoculture","<p>A large area of land growing a single crop species.</p>","<p>(e.g. wheat, oil palm, rice), often year after year, with almost no other species present.</p>",0],["d-b19-biodiversity",19,"Biodiversity","<p>The number of different species that live in an area.</p>","<p>(High in rainforests and coral reefs.)</p>",0],["d-b19-deforestation",19,"Deforestation","<p>The removal of large areas of forest.</p>","<p>(for timber, fuel, farmland, cattle ranching, roads, housing, mining). <strong>Undesirable effects:</strong> <strong>reducing biodiversity</strong> (habitats lost); <strong>extinction</strong> of species; <strong>loss of soil</strong> (soil erosion — no roots to hold it, no canopy to break rain; nutrients leached); <strong>flooding</strong> (water runs off instead of being absorbed and transpired; soil washed into rivers); <strong>increase of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere</strong> (fewer trees for photosynthesis; trees burned/decomposed release CO₂) → climate change. Also less rainfall locally, loss of medicines and indigenous peoples' homes.</p>",0],["d-b19-extinction",19,"Extinction","<p>The complete and permanent loss of a species from the Earth.</p>","<p>All its individuals have died; its genes are lost forever.</p>",0],["d-b19-pollution",19,"Pollution","<p>Pollution — the addition of harmful substances (or energy) to the environment by humans.</p>","<p>Water: untreated sewage, fertilisers, oil, plastics, chemicals. Air: CO₂, methane, sulfur dioxide. Land: plastics, pesticides.</p>",0],["d-b19-greenhouse-effect",19,"Greenhouse effect","<p>The natural warming of the Earth by greenhouse gases.</p>","<p>Short-wave radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere and warms the Earth's surface; the Earth gives off <strong>infrared (heat) radiation</strong>, some of which is <strong>absorbed by greenhouse gases</strong> and re-radiated, keeping the Earth warm. <strong>Without it the Earth's average temperature would be about 33 °C lower</strong> — too cold for most life.</p>",0],["d-b19-enhanced-greenhouse-effect",19,"Enhanced greenhouse effect","<p>The increased heating caused by the increase in greenhouse gases (CO₂ and methane) in the atmosphere due to human activities.</p>","<p>→ <strong>global warming and climate change</strong>: rising sea levels (melting ice, thermal expansion) → flooding of low-lying land; more extreme weather (storms, droughts, floods); changes in rainfall → crop failure, desertification; species extinction and changes in distribution (habitats change faster than species can adapt); spread of tropical diseases.</p>",0],["d-b19-leaching",19,"Leaching","<p>The washing (draining) of soluble substances — especially nitrate and phosphate ions from fertilisers — out of the soil by rainwater.</p>","<p>Into rivers and lakes. Causes loss of soil fertility and <strong>eutrophication</strong>.</p>",0],["d-b19-sustainable-resource",19,"Sustainable resource","<p>A resource which is produced as rapidly as it is removed from the environment so that it does not run out.</p>","<p><strong>Some resources can be conserved and managed sustainably — limited to forests and fish stocks</strong>. (Fossil fuels are non-renewable — not sustainable.)</p>",0],["d-b19-conservation",19,"Conservation","<p>The protection and careful use (management) of natural resources, habitats and species.</p>","<p>So they are not destroyed and remain available for the future.</p>",0],["d-b19-endangered-species",19,"Endangered species","<p>A species at serious risk of becoming extinct.</p>","<p>Because its population is very small or falling rapidly (e.g. tigers, pandas, orangutans, rhinos).</p>",0],["d-b19-captive-breeding-programme",19,"Captive breeding programme","<p>Breeding endangered animals in captivity (zoos, sanctuaries).</p>","<p>To increase their numbers, keep a healthy gene pool (exchanging animals between zoos) and later <strong>release them back into the wild</strong> (e.g. Arabian oryx, golden lion tamarin). May use AI and IVF.</p>",0],["d-b19-seed-banks",19,"Seed banks","<p>Facilities where seeds of many plant species and crop varieties are dried and stored at low temperatures for long periods.</p>","<p>(e.g. Svalbard Global Seed Vault, Kew Millennium Seed Bank) to <strong>conserve species and genetic diversity</strong> — seeds can be grown if a species becomes extinct in the wild or a new crop variety is needed.</p>",0],["d-b19-artificial-insemination-ai",19,"Artificial insemination (AI)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Collecting semen (sperm) from a male and inserting it into the reproductive tract of a female.</p>","<p>Fertilisation happens in her body. Sperm can be <strong>frozen and transported</strong> between zoos/countries — so animals don't need to be moved, and breeding between genetically different (unrelated) individuals is possible → <strong>maintains genetic variation</strong>.</p>",1],["d-b19-in-vitro-fertilisation-ivf",19,"In vitro fertilisation (IVF)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Eggs are collected from a female (after hormone treatment), mixed with sperm in a dish in the laboratory; the resulting embryos are placed in the uterus of the female or a surrogate mother.</p>","<p>(possibly a related species). Used when animals won't mate naturally; increases offspring numbers.</p>",1],["d-b20-biotechnology",20,"Biotechnology","<p>Using organisms (usually microorganisms such as bacteria and fungi/yeast) to produce substances useful to humans.</p>","<p>e.g. bread, ethanol (biofuel), enzymes (pectinase, lactase, biological washing powders), antibiotics (penicillin), human insulin, mycoprotein, yoghurt.</p>",0],["d-b20-genetic-modification-gm",20,"Genetic modification (GM)","<p>Changing the genetic material of an organism by removing, changing or inserting individual genes.</p>","<p>(often from another species). Also called genetic engineering.</p>",0],["d-b20-biofuel",20,"Biofuel","<p>A fuel made from living material.</p>","<p><strong>Ethanol</strong> is made by <strong>anaerobic respiration (fermentation) of yeast</strong>: yeast is grown on sugar (from sugar cane, maize, waste plant material — starch/cellulose broken down to sugars first) <strong>without oxygen</strong> → glucose → <strong>ethanol + carbon dioxide</strong>; ethanol is separated by <strong>distillation</strong> and mixed with petrol (e.g. 'gasohol' in Brazil). Renewable; the CO₂ released was recently absorbed by the crop (but land used for fuel crops can't grow food).</p>",0],["d-b20-fermenter",20,"Fermenter<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A large, sterile vessel (stainless steel or glass) in which microorganisms are grown in a liquid culture under controlled conditions to make a product.</p>","<p>Used for large-scale production by bacteria and fungi of: <strong>insulin</strong> (GM bacteria), <strong>penicillin</strong> (fungus <em>Penicillium</em>), <strong>mycoprotein</strong> (fungus <em>Fusarium</em> → Quorn), enzymes. Conditions controlled (see 'Conditions in a fermenter').</p>",1],["d-b20-bacillus-thuringiensis-bt",20,"<em>Bacillus thuringiensis</em> (Bt)","<p>A soil bacterium that makes a protein (Bt toxin) that is poisonous to insect larvae.</p>","<p>(it binds to receptors only found in the guts of certain insects, not humans). The <strong>Bt gene has been inserted into crop plants</strong> (Bt maize, Bt cotton) so they <strong>make their own insecticide</strong> → <strong>resistance to insect pests</strong> → less insecticide spraying, higher yield.</p>",0],["d-b20-restriction-enzymes",20,"Restriction enzymes <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Enzymes that cut DNA at specific base sequences.</p>","<p>Leaving <strong>sticky ends</strong> (short single-stranded lengths of unpaired bases). The <strong>same</strong> restriction enzyme is used on human DNA and plasmid so the sticky ends are complementary.</p>",1],["d-b20-sticky-ends",20,"Sticky ends <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Short lengths of unpaired bases at the cut ends of DNA.</p>","<p>Left by a restriction enzyme; they can <strong>pair (form bonds) with complementary unpaired bases</strong> on another piece of DNA cut by the same enzyme.</p>",1],["d-b20-dna-ligase",20,"DNA ligase <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An enzyme that joins two pieces of DNA together.</p>","<p>(seals the sugar–phosphate backbone), e.g. joining the human gene into the cut plasmid.</p>",1],["d-b20-recombinant-plasmid",20,"Recombinant plasmid <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A plasmid containing both bacterial DNA and DNA (a gene) from a different organism.</p>","<p>(e.g. the human insulin gene).</p>",1]]});
